('1', '99220040072@klu.ac.in', '1', '2/21/2026 6:36:03 AM +00:00', '2/21/2026 6:36:31 AM +00:00', 'Life Access - AI-Driven Health Record Ecosystem for Inclusive and Emergency-Ready Healthcare', 'The fragmentation of healthcare records, especially for low connectivity and rural areas, represents a significant barrier to effective patient care and leads to a delay in diagnosis and unnecessary replication of tests. The authors present MediVault, a unique digital health locker that is embedded using artificial intelligence and blockchain technology for providing lifetime, portable, and secure access to one’s medical history. The new proposed solution is based on a decentralized architecture focusing on immutability of data and patient control and addresses the disorganized part of health records for over 120 million people acrossIndia. The MediVault digital health locker provides features for emergency access with QR codes, Aadhaar ID, and biometrics. This approach aims to build a robust, secure, health infrastructure tailored to a modern paradigm of the healthcare system while also focusing on the needs of underprivileged and marginalized populations. Beyond technical innovation, the project will directly contribute to global health priorities by supporting United Nations SDG 3 - Good Health and Well-Being, SDG 9 (Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure) and SDG 10 (Reduced Inequalities) through\nimproved digital health infrastructure and equitable access to\nmedical services.', 'Jai Praveena Sai J', '99220040072@klu.ac.in', 'Jai Praveena Sai J (Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education)*; Sai Ganka R (Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education); Mymoon Sulthana K (Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education); Rajasekaran M (Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education)', 'J, Jai Praveena Sai*; R, Sai Ganka; K, Mymoon Sulthana; M, Rajasekaran', '99220040072@klu.ac.in*; 99220040187@klu.ac.in; 99220040136@klu.ac.in; m.rajasekaran@klu.ac.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Capstone formatted (1) (1).docx (190,196 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Anish K R (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Assistant Professor)', 'anish@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Sai Ganka R, Final year student, Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education, 99220040187@klu.ac.in.\nJai Praveena Sai J,Final year student ,Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education, 99220040072@klu.ac.in\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('2', 'mudaliarpavithra2916@gmail.com', '2', '2/22/2026 5:11:16 PM +00:00', '2/22/2026 5:11:16 PM +00:00', 'A Comprehensive Review on Early Prediction and Stage-wise Monitoring of Chronic Kidney Disease for Diabetes Patients', 'Chronic Kidney disease is a serious complication among diabetic patients, often resulting in fatal consequences if not detected and treated early. Most of the diseases, including CKD, during their early stages, usually go unnoticed but gradually develop into serious damage to the kidneys if it is not diagnosed on time. Early detection of CKD and stage-wise monitoring can make a big difference in treatment and outcomes of long-term health. This paper os study presents early prediction and stage-wise monitoring of CKD in diabetic patients. Out of several risk factors, diabetes is considered a major cause of kidney problems. This work aims at developing a predictive system for identifying CKD at an early stage, classification of disease into different stages, and monitoring the changes over time by considering patient health records, laboratory test reports, and hybrid techniques. The ultimate goal is to provide a reliable tool that supports early prevention, timely medical decisions, and better awareness about kidney health. Finally, the work tries to provide an overall tool that supports not only early CKD prediction but also enhances awareness regarding long-term kidney health.', 'pavithra kandan', 'mudaliarpavithra2916@gmail.com', 'pavithra kandan (gujarat technological university)*', 'kandan, pavithra*', 'mudaliarpavithra2916@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'pavithramudaliar_reviewpaper (6) (1).pdf (222,786 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Shilpa Yohannan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shilpa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Komal Prajapathi', 'Paper Presentation'),
('3', 'pparul0123@gmail.com', '3', '2/23/2026 3:10:46 PM +00:00', '2/23/2026 3:24:50 PM +00:00', 'Sign Language Recognition System for American and Indian Sign Language Using Deep Learning', 'ABSTRACTS: This research presents a comprehensive deep learning-based system for recognizing gestures from American Sign Language (ASL) and Indian Sign Language (ISL). The proposed framework leverages Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) for spatial feature extraction and classification. The system is designed for real-time deployment and achieves high recognition accuracy. Extensive experimental evaluation demonstrates robustness under varying environmental conditions. The study contributes toward inclusive communication technology for differently-abled communities.', 'PARUL PAL', 'pparul0123@gmail.com', 'PARUL PAL (Parul pal)*', 'PAL, PARUL*', 'pparul0123@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Sign_Language_Recognition_ASL_ISL_Full_Content_IEEE_Paper (1) (1).pdf (87,674 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Reena Cherian (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'reena@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Parul Pal (Maharishi university of information technology ) pparul0123@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('4', 'vinaykumarsn2031@gmail.com', '5', '2/24/2026 7:28:14 AM +00:00', '2/24/2026 7:28:53 AM +00:00', 'Explainable AI (XAI) in Healthcare: A Systematic Review of Taxonomies, Methodologies and Clinical Trust', 'The \"black-box\" nature of Deep Learning models\nremains a primary obstacle to their integration into\nclinical practice, as clinicians require transparent and\ninterpretable justifications for high-stakes medical\ndecisions. This systematic review explores the\nlandscape of Explainable AI (XAI) in healthcare,\nexamining current taxonomies, methodologies, and\ntheir impact on clinical trust. We analyze various\nframeworks, including the proposed FedXAI for\nprivacy-preserving cardiovascular disease detection and\nthe AutoXAI framework for automated, domainspecific explainability. Key methodologies such as\nSHAP, LIME, and attention mechanisms are evaluated\nacross diverse medical applications, including\noncology, cardiology, and mental health. The findings\nhighlight a significant \"evaluation gap\" where technical\naccuracy is often prioritized over clinical utility and\nstandardized quantitative metrics. Furthermore, this\nstudy investigates the intersection of XAI with\nemerging technologies such as Federated Learning,\nBlockchain, and Digital Twins to ensure HIPAA/GDPR\ncompliance and data integrity. We conclude by\nadvocating for a transition toward Human-Centered AI\n(HCXAI) and providing a strategic roadmap for\ndeveloping lightweight, ethically grounded, and\nclinically validated interpretable systems.', 'Vinay Kumar S N', 'vinaykumarsn2031@gmail.com', 'Vinay Kumar S N (CMR University)*', 'S N, Vinay Kumar*', 'vinaykumarsn2031@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'A Systematic Review of Taxonomies, Methodologies and Clinical Trust in Healthcare using XAI.pdf (960,570 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Lorane Scaria (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'lorane@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Dr. K. P. Agarwal\nProfessor\nDept of CSE(AI), SOET,\nCMR University, Bengaluru\nkpagrawal.a@cmr.edu.in\n\nProf. Gripsy Paul\nAssistant Professor\nDept of CSE, SOET,\nCMR University, Bengaluru\ngripsy.p@cmr.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('5', '99220041289@klu.ac.in', '6', '2/24/2026 3:30:17 PM +00:00', '2/24/2026 3:30:17 PM +00:00', 'Deepfake Image and Video Detection: Techniques, Challenges, and Human Factors in Multi-modal  Forensics ', '— DeepFake media presents a major threat to digital trust and the need for effective and accurate detection systems is high. In this paper, we propose an integrated DeepFake detection framework that learns both spatial and temporal features for recognizing both images and video data. For image-based classification, we use a transfer learning framework with MobileNetV2, which enables us to extract discriminative facial features while using minimal processing power. For video analysis, we employ a hybrid CNN-LSTM model to learn both frame-based spatial characteristics as well as sequences of timebased dependencies (i.e., temporal). Additionally, this framework employs optimized preprocessing of data, lightweight design of models, and early stopping to facilitate fast training and improved generalization on small training datasets. Our experimental evaluations validate our system\'s consistent performance across real versus fake samples, while also improving detection rates of frame-based anomalies. Our proposed model is an efficient, endto-end solution for real-time DeepFake detection systems, which can be implemented within resource constrained environments without sacrificing robust classification accuracies.  ', 'Pallapu Sreenivasulu', '99220041289@klu.ac.in', 'Pallapu Sreenivasulu (Kalasalingam university)*; Gella Raghuram (Kalasalingam university); Guduru Pavan Kumar (Kalasalingam university); Gadi Bhumika (Kalasalingam university)', 'Sreenivasulu, Pallapu*; Raghuram, Gella; Pavan Kumar, Guduru; Bhumika, Gadi', '99220041289@klu.ac.in*; 99220041655@klu.ac.in; 99220041700@klu.ac.in; 99220041472@klu.ac.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'PAPER12.pdf (446,700 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Shilpa Yohannan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shilpa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Pallapu Sreenivasulu - Kalasalingam university - 99220041289@klu.ac.in\nGella Raghuram - Kalasalingam university - 99220041655@klu.ac.in\nGadi Bhumika - Kalasalingam university - 99220041472@klu.ac.in\nGuduru Pavan kumar - Kalasalingam university - 99220041700@klu.ac.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('6', 'sriramt384@gmail.com', '7', '2/25/2026 8:18:23 AM +00:00', '2/25/2026 8:23:32 AM +00:00', 'EXHALESENSE – NON INVASIVE VOC ANALYSIS FOR EARLY DISEASE DIAGNOSIS', 'This paper presents ExhaleSense, a compact, TinyML-enabled embedded system designed for non-invasive breath analysis and early disease detection using volatile organic compounds (VOCs). Human breath contains metabolically derived biomarkers such as VOCs and carbon dioxide (CO₂), which reflect physiological and respiratory conditions. The proposed system integrates a hybrid multi-gas sensor array with an ESP32-S3 microcontroller and deploys a Random Forest classifier using TensorFlow Lite Micro for on-device inference. Unlike cloud-dependent diagnostic frameworks, ExhaleSense performs real-time classification locally, ensuring low latency, enhanced data privacy, and energy-efficient operation. Experimental validation demonstrates 98% accuracy in VOC classification and 100% accuracy in CO₂ level classification. The embedded model achieves an average inference time of 14 ms with minimal memory footprint and optimized power consumption. The system demonstrates the feasibility of edge artificial intelligence for portable, low-cost, and privacy-preserving healthcare monitoring applications.\n', 'Sriram Arasu  T', 'sriramt384@gmail.com', 'Sivaranjani  S (Sri Krishna College Of Engineering and Technology); Sriram Arasu  T (Sri Krishna College Of Engineering and Technology)*; Shreyas Viswanath K (Sri Krishna College Of Engineering and Technology); Shankar  N (Sri Krishna College Of Engineering and Technology); Sowmiya Sree  P (Sri Krishna College Of Engineering and Technology)', 'S, Sivaranjani ; T, Sriram Arasu *; K, Shreyas Viswanath; N, Shankar ; P, Sowmiya Sree ', 'sivaranjanis@skcet.ac.in; sriramt384@gmail.com*; 727722euee109@skcet.ac.in; 727722euee102@skcet.ac.in; 727722euee112@skcet.ac.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Updated-Project -Conference -Paper.pdf (921,715 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Lorane Scaria (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'lorane@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Dr. S. Sivaranjani – Mentor, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Sri Krishna College of Engineering and Technology, sivaranjanis@skcet.ac.in\n\nShreyas Viswanath K – UG Scholar, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Sri Krishna College of Engineering and Technology, 727722euee109@skcet.ac.in\n\nShankar N – UG Scholar, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Sri Krishna College of Engineering and Technology, 727722euee102@skcet.ac.in\n\nSowmiya Sree P – UG Scholar, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Sri Krishna College of Engineering and Technology, 727722euee112@skcet.ac.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('7', 'snehachhetri401@gmail.com', '8', '2/25/2026 3:14:35 PM +00:00', '2/25/2026 3:14:35 PM +00:00', 'LeukoScan: An AI-Driven Image Segmentation  and   Classification System for Early Diagnosis of Acute   Lymphoblastic Leukemia', ' Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a \nhematological malignancy that is rapidly progressive among \nchildren and young adults and exact and early diagnosis is a \nmajor factor to enhance patient survival. Traditional \ndiagnostic services are based on the manual microscopic \nexamination of peripheral blood smears by qualified \npathologists. Even though effective, it is a labor-intensive, \nsubjective, and inter-observer variant process, which may \neasily lead to inter-observer variation, especially in resource\nconstrained clinical settings. We present LeukoScan, a two\nstage deep learning system, in this paper to automate the \nprocess of detecting and classifying ALL in images of \nperipheral blood smear in this paper. The system proposed \nincorporates two supplementary modules. In phase one, a \nconvolutional neural network with U-Net is used to \nsemantically segment leukemic blast cells in full-slide smear \nimages, obtaining a Dice Similarity Coefficient of 85.96% \nand an Intersection over Union of 76.56% on the test data. \nThe second stage involves a ResNet-50 model with transfer \nlearning to classify single cells as normal or malignant with a \nclassification accuracy of 92.31. Gradient-weighted Class \nActivation Mapping (Grad-CAM), which gives visual \nexplanations of the model in terms of highlighting \ndiagnostically interesting nuclear and chromatin events, is \nadded to improve the clinical interpretability of the model. \nThe system is tested on the ALL-IDB data, and it has a high \nsensitivity, and this is required to reduce false-negative \nresults in the screening of leukemia. In general, LeukoScan is \na fast, objective, and explainable diagnostic support tool, \nwhich can potentially benefit clinicians, especially in the \npractice environment where the use of professional \nhematopathologists is limited. ', 'Sneha  Chhetri', 'snehachhetri401@gmail.com', 'Sneha  Chhetri (Jain Engineering College)*', 'Chhetri, Sneha *', 'snehachhetri401@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'rp_new.pdf (996,272 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Anish K R (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Assistant Professor)', 'anish@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Abin Muhammed A ,JAIN (Deemed-to-be University) \nBangalore, India., \nabinmuhammed.a@gmail.com \nMuhammed Sajal JAIN (Deemed-to-be University) \nBangalore, India. \nmuhammedsajal7@gmail.com   ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('8', '220801074@rajalakshmi.edu.in', '9', '2/25/2026 3:32:22 PM +00:00', '2/25/2026 3:32:22 PM +00:00', 'Machine Learning Based CO-Planer Microstrip Patch Antenna design for Lung Cancer Detection', 'Abstract— Microstrip patch antennas have been extensively employed in the last few years in the biomedical field because they are relatively small, cheap, and do not interact with human tissues. As part of this project, the design and optimization of a rectangular patch of microstrip material to work at 2.4 GHz ISM band with non-invasive lung cancer detection was given. The anthropomorphic model used is a realistic multilayer lung model which is used to analyze the electromagnetic interaction between healthy and cancerous tissues in connection with the antenna. Tumors in the lung change dielectric properties of the lung and cause measurable changes in resonant frequency and the reflection coefficient (S11). Increasingly, parametric optimization of the antenna dimensions is conducted to give minimum S11 and perfect frequency resonance to aid in detecting more. Data in frequency format and S11 data obtained at CST simulations are normalized to create a dataset to be analyzed. An automated algorithm and machine learning-based system that is implemented as k-Nearest Neighbors (kNN) algorithm is used to identify the presence of healthy and cancerous lungs, which can be effectively classified in relation to the parameters of the antenna. The optimized antenna is of better sensitivity with stronger S11 minima and greater gain and directivity towards the lung area. The outputs of the simulation prove that the integration of machine learning with the design of the antenna is a valid, conservative, and non-invasive method to detect lung cancer, which will contribute to the creation of sophisticated biomedical diagnostic machines.', 'P Harish  Kumar', '220801074@rajalakshmi.edu.in', 'P Harish  Kumar (Rajalakshmi Engineering college)*', 'Kumar, P Harish *', '220801074@rajalakshmi.edu.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'IEEE(1).doc (2,660,352 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Kalyani - (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences )', 'kalyani@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Mrs. Indhumathi\nAssociate Professor \nElectronics and Communication\nRajalakshmi Engineering College Chennai, India\nbtindhu07@gmail.com\n\n\nHarish kumar P\nElectronics and Communication\nRajalakshmi Engineering College\nChennai, India\n220801074@rajalakshmi.edu.in\n\n\nGokul Raam P\nElectronics and Communication\nRajalakshmi Engineering College\nChennai, India\n220801063@rajalakshmi.edu.in\n\nKamaleshwarasiva P.A\nElectronics and Communication\nRajalakshmi Engineering College\nChennai, India\n220801097@rajalakshmi.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('9', 'drranjithkumara@veltech.edu.in', '10', '2/26/2026 4:59:59 AM +00:00', '2/26/2026 5:20:19 AM +00:00', 'Understanding Mental Health Stigma among Adolescents in Rural Tamil Nadu: An Empirical Study for Community-Based Social Work Interventions', 'Mental health stigma remains a significant barrier to help-seeking, early intervention, and overall well-being among adolescents, particularly in rural and socioeconomically marginalized settings. In India, limited attention has been given to understanding how stigma operates within rural youth populations and how social work interventions can address it effectively. This study empirically examines the nature, dimensions, and determinants of mental health stigma among adolescents in rural Tamil Nadu.\n\nUsing a community-based research design, primary data were collected from rural adolescents through a structured stigma assessment scale complemented by focused group discussions. The study analyzes levels of perceived stigma, self-stigma, and public stigma, and examines their association with gender, socioeconomic background, family attitudes, and exposure to mental health awareness programs.\n\nFindings indicate that stigma is strongly influenced by cultural beliefs, fear of social exclusion, and limited mental health literacy. Female adolescents and those from economically vulnerable households reported higher levels of internalized stigma. The study highlights that stigma significantly reduces willingness to seek professional support, thereby reinforcing untreated mental health concerns.\n\nThe paper argues that community-based social work interventions—such as school mental health education programs, peer support initiatives, family counseling, and community awareness campaigns—are critical in reducing stigma and strengthening early help-seeking behaviors. By situating mental health within a social determinants framework, the study contributes to policy discussions on integrating stigma-reduction strategies into rural public health and social work practice.', 'RANJITHKUMAR A', 'drranjithkumara@veltech.edu.in', 'RANJITHKUMAR A (Vel Tech Rangarajan Dr. Sagunthala R&D Institute of Science and Technology)*', 'A, RANJITHKUMAR*', 'drranjithkumara@veltech.edu.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract.docx (14,951 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Kalyani - (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences )', 'kalyani@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'nil', 'Paper Presentation'),
('10', 'srinivasayyappan13@gmail.com', '11', '2/26/2026 4:29:56 PM +00:00', '2/26/2026 4:29:56 PM +00:00', 'Intelligent Multi-Disease Prediction System Using Machine Learning', 'Chronic diseases such as heart disease, diabetes, and hypertension are among the leading causes of mortality worldwide and account for a large share of long-term healthcare expenditure. These diseases are highly interconnected, where the presence of one condition significantly increases the likelihood of developing others. For example, diabetes can accelerate cardiovascular complications, while uncontrolled hypertension may lead to stroke and chronic kidney disease. Most existing healthcare prediction systems focus on single-disease prediction and therefore fail to capture cross-disease relationships, complication risks, and unified cardio-metabolic scoring. Such isolated approaches provide limited clinical insight and are insufficient for preventive healthcare decision making.To address these limitations, this work presents an intelligent multi-disease prediction framework based on machine learning techniques. The proposed system predicts heart disease, diabetes, and hypertension using trained models, integrates severity classification layers, performs complication risk modeling, predicts stroke risk, and estimates chronic kidney disease (CKD) interdependency by analyzing combined disease probabilities and patient-specific factors. In addition, the system provides a unified chronic disease dashboard that summarizes risk levels in an interpretable format and generates structured medical PDF reports to support early diagnosis and monitoring. By combining multi-disease intelligence, interdependency analysis, and real-time reporting, the proposed framework enhances preventive healthcare analysis and offers a scalable foundation for future clinical deployment and integration with hospital information systems.', 'Srinivasahan A', 'srinivasayyappan13@gmail.com', 'Srinivasahan A (Sri Krishna College of engineering and technology)*; Vasantha Kumar M (Sri Krishna College of Engineering and Technology)', 'A, Srinivasahan*; M, Vasantha Kumar', 'srinivasayyappan13@gmail.com*; vasanthmksv@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'INTELLIGENT_DISEASE_PREDICTION_SYSTEM.pdf (517,160 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Reena Cherian (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'reena@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Sonika N\nDepartment of Information Technology \nSri Krishna College of Engineering and Technology,Coimbatore, India \n727722euit179@skcet.ac.in\n\nVasantha Kumar M\nDepartment of Information Technology \nSri Krishna College of Engineering and Technology,Coimbatore, India \nvasanthmksv@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('11', '23211a3241@bvrit.ac.in', '12', '2/27/2026 6:30:45 AM +00:00', '2/27/2026 6:30:45 AM +00:00', 'Mental Risk Assessment Using Natural Language Processing on Social Media Posts', 'Depression and other mental illnesses have become urgent subjects of social health in the modern digital environ- ment, where social media platforms provide more visibility than ever on the psychological state of people [1], [7]. The paper provides a superior approach to the mental risk assessment based on Natural Language Processing (NLP) algorithms implemented on social media posts.Although currently used methods are mostly aimed at the accuracy of classification, they typically work as a black box, so they are not visible and they do not involve the aspect of transparency and privacy that are the key to the successful implementation of the methods in practice [3], [4].Our suggested system will overcome those crucial constraints by adding emotion-sensitive text characteristics [10], ensuring privacy-saving strategies by eliminating personal iden- tifiers [5], and making the classifications decisions explainable [4].The methodology applies full NLP preprocessing such as tokenization and stop-word removal and lemmatizing to extract the features through the use of TF-IDF analysis and N-gram analysis.Depressive tendencies are identified in textual content using machine learning classifiers trained to identify depressive content. Critically, we do not intend our system as a diagnostic tool but an early-warning support system and that is where ethical considerations should be applied in the technology of men- tal health.Experimental findings have shown that the improved method does not only not compromise on the competitive accu- racy, but also gives the stakeholders interpretable information and privacy assurances.', 'yogeshwar yogeshwar', '23211a3241@bvrit.ac.in', 'yogeshwar yogeshwar (india)*', 'yogeshwar, yogeshwar*', '23211a3241@bvrit.ac.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ieee-2 final.docx (298,358 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Lorane Scaria (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'lorane@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', '1.Dr.U.Ganesh Naidu ,Assistant professor,B V raju institute of technology,ganesh613@gmail.com.\n2. Guraja Rahul , student, B V raju of institute of technology,23211a3222@bvrit.ac.in\n3.Matham Karthik, Student , B V raju  institute of technology,24215a3202@bvrit.ac.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('12', 'jahnavipayawala@gmail.com', '13', '2/28/2026 4:17:31 PM +00:00', '2/28/2026 4:17:31 PM +00:00', 'Heart Beat Sound Classifier Using Multiple Machine Learning Models', 'The early diagnosis and monitoring of cardiovascular diseases, which are still considered to be among the leading causes of death worldwide, greatly depend on the proper RH classification of heart sounds. In order to classify heart sounds as either normal or abnormal, the project proposes a machine learning-based system that uses Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs) as a feature extraction method. Several machine learning classifiers have been implemented in the system, such as Support Vector Machines (SVM), Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and k-Nearest Neighbors (k-NN). These classifiers are trained using a range of heart sounds from both healthy subjects and patients with known cardiac conditions, such as arrhythmias and murmurs. The model\'s rigorous pipeline for processing heart sound signals includes pre-processing steps like noise filtering, amplitude normalization, and heart cycle segmentation. Accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score are used to evaluate each classifier', 'P.Jahnavi Priya', 'jahnavipayawala@gmail.com', 'P.Jahnavi Priya (Sathyabama Institute of Science and Technology)*', 'Priya, P.Jahnavi*', 'jahnavipayawala@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'RESEARCH PAPER.pdf (399,493 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Shilpa Yohannan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shilpa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'M.J. HARSHA SRI\nB.E - CSE\nSATHYABAMA INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY\nharshadimpu2005@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('13', 'paveithra23@gmail.com', '14', '3/2/2026 7:59:19 AM +00:00', '3/2/2026 7:59:19 AM +00:00', 'Design and Implementation of a Real-Time Intrusion Detection System Using Deep Learning', 'Abstract - With the ubiquitous internet connectivity, many embedded and Internet of Things devices are now susceptible to cyber-attacks. Since most traditional intrusion detection systems are based on known attack signatures, they often fail to detect unknown or unseen attacks and are also too complex for embedded systems. This paper applies a convolutional autoencoder on an embedded edge platform to present an anomaly-based intrusion detection system. To train the system on what normal network traffic should look like, Wireshark is employed to capture and analyze network packets. The system can be used to detect intrusions by detecting packets that display different behavior and have higher reconstruction errors since it has only been trained on normal data. The experiment shows that the system can effectively detect anomalies with a low computational cost when applied to real network data. This makes the proposed system eligible for embedded and Internet of Things real-time security. ', 'Paveithra C', 'paveithra23@gmail.com', 'Paveithra C (Chennai Institute of Technology)*', 'C, Paveithra*', 'paveithra23@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Design and Implementation of a Real-Time Intrusion Detection System Using Deep Learning.docx (292,263 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Shilpa Yohannan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shilpa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Thenmozhi Jai Shankar, Student, Chennai Institute of technology, \nthenmozhi2022236@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('14', 'albertnitheesh@gmail.com', '15', '3/2/2026 8:26:21 AM +00:00', '3/2/2026 8:26:21 AM +00:00', 'Data Analysis using Agentic AI', 'This paper describes the design and development of a modular, agent-based analytical framework that supports natural language interaction with diverse enterprise data systems. In contrast to conventional business intelligence dashboards, which support static, predefined views, the proposed system dynamically translates ad-hoc user queries into executable analytical workflows using multi-agent orchestration. The system design has three tiers: a interaction gateway, an orchestrated analytical core, and a validated multi-source data management tier. A Planning Orchestrator break down natural language inputs into formal tasks, which are assigned to dedicated agents for data retrieval, machine learning inference, statistical computation, and visualization. A Data Source Validation Module validates schema consistency and governed query execution on distributed SQL, NoSQL, API, document, and vector data sources. The proposed framework is centered on modular specialization, separation of concerns, and extensibility, illustrating how agent-based orchestration facilitates adaptive, context-aware analytics that go beyond the capabilities of conventional dashboard-based analytical systems while retaining the structural integrity of an enterprise-grade system.', 'Nithesh Chakaravarthy Seelan K', 'albertnitheesh@gmail.com', 'Nithesh Chakaravarthy Seelan K (Sathyabama Institute of Science and Technology)*; CH Netaji Bhadraiahnath Chowdary (Sathyabama Institute of Science and Technology); Mrs. Scinthia Clarinda S (Sathyabama Institute of Science and Technology)', 'Seelan K, Nithesh Chakaravarthy*; Bhadraiahnath Chowdary, CH Netaji; Clarinda S, Mrs. Scinthia', 'albertnitheesh@gmail.com*; chnetajibc@gmail.com; scinthiaclarinda.s.cse@sathyabama.ac.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ResearchPaper.pdf (839,169 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Kalyani - (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences )', 'kalyani@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'CH Netaji Bhadraiahnath Chowdary\nStudent, Department of Computer Science and Engineering\nwith specialization in Data Science,\nSathyabama Institute of Science and Technology,\nChennai, India\nchnetajibc@gmail.com\n\n\nMrs. Scinthia Clarinda S\n Assistant Professor, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Sathyabama Institute of Science and Technology,\nChennai, India\nscinthiaclarinda.s.cse@sathyabama.ac.in\n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('15', 'maratuech.c@gmail.com', '16', '3/2/2026 10:57:27 AM +00:00', '3/2/2026 10:57:27 AM +00:00', 'Mobile Music Therapy Outreach with People Experiencing Homelessness: Foundations, Field-Based Practices, and First Observations', 'People experiencing homelessness face cumulative barriers to accessing mental health support, including stigma, institutional mistrust, mobility constraints, and the prioritization of basic survival needs over psychological care. In response to these challenges, the Musicobus project develops a mobile, community-based mental health outreach model using music therapy as a low-threshold, relational, and non-stigmatizing intervention embedded within street outreach practices.\n\nImplemented in Toulouse, France, Musicobus integrates music therapy into regular outreach rounds through a mobile unit equipped for individual therapeutic encounters in the street environment. The project follows a flexible and progressive pathway: initial in-situ individual sessions during outreach, optional referral to group workshops in community spaces, and opportunities for collective creative activities that support social connection, empowerment, and community participation. This stepped model seeks to reduce barriers to care, strengthen therapeutic alliance, and foster continuity of engagement with people living in highly unstable conditions.\n\nGrounded in trauma-informed and community mental health approaches, Musicobus emphasizes relational safety, contextual adaptability, and respect for participants’ rhythms, choices, and life environments. Preliminary field observations over two years indicate positive impacts on emotional expression, relational engagement, perceived safety, and motivation for social participation. Participants have shown increased willingness to engage in supportive relationships and group-based activities, suggesting potential pathways toward recovery-oriented and socially inclusive trajectories.\n\nThe project also highlights key implementation challenges inherent to mobile mental health interventions with marginalized populations, including continuity of care, ethical engagement in contexts of vulnerability, practitioner safety, intersectoral coordination, and the evaluation of outcomes with highly mobile participants. Implications are discussed for social work practice, community mental health services, health equity, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Musicobus offers a transferable outreach-based model illustrating how arts-informed interventions can contribute to trauma-informed care, population well-being, and social justice-oriented mental health practices.', 'Camille Maratuech', 'maratuech.c@gmail.com', 'Camille Maratuech (Musicobus)*', 'Maratuech, Camille*', 'maratuech.c@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Article Musicobus Revue Française de Musicothérapie.pdf (1,465,023 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Deepa Rasheed (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences(Autonomous))', 'deepa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'None. This submission is based on a single-author research and field project conducted by Camille Maratuech (Music Therapist, University Paul-Valéry Montpellier 3, France ; email: maratuech.c@gmail.com).', 'Poster Presentation'),
('16', 'udhayakumar.vlsi@gmail.com', '17', '3/2/2026 1:37:48 PM +00:00', '3/2/2026 1:39:40 PM +00:00', 'STM32 Based Health Monitoring and Fall Detection using Optimized Gradient Boosting Algorithm', 'Aim: The aim of this research is to design and develop an intelligent Iot -based fall Prediction and health monitoring system for elderly individuals using machine learning techniques. The system aims to accurately detect fall-like events in real time,minimize false alarms, and ensure rapid emergency response through automated alerts and location sharing .Materials and Methods: Two groups are considered in current research. Group 1 represents the existing SVM algorithm with 10 samples and Group 2 represents the proposed Optimized Gradient Boosting algorithm with 10 samples. The performance is evaluated and statistically compared using accuracy values. Result: The experimental results shows that the proposed optimized gradient boosting algorithm has an accuracy of  98% and alert generation time is <1 second with low false alarm rate when compared to SVM algorithm which has an accuracy of 88.9 % and alert generation time is 2.0 seconds. Conclusion: In this analysis, it is observed that the STM32 based health monitoring and fall detection using optimized gradient boosting algorithm has significantly better accuracy and provides faster alerts than traditional SVM algorithm method.', 'UDHAYAKUMAR M', 'udhayakumar.vlsi@gmail.com', 'UDHAYAKUMAR M (K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING)*; DHAVAMANI S (K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING); AGILADEVI S (K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING); HARINI S (K.S.R INSTITUTE FOR ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY); PADMASRI K J (K.S.R INSTITUTE FOR ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY); THARANISH R T (K.S.R INSTITUTE FOR ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY)', 'M, UDHAYAKUMAR*; S, DHAVAMANI; S, AGILADEVI; S, HARINI; K J, PADMASRI; R T, THARANISH', 'udhayakumar.vlsi@gmail.com*; lathumurugan.s@gmail.com; agilakarmega@gmail.com; harini219harini@gmail.com; padmasri783@gmail.com; tharanishrt2005@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'STM 32 Based Health Monitoring and Fall Detection using Optimized Gradient Boosting Algorithm.pdf (726,058 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Kiran Thampi (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'kiran@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', '1) Udhayakumar M \nAssistant Professor\nK.S.R. College of Engineering\nTiruchengode, India\nudhayakumar.vlsi@gmail.com\n\n2) Dhavamani S\nAssistant Professor\nK.S.R. College of Engineering\nTiruchengode, India\nlathumurugan.s@gmail.com\n\n3)Agiladevi S\nAssistant Professor\nK.S.R. College of Engineering\nTiruchengode, India\nagilakarmega@gmail.com\n\n4)Harini S\nResearch Scholar\nK.S.R. Institute for Engineering Technology\nTiruchengode, India\nharini219harini@gmail.com\n\n5) Padma Sri K.J\nResearch Scholar\nK.S.R. Institute for Engineering Technology\nTiruchengode, India\npadmasri783@gmail.com\n\n6)Tharanish R.T\nResearch Scholar\nK.S.R. Institute for Engineering Technology\nTiruchengode, India\ntharanishrt2005@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('17', 'rahulv210804@gmail.com', '18', '3/2/2026 7:28:31 PM +00:00', '3/2/2026 7:28:31 PM +00:00', 'Hybrid Autoencoder & K-Means Customer Segmentation with SHAP explainability', 'Customer Segmentation systems traditionally rely on linear clustering techniques, making it difficult to capture complex behavioral patterns, ensure interpretability, or explain model outputs. This limitation becomes critical as modern organizations increasingly analyze high-dimensional, evolving customer data, personalized interactions, and data-driven decision processes. Hybrid Autoencoder and K-Means Segmentation proposes a unified architecture that introduces non-linear representation learning, latent-space clustering, and explainability-driven reasoning to improve segmentation reliability and transparency in analytics pipelines. This study synthesizes established research trends across autoencoder-based representation learning, centroid-based clustering, explainable artificial intelligence, and interpretable machine learning frameworks. By integrating non-linear encoders, K-Means clustering, and SHAP-based feature attribution, this work highlights the growing need for transparent, trustworthy, and stable customer segmentation systems in real-world applications. We analyze architectural components, design choices, evaluation strategies, and practical challenges, ultimately positioning the proposed framework as a robust, interpretable, and scalable solution for data-driven customer-centric decision-making.', 'Rahul V', 'rahulv210804@gmail.com', 'Rahul V (Chennai Institute of Technology)*', 'V, Rahul*', 'rahulv210804@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Hybrid_AutoEncoder_Segmentation.docx (24,173 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Deepa Rasheed (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences(Autonomous))', 'deepa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'S AMUTHA\nCHENNAI INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY\namuthas@citchennai.net', 'Paper Presentation'),
('18', 'gaganb2543@gmail.com', '19', '3/5/2026 6:15:24 AM +00:00', '3/5/2026 6:15:24 AM +00:00', 'Deep Learning – Driven Dermoscopic Image Analysis with Classification  for Skin Cancer Diagnosis', '-Skin cancer is one of the most common and serious diseases worldwide, and early detection is essential for effective \ntreatment. Traditional diagnosis relies on visual examination by dermatologists, which can be subjective, time-consuming, and difficult \nto access in remote areas. To overcome these limitations, this project presents an AI-assisted skin cancer detection system using the \nEfficientNet-B3 deep learning model to classify dermoscopic images as benign or malignant with high accuracy. The system uses the \nHAM10000 dataset, and images undergo preprocessing steps such as resizing, hair removal, normalization, and contrast enhancement. \nThe fine-tuned EfficientNet-B3 model extracts key features like colour, texture, and shape to achieve reliable lesion classification. An \nintegrated AI Consultant interprets predictions and provides risk guidance, preventive tips, and suggestions for nearby dermatology \nspecialists. Model performance is evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and other metrics. The final system is deployed \non a user-friendly web interface for real-time screening and improved accessibility.', 'Gagan B', 'gaganb2543@gmail.com', 'Gagan B (Mangalore Institute of Technology and Engineering)*', 'B, Gagan*', 'gaganb2543@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'AURA SCAN 2.pdf (431,936 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Deepa Rasheed (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences(Autonomous))', 'deepa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Likhith prasad  N B \nstudent\nMangalore Institute of technology and engineeringnb\nnblikhith@gmail.com\n\nAjay\nstudent\nMangalore Institute of technology and engineeringnb\najaynv24@gmail.com\n\nLesten Dsilva\nstudent\nMangalore Institute of technology and engineeringnb\nlestendsilva27@gmail.com\n\n\n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('19', 'alka.munjal@saii.siu.edu.in', '20', '3/5/2026 9:08:26 AM +00:00', '3/5/2026 9:08:26 AM +00:00', 'Automated Detection and Explainable Classification of Pituitary Tumors in Brain MRI Using Transfer Learning and Grad-CAM Saliency Visualization', 'Pituitary tumors account for 10–15% of primary intracranial neoplasms, frequently causing\nendocrine dysfunction and visual impairment. Manual MRI interpretation is time-intensive and\nsubject to inter-observer variability, particularly for microadenomas below 10 mm, highlighting\nan urgent need for automated diagnostic tools.\nTo develop and validate an end-to-end deep learning framework for automated pituitary tumor\ndetection from T1-weighted brain MRI, benchmarking multiple convolutional architectures and\nintegrating Grad-CAM for clinical interpretability.\nA dataset of 400 contrast-enhanced brain MRI images (sagittal and axial) underwent preprocessing\nvia intensity normalisation and CLAHE, followed by five-fold augmentation. Four models\nwere evaluated under identical conditions: a custom CNN baseline, VGG-16, EfficientNet-B0,\nand ResNet-50, using transfer learning with ImageNet weights. Performance was assessed via\n5-fold stratified cross-validation across accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, F1-score, and AUC-ROC.\nStatistical significance was confirmed using McNemar’s test (α = 0.05).\nResNet-50 achieved the highest performance: accuracy 97.3%, sensitivity 96.8%, specificity\n97.9%, F1-score 97.1%, and AUC 0.981, outperforming the baseline by 8.8 percentage points\n(p < 0.05). Grad-CAM maps confirmed anatomically coherent activation within the sellar region\nin 94.2% of tumor-positive cases.\nThe proposed framework demonstrates radiologist-level accuracy in pituitary tumor detection\nwith strong explainability, offering a scalable and clinically trustworthy AI decision-support\ntool for neuroradiology. Future work will target 3D volumetric analysis and multi-institutional\nvalidation.', 'Alka Munjal', 'alka.munjal@saii.siu.edu.in', 'Alka Munjal (SIU)*', 'Munjal, Alka*', 'alka.munjal@saii.siu.edu.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Conference_paper Dr Alka Munjal.pdf (117,556 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Kiran Thampi (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'kiran@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Alka Munjal\nSymbiosis Artificial Intelligence Institute, Symbiosis International (Deemed University),\nPune - 412115, India\nalkamunjal8@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('20', '23211a3206@bvrit.ac.in', '21', '3/6/2026 6:19:24 PM +00:00', '3/6/2026 6:19:24 PM +00:00', 'AI-Powered A Cross-Sector Predictive Employee Wellness Intelligence Framework Using Machine Learning With Risk Classification & Decision-Support Dashboard', ' The term employee well-being has gained a strategic status of different organizations today because it directly influences both productivity and engagement as well as long-term sustainability. Nevertheless, the majority of current workplace wellness platforms are based on surveys or post hoc analysis, which does not offer an early warning of a risk or a predictive intelligence. This is a limitation that does not allow organizations to adopt proactive intervention strategies. To bridge this gap, this study suggests a cross-sector predictive employee wellness Intelligence machine that incorporates the indicators of physical activity, the behavioral characteristics, and the measures of organizational performance through the application of supervised machine learning. This system is based on the formulation of employee wellness prediction as a structured classification problem and feature engineering to gain insightful behavioral patterns through meta-departmental data sources. It has created a scalable system that enables a flexible adjustment in any field within an organization which may be marketing, finance, R&D or sales. The predictive engine relies on many different ways of establishing the existing wellness risk rate and is also able to offer a real-time decision-support dashboard to track and plan intervention. The findings of several experimental reviews demonstrate that there was a common performance categorization and a high predictive consistency compared to the current procedures of applying the assessment to make wellness judgments. The new structure provides organizations with capacity to transform their reactionary to active data driven enterprise intelligence solution by involving early identification of possible wellness threats in order to establish preventive interventions by the organization. \nAlso, the project is based on a portal using secure login analytics to be able to upload your own datasets, execute automated predictions, interactively view dashboards, and produce recommendations. The reality with this setup demonstrates how practical this framework is as an AI-driven decision support platform for businesses.\n', 'Azeeb Architha', '23211a3206@bvrit.ac.in', 'Azeeb Architha (college)*; K Khushi (college); B Harshitha (college); K Bhima (college)', 'Architha, Azeeb*; Khushi, K; Harshitha, B; Bhima, K', '23211a3206@bvrit.ac.in*; 23211a3226@bvrit.ac.in; 23211a3208@bvrit.ac.in; bhima.k@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Workforce Development, Wellbeing, and Leadership', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'IEE Batch-21[2]_compressed.pdf (367,629 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Kiran Thampi (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'kiran@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Azeeb Architha\nStudent\n23211a3206@bvrit.ac.in\nB V Raju Institute Of Technology, Narsapur, Medak, Telangana, India\nK.Khushi\nstudent\n23211a3226@bvrit.ac.in\nB V Raju Institute Of Technology, Narsapur, Medak, Telangana, India\nB.Harshitha\nstudent\n23211a3208@bvrit.ac.in\nB V Raju Institute Of Technology, Narsapur, Medak, Telangana, India\nK.Bhima\nbhima.k@gmail.com\nHead of Department\nB V Raju Institute Of Technology, Narsapur, Medak, Telangana, India\n\n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('21', 'as2147@srmist.edu.in', '22', '3/7/2026 5:47:35 AM +00:00', '3/7/2026 5:47:35 AM +00:00', 'DrowsyGuard: A Real-Time Driver Drowsiness and Yawn Detection System', 'DrowsyGuard is a real-time browser-based fatigue detection system that monitors driver alertness using a standard webcam and computer vision and geometry analysis. The Eye Aspect Ratio (EAR) and Mouth Aspect Ratio (MAR) algorithms are used by the system to identify the two main signs of driver fatigue: yawning and eye closure (drowsiness). Implemented entirely in client-side JavaScript using TensorFlow.js and the MediaPipe FaceMesh model, the system extracts 468 three-dimensional facial landmarks per frame, computes geometric ratios from specific landmark subsets, and applies adaptive per-user calibration to derive personalized detection thresholds — eliminating the need for manual configuration.', 'Aryan Sinha', 'as2147@srmist.edu.in', 'Aryan Sinha (SRM INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY)*; Adhiraj Pratap Singh (SRM INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY); Anish Sarkar (SRM INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY); M Prakash (SRM INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY)', 'Sinha, Aryan*; Pratap Singh, Adhiraj; Sarkar, Anish; Prakash, M', 'as2147@srmist.edu.in*; as6082@srmist.edu.in; as1950@srmist.edu.in; prakashm2@srmist.edu.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Research paper.pdf (421,029 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Mathew John (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'mathewtj@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Aryan Sinha , Student , SRM Institute of Science and Technology, as2147@srmist.edu.in\nAdhiraj Pratap Singh , Student , SRM Institute of Science and Technology, as6082@srmist.edu.in\nAnish Sarkar , Student , SRM Institute of Science and Technology, as1950@srmist.edu.in\nDr. M Prakash , Professor , SRM Institute of Science and Technology, prakashm2@srmist.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('22', 'gopicse24@gmail.com', '23', '3/7/2026 11:48:44 AM +00:00', '3/7/2026 11:48:44 AM +00:00', 'HIGH ACCURACY PHISHING URL DETECTION AND PREVENTION SYSTEM USING TRANSFORMER AND LIGHTGBM MODELS WITH REAL TIME USER ALERTS  ', 'The main goal of this research is to design and build an intelligent phishing URL detection system using machine learning techniques methods that outperform traditional blacklist and rule-based systems by offering higher accuracy, better reliability, and faster real-time detection. Two approaches were evaluated. Group 1 represents the existing phishing detection method give the another words in exactly human written Group 2 uses the proposed PhishNet framework, which is based on an optimized LightGBM model combined with risk-based heuristics to extract URL features. A 95% confidence level was used to test statistical significance across the full dataset of phishing and legitimate URLs.The independent samples t-test results indicated that the performance gain of the new PhishNet model over the existing model was statistically significant Levene’s test was used because the results indicated unequal variances (F=6.742, p=0.001)new model was found to have a higher Gaint (562.31)=14.52,p<0.001, with a difference of 8.22 (CI: 7.23,9.20).Evaluated to current manual and rule-based systems, PhishNet provides more reliable and accurate phishing detection. The model improves dependability and offers real-time support for user-facing phishing protection platforms, browsers, and security systems.', 'GOPINATH  V', 'gopicse24@gmail.com', 'GOPINATH  V (K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING)*; Deebika R (K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING); Hemalatha S (K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING); Harshitha N (K.S.R. Institute for Engineering and Technology); gayathri M (K.S.R. Institute for Engineering and Technology); Bhuvaneshwari  P (K.S.R. Institute for Engineering and Technology)', 'V, GOPINATH *; R, Deebika; S, Hemalatha; N, Harshitha; M, gayathri; P, Bhuvaneshwari ', 'gopicse24@gmail.com*; rdeebikame@gmail.com; hemalatha00.cse@gmail.com; harshithanallasivam@gmail.com; gayathrimv0208@gmail.com; bhuvanapalanivel14@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'URL_Phishing_Detection.docx (330,816 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Mathew John (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'mathewtj@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', '1)Name: Deebika R\nPosition:Assistant Professor\nEmail:rdeebikame@gmail.com\nInstitution:K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING\n\n2)Name: Hemalatha S\nPosition: Associate Professor\nEmail: hemalatha00.cse@gmail.com\nInstitution: K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING', 'Paper Presentation'),
('23', 'Edith.blit-cohen@mail.huji.ac.il', '24', '3/7/2026 11:30:02 PM +00:00', '3/7/2026 11:33:40 PM +00:00', 'Striving for intergenerational equity: Youth environmental activism as a pursuit of social justice', 'Background: Climate change is fundamentally a social justice issue, representing a profound intergenerational inequity. The younger generation faces disproportionate health and environmental risks, as they will experience the most severe consequences of the climate crisis. In response to this injustice, a growing number of adolescents are engaging in environmental activism, demanding a voice in the decisions that will shape their future health and well-being.\nResearch Objectives: This study examines the experiences of youth activists, focusing on their motives for engagement, the challenges they face within social and institutional structures, and the meanings they attribute to their struggle for environmental justice.\nDesign: The study employs a qualitative approach, based on in-depth interviews with 15 youth activists from diverse geographical and social backgrounds across Israel.\nResults: Analysis revealed four primary themes: motives rooted in a sense of social responsibility and justice; the identity-shaping meaning of activism; navigating complex relationships with family and immediate environments; and strategic action in educational, protest-based, and digital arenas. The findings highlight that youth perceive their activism not merely as a hobby, but as a struggle for their fundamental right to a healthy and sustainable future.\nConclusions and Practical Implications: For these youth, activism is a source of agency and social mission, yet it is often accompanied by the burden of \"eco-anxiety\" and social friction. Environmental social work plays a critical role in promoting social justice by identifying these burdens and providing spaces for support. Furthermore, social workers must act as advocates for \"health equity\", mediating between youth and institutional powers (schools, government, and local authorities) to ensure young people are recognized as active partners in addressing the environmental and social crises.\nKeywords: Youth Activism, Environmental Justice, Climate Change, Intergenerational Equity, Qualitative Research, Environmental Social Work\n', 'Edith Blit-cohen', 'Edith.blit-cohen@mail.huji.ac.il', 'Edith Blit-cohen (Hebrew University)*', 'Blit-cohen, Edith*', 'Edith.blit-cohen@mail.huji.ac.il*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'environmental activism.docx (24,461 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Mathew John (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'mathewtj@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Professor Edith Blit-Cohen, \nSchool of Social Work and Social Welfare, Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Israel\nEdith.blit-cohen@mail.huji.ac.il\nContact number: +972523339239\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('24', 'jkaurdsf27@iifmbhopal.edu.in', '25', '3/8/2026 5:06:43 AM +00:00', '3/8/2026 5:06:43 AM +00:00', 'Reaching the Unreached: A Community-Based Task-Shifting Model for Digital Mental  Health Support in Rural Primary Health Centers ', ' To address the severe shortage of mental health professionals in low-resource rural \nsettings in India, this study evaluates the feasibility and preliminary effectiveness of a stepped-care \nmodel integrating digital mental health tools with task-shifting. The primary objective was to train \ncommunity health workers (CHWs) to deliver a technology-assisted psychological intervention to \nadults with common mental disorders (CMDs) in rural Madhya Pradesh. \nDesign: A mixed-methods quasi-experimental design was employed across 15 rural primary health \ncenters (PHCs) over 14 months. A total of 85 female CHWs were trained to use a tablet-based \napplication featuring culturally adapted screening tools and guided self-help modules in Hindi. \nQuantitative data on patient outcomes (PHQ-9 and GAD-7 scores) were collected from 410 \nparticipants at baseline, 3 months, and 6 months. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with \n25 CHWs and 30 patients to explore acceptability, barriers, and facilitators. \nModel: The intervention utilized a collaborative stepped-care model. CHWs conducted initial \nscreenings using the digital application. Patients with mild to moderate symptoms received brief \nbehavioral interventions delivered via the app, supported by weekly in-person check-ins from the \nCHW. Complex or severe cases were referred to a central mental health specialist at the district \nhospital through a tele-consultation feature integrated within the same platform, ensuring specialist \nsupervision and a clear referral pathway despite geographical distances and limited connectivity. \nResult: A total of 410 participants were enrolled (68% female, mean age 38.4 years). Intention-to\ntreat analysis showed a statistically significant reduction in mean PHQ-9 scores from 14.2 (baseline) \nto 7.8 (6 months) (p < 0.001) and GAD-7 scores from 12.1 to 6.4 (p < 0.001). Qualitative findings \nhighlighted high acceptability due to the privacy of the digital tool and the trusted relationship with \nlocal CHWs. Key challenges included intermittent internet connectivity in remote locations and initial \ndigital literacy barriers among older CHWs, addressed through peer mentoring.', 'Jasleen Kaur', 'jkaurdsf27@iifmbhopal.edu.in', 'Jasleen Kaur (Indian Institute of Forest Management Bhopal)*', 'Kaur, Jasleen*', 'jkaurdsf27@iifmbhopal.edu.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'A Community-Based Task-Shifting Model for Digital Mental Health Support in Rural Primary Health Centers.pdf (206,452 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Mathew John (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'mathewtj@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Jasleen Kaur MBA student in Development and Sustainable Finance jkaurdsf27@iifmbhopal.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('25', 'swathiumaa@gmail.com', '26', '3/8/2026 12:22:44 PM +00:00', '3/8/2026 12:22:44 PM +00:00', 'Multimodal AI-based Emotion Monitoring Early Warning System in Healthcare', 'It is essential to appreciate the role of emotion improving the health and recovery trajectory of patients intensive care units (ICU\'s). However, existing monitoring protocols within ICU\'s focus primarily on physical vital signs heart rate, blood pressure and oxygen saturation - while emotional states (stress, fear, panic or confusion) remain largely  unmonitored, despite their importance as potential precursors in a patient\'s physical or mental decline. This paper proposes to address these shortcomings by creating an Emotion-Aware Early Warning System for ICU Patients, which is an AI-based framework for real-time awareness and monitoring, analysis,and interpretation of patients’ emotional state.To ensure continuous monitoring of emotional states, the proposed system uses multiple sources of input, including facial expressions, voice tone, micro-expressions, and patterns of body movement.Advanced deep learning and sentiment analysis techniques will be used to effectively and accurately identify emotional states.The system will use a patients’ usual baseline behavior to detect emotional states as an alert to abnormal distress. If severe negative shifts occur for the patient—anxiety, agitation, and  either alerts will trigger to notify the medical team through potential alerts or notifications on the ICU monitor dashboard of the distressful status.', 'SWATHI K', 'swathiumaa@gmail.com', 'Kokilavani T ( Velalar College of Engineering and Technology  Erode); SWATHI K (Velalar College of Engineering and  Technology  Erode,)*; Yathishna  S J ( Velalar College of Engineering and Technology  Erode); Rahul G (Velalar College of Engineering and Technology  Erode)', 'T, Kokilavani; K, SWATHI*; S J, Yathishna ; G, Rahul', 'kokilavanit12@gmail.com; swathiumaa@gmail.com*; yathishna2006sj@gmail.com; rahul.tn56@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Multimodal AI-based Emotion Monitoring Early  Warning System in Healthcare  final.docx (441,908 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Nycil Thomas (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'nycil@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Swathi K – Student – Velalar College of Engineering and Technology – swathiumaa@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('27', 'madhuruba59@gmail.com', '29', '3/11/2026 5:16:36 AM +00:00', '3/11/2026 5:16:36 AM +00:00', 'An Integrated Telehealth Platform for Modern  Healthcare Services ', 'The rapid growth of digital healthcare has \nincreased the demand for platforms that provide easy access to \nmedical services. This paper presents a Smart Telehealth and \nRemote Wellness System, a web-based dashboard designed to \nimprove healthcare accessibility and user convenience. The \nsystem integrates features such as a chatbot for instant \nassistance, online medicine ordering, and doctor appointment \nscheduling. These functionalities help users quickly access \nhealthcare information, book appointments, and order \nmedicines through a single platform. The proposed system \nreduces manual processes, saves time, and enhances the overall healthcare experience through an intuitive and efficient digital interface.', 'Madhuruba R', 'madhuruba59@gmail.com', 'Madhuruba R (K.S Rangasamy College of Technology)*; Mahalakshmi G P (K.S Rangasamy College of Technology); Mahalakshmi K (K.S Rangasamy College of Technology); DineshKumar P (K.S Rangasamy College of Technology)', 'R, Madhuruba*; G P, Mahalakshmi; K, Mahalakshmi; P, DineshKumar', 'madhuruba59@gmail.com*; vgpmahamamatha@gmail.com; mahapravin2003@gmail.com; drdineshkumarphd24@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Madhuruba R , Mahalakshmi G P (2).pdf (905,137 bytes); paper 2026.pdf (678,076 bytes)', '2', '', '0', 'Nycil Thomas (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'nycil@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Name: Mahalakshmi G P\nPosition: Student\nInstitution : K. S. Rangasamy college of Technology\nEmail Id: vgpmahamamatha@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('28', 'aditya.lh17@gmail.com', '30', '3/11/2026 1:47:57 PM +00:00', '3/11/2026 1:47:57 PM +00:00', 'Intelligent Leukemia Diagnosis Using Graph-Based Deep Learning and  Clinical Feature Relationships  ', 'Leukemia represents a severe form of \nblood malignancy that disrupts healthy blood cell \nformation, demanding prompt and precise \nidentification to enhance survival outcomes. \nConventional approaches depend on labor\nintensive, error-prone evaluations of blood metrics \nby clinicians, often leading to delays in critical \ninterventions. Our initiative introduces a smart \ndiagnostic platform utilizing Graph-based Neural \nArchitectures to explore intricate interconnections \nwithin blood-related clinical data, transforming \nraw hematological inputs into actionable insights. \nBy structuring 20 essential hematological \nindicators—such as white blood cell counts, \nhemoglobin levels, platelet distribution, and \nlymphocyte percentages—as graph nodes and \nedges, the model adeptly captures feature \ninterdependencies and relational patterns \noverlooked by standard techniques. A graph\noriented AI framework, built using PyTorch, \nanalyzes these inputs to separate leukemia cases \nfrom non-leukemic ones, identifying subtle \npatterns in patient profiles via information \nexchange across the network structure. After \ndiagnosis, the system evaluates and displays \nseverity levels based on feature deviations, aiding \nin risk stratification. Deployed via a user-friendly \nweb interface built with HTML, CSS, JavaScript \nfor dynamic frontend interactions, and a Python \nFlask API for robust backend processing, it \ndelivers instant results, including interactive \nvisualizations of predictions and severity scores for \nbetter clinical insight.', 'Aditya L', 'aditya.lh17@gmail.com', 'Aditya L (Sathyabama Institute of Science and Technology)*', 'L, Aditya*', 'aditya.lh17@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'IntelLeuke.pdf (803,573 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Nycil Thomas (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'nycil@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Name : Ajay Keerthi E\nPosition : Under Graduate Student\nInstitution : Sathyabama Institute of Science and Technology', 'Paper Presentation'),
('29', 'chandrusuriyaprakasam@gmail.com', '31', '3/11/2026 4:50:29 PM +00:00', '3/11/2026 4:50:29 PM +00:00', 'A Federated Inter-Institutional Academic Curated Knowledge-Sharing Platform with Event-Triggered Content Moderation', 'StudySphere is a federated inter-institutional academic collaboration and curated knowledge-sharing platform designed to support secure and scalable cooperation among autonomous educational institutions. Unlike centralized academic systems that face challenges related to data ownership, privacy, and interoperability, StudySphere adopts a federated architecture in which institutions retain control over their local academic data while sharing standardized metadata for collaborative discovery. The platform follows a cloud-native, microservices-based design that enables institution-specific workspaces, distributed knowledge exchange, and cross-institution research collaboration. A relevance-aware federated search mechanism is integrated to enhance the discovery of academic resources using distributed metadata indexing and semantic relevance ranking. In addition, event-triggered content moderation is incorporated during content submission, updates, and inter-institutional sharing events. Automated content validation employs lightweight natural language processing techniques for domain classification and semantic relevance assessment of academic metadata. This hybrid approach combines rule-based validation with embedding-based similarity scoring to maintain topic alignment and academic integrity while minimizing manual moderation overhead. By performing moderation and classification locally at institutional nodes, the system preserves data privacy, institutional autonomy, and federated principles, providing a scalable and privacy-preserving foundation for trusted academic collaboration within higher education ecosystems.\n\nKeywords—Federated systems, content moderation, taxonomy-driven knowledge hub, NLP\n\n', 'Chandru S', 'chandrusuriyaprakasam@gmail.com', 'Chandru S (R.M.K Engineering College)*', 'S, Chandru*', 'chandrusuriyaprakasam@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'STUDYSPHERE_ A FEDERATED INTER-INSTITUTIONAL ACADEMIC CURATED KNOWLEDGE-SHARING PLATFORM WITH EVENT TRIGGERED CONTENT MODERATION.pdf (333,992 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Rajeev SP (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'rajeev@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Akila T\nAssistant Professor\nDepartment of Information Technology\nR.M.K. Engineering College\nChennai, India\ntaa.it@rmkec.ac.in\n\nLokesh E\nDepartment of Information Technology\nR.M.K. Engineering College\nChennai, India\nloke22052.it@rmkec.ac.in\nChandru S\nDepartment of Information Technology\nR.M.K. Engineering College\nChennai, India\nchan22016.it@rmkec.ac.in\nDinesh S\nDepartment of Information Technology\nR.M.K. Engineering College\nChennai, India\ndine22021.it@rmkec.ac.in\n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('30', 'skrithickajay@gmail.com', '32', '3/11/2026 10:00:58 PM +00:00', '3/11/2026 10:00:58 PM +00:00', 'HAVEN - AN ANONYMOUS MENTAL HEALTH PEER SUPPORT PLATFORM ', 'Abstract— Mental health challenges among students and young adults are increasingly prevalent, yet access to timely support remains limited. Haven is an anonymous, peer-to-peer mental health support platform designed to bridge this gap. The platform facilitates safe, confidential communication between individuals seeking guidance and trained peers, fostering emotional well-being and resilience. Leveraging a user-friendly web and mobile interface, Haven incorporates real-time chat, anonymized interactions, and resource sharing to provide immediate support. This paper presents the design, implementation, and evaluation of Haven, highlighting its potential to improve mental health outcomes through accessible peer engagement. Initial pilot studies indicate enhanced user engagement, reduced stigma, and positive feedback regarding emotional support and community building. Haven demonstrates how technology-driven solutions can complement traditional mental health services, offering scalable, secure, and empathetic support networks.', 'Krithick S', 'skrithickajay@gmail.com', 'Krithick S (Panimalar Engineeering College)*', 'S, Krithick*', 'skrithickajay@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'haven_conference_paper_final_cochin.pdf (549,868 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Rajeev SP (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'rajeev@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'AUTHOR 1 :\nKrithick Ajay S\nDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering,\nPanimalar Engineering College, Chennai, Tamilnadu, India\nskrithickajay@gmail.com\n\nAUTHOR 2:\nLohith Adithya M\nDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering,\nPanimalar Engineering College, Chennai, Tamilnadu, India lohithadithya2006@gmail.com\n\nAUTHOR 3:\nDr. Jackulin C\nDepartment of CSE\nPanimalar Engineeing College\nChennai, India\nchin.jackulin@gmail.com\n\nAUTHOR 4:\nMrs.P.Vijayalakshmi \nDepartment of CSE\nPanimalar Engineeing College\nChennai, India\ncseviji2021@gmail.com \n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('31', 'banupriya12317@gmail.com', '33', '3/12/2026 4:52:30 AM +00:00', '3/12/2026 4:52:30 AM +00:00', 'SLEEP DISORDER PREDICTION VIA SMARTPHONE SCREEN TIME PATTERNS', 'Sleep disorders, including insomnia and disrupted sleep quality, are increasingly influenced by prolonged smartphone usage during nighttime. Continuous interaction with mobile screens delays sleep onset, causes frequent awakenings, and negatively impacts overall sleep health. This paper presents a deep learning-based approach for predicting sleep disorder risks using non-intrusive smartphone screen-time behavior. The system collects structured data such as night screen-on duration, unlock frequency, and bedtime phone usage, which are preprocessed and fed into a multilayer perceptron model for classification. The proposed solution eliminates the need for clinical equipment or wearable sensors, making it highly scalable and cost-effective. By analyzing digital behavior patterns, the model assists in early identification of unhealthy sleep habits and provides insights that may encourage users to adopt better sleep hygiene. The work demonstrates that smartphone usage patterns serve as reliable digital biomarkers for continuous monitoring of sleep health.', 'Biruntha S', 'banupriya12317@gmail.com', 'Sreenithi Nataraj (Dr. N.G.P Institute of Technology); Reshmaa SS (Dr. N.G.P Institute of Technology); Sindhuja R (Dr. N.G.P Institute of Technology); Biruntha S (Dr. N.G.P Institute of Technology)*', 'Nataraj, Sreenithi; SS, Reshmaa; R, Sindhuja; S, Biruntha*', 'sreenithi.nataraj@gmail.com; reshmaass72@gmail.com; rsindhuja925@gmail.com; banupriya12317@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '1Fpreconference.pdf (393,125 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Rajeev SP (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'rajeev@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Reshmaa SS – Student, Department of Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, Dr. N.G.P Institute of Technology, Coimbatore, India – reshmaass72@email.com\n\nSindhuja R – Student, Department of Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, Dr. N.G.P Institute of Technology, Coimbatore, India – rsindhuja925@email.com\n\nSreenithi N - Student, Department of Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, Dr. N.G.P Institute of Technology, Coimbatore, India – sreenithi.nataraj@email.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('32', 'kvinothcse85@gmail.com', '34', '3/12/2026 4:53:44 AM +00:00', '3/12/2026 4:53:44 AM +00:00', 'EFFICIENT NET-B0 MODEL FOR AUTOMATED ORAL LESION CLASSIFICATION AND EARLY ORAL  CANCER DETECTION', 'Aim: In this study,an automated deep learning technique for the early detection of oral cancer is introduced. The technique classifies oral cavity images into three classes namely normal, pre-cancerous, and cancerous using the EfficientNet-B0 model. Materials and Methods: Group 1 (Existing System) The existing system applied ResNet-101 and Faster R-CNN on 2155 oral images. Group 2 (Proposed System) The proposed system uses an EfficientNet-B0 to classify oral lesions on kaggle 1312 oral images which results in approximately 95% accurate performance and requires minimal computational resources. Result: The result confirm that EfficientNet-B0 achieved through 94.0% to 95.8% inference time  of 114.60 milliseconds, whereas ResNet-101 operators with an accuracy range of 87.9% to 90.0% process data at 148.60miliseconds and this improvement is statistically significant(p<0.001), demonstrating its effectiveness for oral cancer detection. Conclusion: These results show that the EfficientNet-B0 model which we developed achieves better accuracy in oral cancer classification. The system requires less time to process and has simplified design elements which make it appropriate for use in actual clinical screening that requires fast and efficient resource usage.', 'Vinoth  KeralaVarman', 'kvinothcse85@gmail.com', 'Vinoth  KeralaVarman (K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING)*; LATHA R (K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING); PRAKASHAM V (K.S.R. COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING); KAVYA P (K S R INSTITUTE FOR ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY); SACHIN R (K S R INSTITUTE FOR ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY); SHEELA S (K S R INSTITUTE FOR ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY)', 'KeralaVarman, Vinoth *; R, LATHA; V, PRAKASHAM; P, KAVYA; R, SACHIN; S, SHEELA', 'kvinothcse85@gmail.com*; lara1181@gmail.com; vprakashamcse@gmail.com; kavyakeerthika0208@gmail.com; sachinramesh206@gmail.com; sheelaselvam09@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Final conference paper (1).pdf (853,010 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Geok Ling Lee (National University of Singapore)', 'swklgl@nus.edu.sg', '', '', '', '', 'Co-authors 1 : \nR. LATHA\nAssistant Professor\nDepartment of CSE\nK.S.R. College of Engineering\nTiruchengode-637215\nlara1181@gmail.com\n\nCo-authors  2 :\n V. PRAKASHAM\n Assistant Professor\n Department of CSE\n K.S.R. College of Engineering\nTiruchengode-637215\nvprakashamcse@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('33', 'wsnsusi@gmail.com', '35', '3/12/2026 8:37:43 AM +00:00', '3/12/2026 8:37:43 AM +00:00', 'Stress at work and its implications on employee retention in social work in health and mental health.', 'Introduction\nHealth and mental health social workers are essential in offering psychosocial support, intervention of crises, and community-based services. Nonetheless, they can be largely exposed to work related stress due to the stressful nature of their work, which is combined with high caseload, emotional labor and administrative duties. The growing rates of stress among social workers have been a significant issue because they adversely impact the wellbeing of the workforce and lead to a high turnover rate of staff working in health and mental health facilities.\nObjective\nThe primary aim of this research was to determine the correlation between work stress and employee retention of social workers in health and mental health disciplines, as well as uncovering the main aspects of work that contribute to stress and intentions of the staff to stay in their jobs or quit.\nDesign\nThe present study took a mixed-method research design with a combination of quantitative and qualitative designs. Quantitative data on workplace stress and job satisfaction along with retention intentions were gathered with the help of a structured questionnaire, and semi-structured interviews were held to learn more about the experiences of social workers in their workplaces. Data was collected among 60 social workers. \nModel\nThe research is anchored on a theoretical framework that interconnects workload, emotional requirements, supervisory support, and organizational recognition with stressors at work as the main predictors of staff retention as measured by job satisfaction and staying in the profession.\nResults\nThe results show that overworking, emotional burnout, a low level of supervisory support, and inadequate professional recognition are important factors that enhance stress levels among social workers in the workplace. Increased stress levels were also established to be highly related to low job satisfaction and increased desire of employees to exit their organizations.\nConclusion\nThe research indicates the significance of organizational support, strong leadership, and workloads and wellbeing programmes in alleviating work stress. By reinforcing these, the staff retention level could be enhanced and can help create a resilient and sustainable social work workforce in health and mental health services.\nKeywords: Stress, Employee retention, Social work, Health and mental health.\n', 'Susith Siriwardhana', 'wsnsusi@gmail.com', 'Susith Siriwardhana (University of Colombo)*', 'Siriwardhana, Susith*', 'wsnsusi@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Workforce Development, Wellbeing, and Leadership', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Stress at work and its implications on employee retention in social work in health and mental health..docx (14,136 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Geok Ling Lee (National University of Singapore)', 'swklgl@nus.edu.sg', '', '', '', '', 'No Co Authors', 'Paper Presentation'),
('34', 'anusreehari29@gmail.com', '36', '3/12/2026 9:51:48 AM +00:00', '3/12/2026 9:51:48 AM +00:00', 'Multi-Modal Deep Learning Framework for Early Detection and Prediction of Diseases in Dairy Cattle Using Visual, Environmental, Physiological, and Behavioral Data', 'The essential goal of the current research is to design a new multi-modal Deep Learning (DL) to track the initial phases of disease progression in milk cows and forecast it. The system combines visual (RGB and IR) data, environmental (Temperature- Humidity Index) data, physiological (heart rate, skin temperature) data and behavioral data (activity, rumination) to provide a more comprehensive automated health inspection device to the field of precision dairy farming. However, most of the currently existing solutions are silo-based, i.e. visual cues-based or wearable-based. The procedure of data integration of heterogeneous data on Precision Beef and Animal Gait on visual/behavioral training and IoT based smart cattle health monitor data on physiological / environmental indicators will be used as the methodology. The given architecture is called Cattle-MultiModalNet, and it is based on a three-branch architecture of processing visual gait and facial information, behavioral and physiological sensor information, and spatial environmental information through a Graph Neural Network (GNN). These characteristics are integrated with the help of Cross-Attention mechanism which gives modalities weights which are fixed dynamically on the quality of data and usefulness of this data to the particular disease markers. The proposed multi-modal framework was found to be 94.8% more accurate than the single-modality baselines (visual: 82.3%, physiological: 78.5), as well as IoT baseline systems (73.2%). Disease-specific detection: Lameness (96.5 percent), heat stress (97.2 percent), mastitis (93.8 percent), respiratory disease (91.5 percent) and metabolic disorders (92.8 percent) all had the highest results. The study proves that a single DL model based on multiple streams of data offers a more robust and precise health monitoring system to dairy cattle.  ', 'Anusree K', 'anusreehari29@gmail.com', 'Anusree K (Karunya Institute of Technology and Science)*', 'K, Anusree*', 'anusreehari29@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Multi-Modal Deep Learning Framework (1).pdf (623,303 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Giji George (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences, Kalamassery)', 'gijigeorge@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Dr. M. Rajeswari3\n3Department of Computer Science and Engineering,\nKarunya Institute of Technology and Science,\nTamil Nadu, India\nrajeswari@karunya.edu\n\nDr. C. Priyadharshini4\n4Department of Computer Science and Engineering,\nKarunya Institute of Technology and Science,\nTamil Nadu, India\npriyadharshini@karunya.edu', 'Paper Presentation'),
('35', 'Sk6476@srmist.edu.in', '37', '3/12/2026 6:56:20 PM +00:00', '3/12/2026 6:56:20 PM +00:00', 'Interpretable Multimodal Machine Learning Framework for Cognitive Severity Estimation in Alzheimer’s Disease Using MRI-Derived Brain Features and Blood Biomarkers', 'Alzheimer disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative\ndisease that is associated with memory impairment,\ncognitive impairment and atrophy of the brain in its structure.\nConventional clinical assessment methods do not allow early\ndiagnosis. This paper presents a multimodal machine learning\nmodel that would combine both structural MRI images\nand blood biomarkers early diagnosis of Alzheimer disease.\nThe suggested model involves modality-sensitive preprocessing,\nfeatures selection based on SHAP-based importance ranking,\nand classification based on ensemble models, including XGBoost\nand Random Forest. Predictive accuracy The experimental test\nshows that multimodal fusion is highly superior to the unimodal\nmodels. The framework proposes an interpretable, scaled,\nand non-invasive framework of AI-aided neurodiagnostic. Index\nTerms—Alzheimer’s Disease, MRI, Blood Biomarkers, Machine\nLearning, Multimodal Learning, XGBoost, Explainable AI, ROCAUC.\nIndex Terms—Alzheimer’s Disease, MRI, Blood Biomarkers,\nMachine Learning, Multimodal Learning, XGBoost, Explainable\nAI, ROC-AUC', 'Sarvesh Kumar', 'Sk6476@srmist.edu.in', 'Sarvesh Kumar (SRM INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY )*; Adarsh Anand (SRM INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY)', 'Kumar, Sarvesh*; Anand, Adarsh', 'Sk6476@srmist.edu.in*; aa0759@srmist.edu.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Alzheimer_Research_Paper.pdf (867,297 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Nycil Thomas (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'nycil@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Sarvesh Kumar, Student, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, sk6476@srmist.edu.in\nAdarsh Anand, Student, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, aa0759@srmist.edu.in\nDr. G. Niranjana, Professor, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, niranjag@srmist.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('36', 'pbmadhangesh@gmail.com', '38', '3/12/2026 8:24:02 PM +00:00', '3/12/2026 8:24:02 PM +00:00', 'PEER TO PEER COMMUNICATION WITH BLOCKCHAIN', 'Peer to Peer communication with blockchain is a decentralized peer-to-peer communication platform integrating blockchain technology, end-to-end encryption, file sharing, and AI-based intrusion detection. Unlike centralized messaging services vulnerable to single points of failure and censorship, this system stores messages immutably on a local proof-of-work blockchain ensuring data integrity and non-repudiation.\n\nThe architecture combines a WebSocket-based P2P server for real-time routing, RSA encryption for secure private communications, file transfers up to 10MB via base64 encoding, and an intrusion detection system using Isolation Forest algorithm to identify anomalous behavior like message flooding. Messages are encrypted with recipient public keys and signed with sender private keys. The blockchain records only metadata—message type, sender/recipient, timestamps, and signatures—while routing content directly between peers, creating an auditable, tamper-evident ledger.\n\nThe IDS continuously monitors behavioral features including message frequency and file sizes to trigger real-time alerts. The graphical interface provides chat with file attachment, blockchain explorer, network topology visualization, and intrusion detection dashboard. Performance analysis shows efficient concurrent user handling with minimal latency, balanced blockchain mining difficulty, and effective anomaly detection after sufficient training.\n\nThis research contributes a secure, decentralized foundation for applications requiring data integrity, privacy, and censorship resistance.', 'Madhangesh  Bhoobathi ', 'pbmadhangesh@gmail.com', 'Madhangesh  Bhoobathi  (Sri Krishna College of Engineering and Technology )*', 'Bhoobathi , Madhangesh *', 'pbmadhangesh@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Paper testing.docx (1,937,566 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Giji George (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences, Kalamassery)', 'gijigeorge@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Renjitha R , Supervisor , Sri Krishna College Of Engineering and Technology \nMadhangesh , Student , Sri Krishna College Of Engineering and Technology , pbmadhangesh@gmail.com\nKaviyan ,Student , Sri Krishna College Of Engineering and Technology , 727722EUEC080@skcet.ac.in\nJotheeshwaran ,Student , Sri Krishna College Of Engineering and Technology , 727722EUEC072@skcet.ac.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('37', 'bandreddilakeshbabu@gmail.com', '39', '3/13/2026 6:30:48 AM +00:00', '3/13/2026 6:30:48 AM +00:00', 'IoT Based Forest Deforestation And Anti-Poaching Of Animals And Resources In Forest', 'Abstract—Forests are essential and very important ecosystems that face the threat of illegal logging, poaching and encroachment by human beings. The proposed project implies a smart solution of real-time and affordable monitoring of forests with ESP32 microcontroller with other sensors and machine learning to preserve biodiversity and prevent deforestation. It is an integrated camera system and ML-based object detection that can identify the presence of humans within a particular region within the forest (with lightweight objects model such as YOLO Nano). Besides visual designation, the system has acoustic detectors used to capture the signature sound waves of chainsaws and gunshots among the poaching or illegal logging activities. PIR motion sensor is applied at the first level of movement detecting to activate the camera or any other modules to save the energy. Also, the system will be equipped with a GPS module based on geotagging events with geographic accuracy and temperature/humidity sensors that allow tracking the environment that may be applied in the evaluation of wildfire risks and monitoring ecological data. ESP32 controls all the intelligent components and processes the information and transmits it to the remote monitoring center using either GSM or Wi-Fi. Every notification includes the nature of event, location information (GPS coordinates), time stamps and in some cases, imagery that gives the forest rangers or authorities the information to swiftly act on any threat. The project is a low-power scalable and smart solution, which employs IoT and embedded AI in order to enhance forest monitoring and sustainable environmental management.\nkeywords:IoT, Forest Monitoring, Deforestation Prevention, Anti-Poaching, ESP32, Machine Learning.\n\n', 'BANDREDDI BABU', 'bandreddilakeshbabu@gmail.com', 'BANDREDDI BABU (SATHYABAMA INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY)*', 'BABU, BANDREDDI*', 'bandreddilakeshbabu@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'CUTTING EDGE TECHNIQUE IN DIGITAL AUDIO REFINEMENT AND MANIPULATION reasearch paper (1) sri ram 2.docx (50,479 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Giji George (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences, Kalamassery)', 'gijigeorge@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'BANDREDDI LAKESH BABU\nSTUDENT\nSATHYABAMA UNIVERSITY\nbandreddilakeshbabu@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('39', 'preetish192005@gmail.com', '41', '3/13/2026 2:35:47 PM +00:00', '3/13/2026 3:03:47 PM +00:00', 'Deep Learning-Based ECG Image Classification System for Early   Detection of Cardiac Conditions ', ' This research proposes a convolutional neural network \n(CNN) based method for automated ECG image classification \nmethod in order to support the early detection of heart diseases. \nThe ECG image data set that is used for this study is collected from the open source Kaggle repository and consists of four clinically important categories of Myocardial Infarction, History of \nMyocardial Infarction, Abnormal Heartbeat and Normal ECG \nimages. All the images are preprocessed, where the images are \nresized and normalized to a fixed size of 224*224 pixels with a \nfocus on increasing training stability and performance. A deep \nCNN architecture with several convolutional and max pooling \nprocessing layers is trained by using the Adam optimizer and a \ncategorical cross entropy loss function. The results of the training is shown and there is a steady increase in the validation accuracy over the number of epochs, showing an effective feature learning is being achieved. Finally, the trained model is deployed using a Streamlit based web application allowing real-time ECG image classification including probability-based prediction output. \nKeywords: ECG Image Classification, Convolutional Neural \nNetwork, Heart Disease Detection, Deep Learning, Medical Image Analysis ', 'Preetish M', 'preetish192005@gmail.com', 'Preetish M (Sathyabama institute of science and technology)*', 'M, Preetish*', 'preetish192005@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Deep Learning-Based ECG Image Classification System for Early.pdf (947,236 bytes)', '1', 'Deep Learning-Based ECG Image Classification System for Early.pdf (947,236 bytes)', '1', 'Bincy C C (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'bincy@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', '1)S.Pravin\nSathyabama institute of science and   \ntechnology \nChennai,India \npravinseenu.123@gmail.com \n\n2)Dr.D.Deepa \nAssistant professor \nSchool of computing \nSathyabama institute of science and \ntechnology \nChennai,India \ndeepa21me@gmail.com\n\n3)Ms.S.Hemalatha \nAssistant professor \nSchool of computing \nSathyabama institute of science and \ntechnology \nChennai,India \nhemalatha.cse@sathyabama.ac.in\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('40', 'khanalgayatri2@gmail.com', '42', '3/14/2026 5:16:47 AM +00:00', '3/14/2026 5:22:36 AM +00:00', 'Effectiveness of Mental Health Literacy among School Students of Kailali District, Nepal: A Pre-Post Study Design ', 'Abstract \nIntroduction \nCompromised mental health literacy (MHL) among young people is associated with pernicious effects. Improving MHL among the cohort creates a ripple effect that increases community-wide understanding, acceptance of mental health care, empowers youth as agents of change. Hence, study aimed to evaluate the effectiveness of an intervention in enhancing MHL.\n\nMethods \nMHL of students was assessed before and after the intervention using the Mental Health Literacy Questionnaire–Young Adults among 362 students. Intervention was designed for 180 min and delivered into two phases allocating 90 min for each phase covering on mental health knowledge, stigma/misconceptions, mental health prevention, treatment seeking and strategies for self-help. Descriptive, Wilcoxon rank sum test and the linear mixed model analysis was performed by using IBM SPSS 20. \n\nResults \nCompared to the prior intervention, significant increase in knowledge on mental health issues, first-aid skills, erroneous beliefs, and seeking help was noticed after the intervention (Z = -8.932, p < 0.0001). There was an increment of 5.6 points in MHL score after the intervention which confirmed a positive effect of the intervention. Female participants had 1.7 higher MHL scores compared to male counterparts.  Parental education played a crucial role on MHL score.\n\nConclusion \nFindings suggest the significance of intervention to enhance MHL of school students. Female gender and parents’ education status were facilitating factors for enhancing MHL while mothers’ status (housewife) had negative impact on MHL. Future research may use randomized controlled trials to validate the findings and assess whether the improvements persist over time.\n\nKeywords: Mental Health Literacy, Intervention, Students, Kailali \n', 'khanal gayatri', 'khanalgayatri2@gmail.com', 'khanal gayatri (SRM Institute of Science and Technology)*', 'gayatri, khanal*', 'khanalgayatri2@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract and author names.docx (17,238 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Nycil Thomas (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'nycil@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', '1.	Gayatri Khanal, PhD Scholar, School of Public Health, Faculty Medicine and Health Science, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, Chennai, Tamilnadu, India, Email: khanalgayatri2@gmail.com  and  gk3837@srmist.edu.in, ORCID ID: 0000-0002-1260-1781\n2.	Dr. Y. Selvamani, Assistant Professor, School of Public Health, Faculty Medicine and Health Science, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, Chennai, Tamilnadu, India, Email: selvamay@srmist.edu.in  ORCID ID: 0000-0002-5393-8495 \n3.	Prabhat Sapkota, Consultant (Psychiatry), Nisarga Hospital and Research Centre, Dhangadhi, Kailali, Nepal, Email: prabhatsapkotabpkihs@gmail.com, ORCID ID: 0009-0008-3919-5796\n4.	Raghul Gandhi Venkatesan, Centre for Statistics, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, Tamil Nadu, India, Email: drrgstat@gmail.com , ORCID ID: 0000-0001-8624-8282\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('41', 'niti.shah.811@gmail.com', '43', '3/14/2026 9:09:17 AM +00:00', '6/29/2026 9:40:34 AM +00:00', 'Robust Depression Detection from Voice Data Using Self-Supervised Learning on the Extended DAIC-WOZ Dataset', 'The issue of depression is one of the most common and serious disorders in modern society, affecting millions of people across the globe. This disorder often remains unreported or goes unnoticed, owing to the fear of social stigma and limited access to healthcare services. Furthermore, there exists a significant gap between actual diagnosis and objective assessment of depression in humans due to the highly subjective nature of current diagnosis procedures. Recent advances in artificial intelligence and accumulation of vast amounts of digital information, however, enable developing a range of novel tools for automated diagnosis of various diseases.\nThis paper proposes an advanced, noise-tolerant approach to detecting depression based on voice samples using self-supervised learning methods. Contrary to existing approaches focusing predominantly on hand-crafted acoustic features, the present model combines deep speech embeddings generated using wav2vec 2.0 architecture with prosodic and spectral features proven clinically useful. One of the main contributions of the proposed methodology consists in incorporating a temporal segmenter and noise handling system into the architecture, enhancing stability of the model under realistic settings with interference.\nThis model training has been carried out via subject-independent evaluation protocols, and optimization has been achieved with respect to approaches related to imbalanced classes and lack of labeled data. From experimental analysis, there is clear evidence of superior performance than baseline models related to machine learning and deep learning methods, especially in terms of recall and F1-score. The latter is particularly important in reducing misses during clinical settings. In summary, these results support the viability of the voice as a digital biomarker.\nResearch aligned with SDG Goals: Robust Depression Detection from Voice Data Using Self-Supervised Learning on the Extended DAIC-WOZ Dataset aligns well with multiple United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) as it is directly contributing to Mental health care, innovation and well-being of a society in a sustainable way. It is directly aligned with SDG 3 – Good Health and Well-being, SDG 9 – Industry, Innovation, and Infrastructure, SDG 10 – Reduced Inequalities and in-directly aligned with SDG 4 – Quality Education, SDG 16 – Peace, Justice, and Strong Institutions.', 'Niti Shah', 'niti.shah.811@gmail.com', 'Niti Shah (Silver Oak University)*; Dr. Satvik Khara (Silver Oak University)', 'Shah, Niti*; Khara, Dr. Satvik', 'niti.shah.811@gmail.com*; satvik.khara@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'IEEE - Robust Depression Detection from Voice Data Using Self-Supervised Learning on the Extended DAIC-WOZ Dataset.pdf (490,268 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Bincy C C (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'bincy@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Dr. Satvik Khara\nDean, College of Technology\nSilver Oak University \nAhmedabad, Gujarat, India\nSMIEEE\nsatvik@socet.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('42', 'smalayalaatanishk2004@gmail.com', '44', '3/20/2026 6:26:24 PM +00:00', '3/20/2026 6:26:24 PM +00:00', 'CREDIT CARD SCAM DETECTION USING A HYBRID CATBOOST AND DEEP NEURAL NETWORK FRAMEWORK', 'The use of digital payment platforms has increased\r\nrapidly in recent years, making online transactions easier and\r\nmore convenient for users. At the same time, the number of\r\ncredit card fraud incidents has also grown. For banks and\r\nfinancial service providers, identifying fraudulent transactions\r\nhas become an important challenge. Fraudulent activities usually\r\ndo not follow simple patterns. Instead, they often appear through\r\nunusual transaction behaviour that changes over time, which\r\nmakes detection difficult for traditional rule-based or static\r\nclassification methods.\r\nIn this work, a Credit Card Scam Detection System is proposed\r\nto study transaction behaviour over time. The system combines machine learning and deep learning techniques to analyse\r\npatterns in transaction data. Time-based feature engineering\r\nis used so that the order and timing of transactions can be\r\ncaptured and studied more effectively. Rather than making an\r\nimmediate binary decision at the early stages, the models first\r\nestimate the probability of a transaction being fraudulent. The\r\nfinal classification is made only at the last stage of the process.\r\nThis strategy helps the system handle datasets where genuine\r\ntransactions greatly outnumber fraudulent ones. The proposed\r\nmodel is also implemented through a secure web-based interface,\r\nallowing transactions to be analysed in real time and improving\r\nits usability in practical environments.\r\n', 'Malayalaa Tanishk  S', 'smalayalaatanishk2004@gmail.com', 'Malayalaa Tanishk  S (Panimalar engineering college )*', 'S, Malayalaa Tanishk *', 'smalayalaatanishk2004@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'IEEE PAPER .pdf (361,752 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Bincy C C (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'bincy@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Bhavani k, panimalar engineering college \nPramoth K, panimalar engineering college \nNandhakumar R, panimalar engineering college ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('43', 'jmenaga7112004@gmail.com', '45', '3/23/2026 7:14:46 AM +00:00', '3/23/2026 7:14:46 AM +00:00', 'Comprehensive Hospital Management System with Emergency Care, Appointments, and Real-Time Queue Management Using MERN Stack', 'Hospital outpatient departments face critical challenges including emergency response delays, appointment scheduling conflicts, extended patient queues, and poor resource visibility. This paper presents a comprehensive Hospital Management System (HMS) developed using MERN stack technology, integrating five specialized modules: Emergency Care Module for geospatial hospital recommendations, Doctor Availability Module for real-time schedule tracking, Appointment Booking Module with automated reminders, Queue Management Module for live patient status updates, and Admin Dashboard Module for operational analytics. The system eliminates information silos through unified MongoDB data storage and Socket.io real-time synchronization across all modules. Implementation results demonstrate 46% faster emergency response times, 78% appointment booking success rate, 40% reduction in average queue wait times, and comprehensive resource utilization tracking. The modular MERN architecture ensures scalability and maintainability for diverse healthcare environments while maintaining robust security through JWT authentication and role-based access control.', 'Menaga  J', 'jmenaga7112004@gmail.com', 'Menaga  J (P.S.R Engineering College)*; Hanishka S (P.S.R Engineering College)', 'J, Menaga *; S, Hanishka', 'jmenaga7112004@gmail.com*; hanishka796@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'mern stack.docx (8,866,388 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'SUNIROSE ISHNASSERY PATHROSE (Rajagiri Coleege of Social Sceinces)', 'sunirose@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Velselvi R\n Department of Computer Science and Engineering\n PSR Engineering College\nSivakasi , India\nvelselvi30@gmail.com\n\nRamathilagam A\nDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering\n PSR Engineering College\nSivakasi, India\nthilla2012@gmail.com\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('44', 'dekhnixtechnologies@gmail.com', '46', '3/24/2026 3:51:36 PM +00:00', '3/24/2026 4:23:24 PM +00:00', 'Residual GraphSAGE  Optimization for Intrusion Detection Systems', 'Graph-based modeling techniques are being used more and more by intrusion detection systems (IDS) to capture the intricate interactions between network components like hosts, flows, and communication sessions. Although Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) have demonstrated great promise in learning such relational structures, current GNN-based intrusion detection systems encounter a number of difficulties, such as a significant class imbalance between normal and attack traffic and representation degradation brought on by over-smoothing in deeper graph architectures. These problems frequently make intrusion detection less effective, especially for uncommon and complex assault tactics. This paper suggests a Residual GraphSAGE (RGS) framework for scalable and efficient intrusion detection in order to overcome these drawbacks. In order to enable deeper message transmission while maintaining crucial node properties and avoiding representation degradation, the suggested method integrates residual skip connections across graph aggregation levels. The model enhances its capacity to identify intricate structural patterns in network traffic data by preserving stronger feature propagation across layers. In this approach, network elements like flows or hosts are represented as nodes in graph representations created from network traffic data, and edges are produced based on shared attributes and communication links. The model can learn relational relationships between network components as well as node-level features thanks to this graph-based structure. Comprehensive tests on the UNSW-NB15 benchmark dataset show that the suggested Residual GraphSAGE model outperforms baseline GNN architectures and traditional machine learning techniques in terms of intrusion detection performance. The outcomes demonstrate residual graph learning\'s efficacy in managing complicated and unbalanced cybersecurity datasets. All things considered, the suggested method offers a reliable and scalable way to incorporate deep graph learning techniques into contemporary intrusion detection systems, advancing more sophisticated and flexible cybersecurity defences.', 'Karthik p', 'dekhnixtechnologies@gmail.com', 'Karthik p (sree vidyanikethan engineering college, tirupati.)*', 'p, Karthik*', 'dekhnixtechnologies@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'CHARISHMA_CONFERENCE PAPER.pdf (719,617 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Reena Cherian (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'reena@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'karthik ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('45', 'ak2039@srmist.edu.in', '47', '3/26/2026 6:40:15 PM +00:00', '3/26/2026 6:40:15 PM +00:00', 'Mapping Digital Attention: A Multi-tier Classification System for Personal Productivity Management', 'Chronic digital distraction, driven by algorithmically optimized feeds, compulsive context-switching, and habitual passive consumption, has emerged as a measurable threat to cognitive focus and mental well-being. While personal productivity trackers offer a pathway toward greater self-awareness of these habits, existing local-first tools rely on brittle, manually maintained rule sets and rigid keyword matching that cannot keep pace with the semantic complexity of modern web activity. Platforms such as YouTube and Reddit simultaneously serve as educational resources and entertainment sinks, rendering simple URL-based classification clinically unreliable for digital well-being interventions. This paper presents Focus Journal, a privacy-preserving, on-device productivity and well-being analytics system designed to bridge the gap between raw behavioral telemetry and meaningful psychological self-reflection. The core contribution is a multi-tier, zero-shot Natural Language Inference (NLI) pipeline, optimized via ONNX Runtime with INT8 quantization, that performs real-time semantic classification of browser and desktop activity without transmitting any data externally. The system moves beyond binary productive/distractive labels to identify granular behavioral patterns directly linked to well-being degradation, including doom-scrolling, binge-watching, and recommendation rabbit holes, enabling users to recognize and interrupt harmful digital habits through an interpretable local dashboard. Evaluation on a 200-case heterogeneous benchmark achieved 95.5% overall classification accuracy with worst-case inference latency below 1.05 seconds on a standard consumer CPU, validating the feasibility of continuous, privacy-safe semantic monitoring as a practical tool for digital well-being.', 'Ashutosh  Kushwaha', 'ak2039@srmist.edu.in', 'Ashutosh  Kushwaha (SRM Institute Of Science and Technology)*; Dr. P.S. Thanigaivelu (SRM Institute of Science and Technology)', 'Kushwaha, Ashutosh *; Thanigaivelu, Dr. P.S.', 'ak2039@srmist.edu.in*; thanigap2@srmist.edu.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Mapping Digital Attention Multi Tier Classification System for Productivity Management.docx (285,268 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'SUNIROSE ISHNASSERY PATHROSE (Rajagiri Coleege of Social Sceinces)', 'sunirose@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', '\ndm4636@srmist.edu.in	Devish	Mittal	SRM Institute of Science and Technology	India', 'Paper Presentation'),
('46', 'sabinal@bezeqint.net', '48', '3/27/2026 11:09:17 AM +00:00', '3/27/2026 12:03:10 PM +00:00', 'Identity Vulnerabilities and Digital Coping: Pathways from Early Adversity to Social Media Addiction among Generation Z  Men', 'Objectives. Social media addiction (SMA) has become a growing concern among young adults, particularly Generation Z, for whom digital environments are central to identity formation, social interaction, and emotional regulation. At the same time, adverse childhood experiences (ACEs) are associated with long-term challenges in self-worth and relational functioning. This is especially relevant among young men navigating evolving yet conflicting norms of masculinity. The present study examines how self-esteem and fear of missing out (FOMO) are linked to the association between ACEs and SMA among Israeli Gen Z men.\nDesign. Grounded in Sociometer Theory and Compensatory Internet Use Theory, the study conceptualizes social media engagement as a potential means of regulating perceived deficits in belonging and social value. This framework situates digital behaviors within broader relational and identity-related processes.\nModel. A sequential mediation model was specified in which ACEs are associated with self-esteem and FOMO as interrelated processes, and SMA as an outcome. Self-esteem reflects internalized perceptions of social worth, while FOMO captures heightened sensitivity to exclusion. Data were collected via an online survey among 361 men aged 18–29, and analyzed using PROCESS Model 6.\nResults. ACEs were associated with lower self-esteem and higher FOMO. Self-esteem was negatively associated with both FOMO and SMA, whereas FOMO was positively associated with SMA. The direct association between ACEs and SMA was not significant when these variables were included.\nConclusion. The findings suggest that early adversity is reflected in identity-related processes linked to self-worth and belonging, which are associated with more intensive social media engagement. From a trauma-informed perspective, such patterns may reflect coping rather than a discrete behavioral problem. Interventions should address self-esteem, belonging, and relational security, using gender-sensitive and developmentally attuned approaches that recognize digital environments as key contexts of emotional regulation.\n', 'Sabina Lissitsa', 'sabinal@bezeqint.net', 'Sabina Lissitsa (Ariel University)*', 'Lissitsa, Sabina*', 'sabinal@bezeqint.net*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'abstract.docx (16,133 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Nycil Thomas (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'nycil@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Maya Kagan, Associate Professor, Ariel University, mayaka@ariel.ac.il', 'Paper Presentation'),
('47', 'revathythirunavukkarasu11@gmail.com', '49', '3/27/2026 11:20:33 AM +00:00', '3/27/2026 11:20:33 AM +00:00', 'DeepVeinDetect: A Machine Learning Approach for Early Varicose Vein Diagnosis', 'Venous enlargement and twisting are the hallmarks ofvaricose veins, a common vascular condition. often leading to discomfort, pain, and complications if left untreated. Early diagnosis is crucial for effective treatment and prevention of disease progression. This project introduces DeepVeinDetect, a web-based diagnostic tool designed to assist in the early identification of varicose veins using machine learning techniques. The system leverages im- age-based data to train and validate a classification model capable of distinguishing between healthy and affected vein patterns. By integrating this predictive model into a user-friendly web application built using the Django framework, DeepVeinDetect offers an accessible platform for healthcare professionals and individuals to assess potential symptoms and seek timely medical consultation. The suggested method shows how machine learning may improve diagnostic precision and aid in clinical judgment in the field of vascular health. ', 'Revathy T', 'revathythirunavukkarasu11@gmail.com', 'Revathy T (Panimalar Engineering College)*; shruti s (Panimalar Engineering College)', 'T, Revathy*; s, shruti', 'revathythirunavukkarasu11@gmail.com*; shrutishankar811@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'publishedLastFinal1 (1).pdf (493,252 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Reena Cherian (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'reena@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Karunkuzhali ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('48', 'claire.mccartan@northerntrust.hscni.net', '50', '3/27/2026 11:32:29 AM +00:00', '3/27/2026 11:36:41 AM +00:00', 'Promoting green and blue spaces to support trauma-informed care for people impacted by the conflict in Northern Ireland', 'Background: The physical health needs of people with severe and enduring mental health problems are often neglected (Pereira, 2019) and trauma exposure adds additional health risks including early incident cardiovascular disease and cardiovascular mortality (Burg & Soufer, 2016; Lohr et al., 2015). Spending time in nature can convey physical and mental health benefits and has led to the development of a wide range of nature-based interventions designed to promote wellbeing, physical health, and social inclusion for people with severe and enduring mental health problems (Coventry et al., 2021). Less, however, is understood about the potential benefits and efficacy of day-to-day routine access to outdoor green and blue spaces for people affected by and living with trauma. Northern Ireland’s 30-year political conflict, often referred to as the ‘Troubles’ has led to widespread community level trauma exposure that has been transmitted across generations. Specialist services have been developed to support those living with conflict-related trauma including social work-led multi-disciplinary teams in statutory health and social care and community and voluntary sector partners who offer wraparound social and welfare support.\nAims: This study explores the barriers/benefits of spending time outdoors with service users impacted by Troubles-related trauma and maps current provision of nature-based interventions across the Regional Trauma Network, a specialist trauma service spanning statutory and voluntary/community provision delivering care to victims and survivors of the Troubles in Northern Ireland.\nMethod: Using a co-produced mixed-methods design, qualitative interviews with service users were conducted by lived experience researchers about their use of green and blue spaces alongside a quantitative survey of trauma service providers involved in the RTN.\nResults: Thematic analysis and descriptive statistics will be presented.  \nDiscussion: Results will be discussed highlighting considerations for developing and delivering nature-based therapeutic interventions for trauma-exposed populations.', 'Claire McCartan', 'claire.mccartan@northerntrust.hscni.net', 'Claire McCartan (Northern Health & Social Care Trust)*', 'McCartan, Claire*', 'claire.mccartan@northerntrust.hscni.net*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'McCartan, Davidson & Mulholland - Promoting green and blue spaces to support trauma.docx (14,662 bytes)', '1', 'McCartan, Davidson & Mulholland - Promoting green and blue spaces to support trauma.docx (14,662 bytes)', '1', 'SUNIROSE ISHNASSERY PATHROSE (Rajagiri Coleege of Social Sceinces)', 'sunirose@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Professor Gavin Davidson, Professor of Social Care, Queen\'s University Belfast g.davidson@qub.ac.uk\nProfessor Ciaran Mulholland, Clinical Director, Regional Trauma Network & Queen\'s University Belfast c.c.mulholland@qub.ac.uk', 'Paper Presentation'),
('49', 'g.davidson@qub.ac.uk', '51', '3/27/2026 11:36:43 AM +00:00', '3/27/2026 11:40:32 AM +00:00', 'Mental health in all policies in Northern Ireland', 'Introduction: The Mental Health Strategy 2021-2031 for Northern Ireland is mainly focused on the development of mental health services but it includes the need for action to prevent mental health problems. Social work in Northern Ireland has a key role in highlighting and addressing ongoing issues related to the social determinants of mental health, equity and social justice. \nObjectives: This rapid evidence review was commissioned by the Department of Health in Northern Ireland to examine the literature on models and approaches to implementing a ‘Mental Health in All Policies’ approach and to identify the possible implications for Northern Ireland.\nDesign: A rapid evidence review approach was used which involves a systematic approach to searching the literature and is appropriate for relatively broad and complex topics. \nResults: \'Health in All Policies’ is an approach to public policies across sectors that systematically considers the health implications of non-health policies. ‘Mental Health in All Policies’ (World Health Organization, 2025) is an extension of the broader Health in All Policies approach, specifically focusing on mental health. It emphasises the need to integrate mental health considerations into policy-making across all sectors.\nWe identified international examples of Mental Health in All Policies in practice, at both national and local levels. Although there may be considerable benefits to achieving cross-government working on complex issues, such as mental health, there are also many challenges. This approach needs to be incentivised, appropriately resourced, supported by good data and further evaluated.\nConclusion: This presentation will present the key learning from the rapid review, examples of best practice, options for how to implement Mental Health in All Policies, and findings from some pilot evaluations in Northern Ireland.\n', 'Gavin Davidson', 'g.davidson@qub.ac.uk', 'Gavin Davidson (Queen\'s University Belfast)*; Claire McCartan (Northern Health and Social Care Trust); Ciaran Mulholland (Northern Health and Social Care Trust)', 'Davidson, Gavin*; McCartan, Claire; Mulholland, Ciaran', 'g.davidson@qub.ac.uk*; claire.mccartan@northerntrust.hscni.net; c.c.mulholland@qub.ac.uk', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Mental health in all policies in Northern Ireland.docx (18,028 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Reena Cherian (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'reena@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Gavin Davidson, Professor of Social Care, Queen’s University Belfast. Email: g.davidson@qub.ac.uk\nClaire McCartan, Senior Researcher, Regional Trauma Network; and Honorary Senior Lecturer, Queen’s University Belfast. Email: claire.mccartan@northerntrust.hscni.net \nCiaran Mulholland, Consultant Psychiatrist, Regional Trauma Network; and Clinical Senior Lecturer, Queen’s University Belfast. Email: c.c.mulholland@qub.ac.uk\n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('51', 'sohinimanne@gmail.com', '53', '3/27/2026 3:00:30 PM +00:00', '3/27/2026 3:00:30 PM +00:00', 'IoMT-Enabled Multimodal Framework for Real-Time Pulmonary Data Processing', 'Spotting lung diseases early is still tough for doctors, especially when they have to rely on just one type of test—like a chest X-ray or a single set of vital signs. These one-off checks often miss the bigger picture. They don’t always pick up on the subtle connections between different kinds of patient data, so it’s easy for problems to slip through the cracks. That’s where IMPACT-Lung comes in. We built this system for the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT), and it doesn’t just look at one thing. It brings together chest X-rays, ECG signals, and structured info from electronic medical records. Each type of data gets its own specialized encoder: ResNet-18 handles the images[1], a 1D-CNN-LSTM network[3] makes sense of the time-series signals, and a multi-layer perceptron deals with the structured clinical data. Then, a self-attention fusion module pulls these insights together, focusing on the stuff that matters most for each patient. When we tested IMPACT-Lung on a carefully chosen dataset, the results were striking: it nailed 100% accuracy, easily outpacing setups that only used one kind of data. And it’s fast—average inference takes just 24 milliseconds on a regular CPU, so it’s ready for real-time use right at the point of care. To make things clearer for clinicians, we added Grad-CAM and SHAP explanations[4][5], so users can actually see what the model’s thinking—both visually and in terms of key features.', 'Sohini Amrutha Manne', 'sohinimanne@gmail.com', 'Sohini Amrutha Manne (SRM Institute of Science and Technology)*; S Umesh Kumar  Reddy (SRM Institute of Science and Technology); Saravanan P (SRM Institute of Science and Technology)', 'Manne, Sohini Amrutha*; Reddy, S Umesh Kumar ; P, Saravanan', 'sohinimanne@gmail.com*; umeshreddy3005@gmail.com; saravanp7@srmist.edu.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Research Paper.docx (277,132 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Lorane Scaria (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'lorane@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', ' S Umesh Kumar Reddy, Computing Technologies, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, Kattankulathur,Chennai,India umeshreddy3005@gmail.com.\n\nSaravanan P, Assistant Professor, Computing Technologies, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, Kattankulathur, Chennai,India, saravanp7@srmist.edu.in.\n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('52', 'clifchina@gmail.com', '54', '3/28/2026 5:19:29 AM +00:00', '3/28/2026 5:19:29 AM +00:00', 'Mental Health Effects of Traumatic Victimization of Girls in India: A 3-D Theory Study', 'Background. The 3-D theory of polyvictimization (Emery, Abdullah, Ho, & Islas-Madlo, 2023) argues that the invasiveness and exploitativeness of victimization, as well as severity, must be included in any understanding of the impact of trauma. It also claims that relationships between dimensions of victimization must be understood multiplicatively. This is known as the multiplicative hypothesis which, when supported, suggests exponential increases in mental health risks when all three dimensions of trauma are high. Emerging evidence suggests both quantitative and qualitative support for 3-D theory. This study examines the 3-D hypotheses in the context of 3-D trauma and suicidal ideation among adolescent girls in India using preliminary data from a nationally representative study. Three-D theory may play a particularly important role in trauma effects for female victims in the context of conservative South Asian sex role norms.  \nObjective. Test for main effects, two-way, and three-way interaction effects of invasiveness, exploitativeness, and severity of traumatic victimization on adolescent suicidal ideation. Maternal mental health sequelae from 3-D childhood trauma are also examined.  \nParticipants and setting. Preliminary findings from 710 mother-daughter dyads come from an ongoing nationally representative, random, multi-stage cluster sample of 2,000 Indian mothers and their adolescent girls in 14 states and Delhi. Updated findings from the full sample will be presented at the conference.\nMethods. Regression models with adjustments for clustering within municipality were used to test the hypotheses. \nResults. 8% of girls and 15% of mothers had some suicidal ideation in the past two weeks. 24% of girls had been physically abused in the past year, 7% neglected, 22% emotionally abused, and 5.2% had experienced contact sexual abuse in the past year. 17% had been exposed to intimate partner violence. Age and urban residence were associated with increased suicidal ideation. A three-way interaction effect between trauma invasiveness, exploitativeness, and severity among adolescent girls was positively associated with adolescent suicidal ideation, indicating exponential increases in risk when all 3 dimensions are high. Controlling for PTSD symptoms, there were main effects of the invasiveness, exploitativeness, and severity of maternal childhood victimization on maternal depression. \nConclusion. Further research is needed to measure and investigate invasiveness, exploitativeness, and severity of traumatic victimization as they bear mental health consequences of trauma. Findings for girls suicidal ideation are consistent with the 3-D theory multiplicative hypothesis; risk for suicidal ideation may increase exponentially when all three trauma dimensions are high. Indian girls are at high risk for suicidal ideation. An estimated 13,000 students die by suicide in India each year (Krishnan, 2025).  The study suggests newly discovered dimensions of trauma may help to pinpoint those girls at highest risk. However, prompt and decisive policy interventions are needed to reduce the high levels of trauma experienced by Indian girls.  ', 'Clifton Emery', 'clifchina@gmail.com', 'Clifton Emery (University of Hong Kong)*', 'Emery, Clifton*', 'clifchina@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', 'Anish K R (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Assistant Professor)', 'anish@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Sneha Najeeb\nRohit Rajan\nProf. S. Rajan', 'Paper Presentation'),
('53', 'hellowjeevan@gmail.com', '55', '3/28/2026 11:11:38 AM +00:00', '3/28/2026 11:11:38 AM +00:00', 'Loneliness Is Becoming an Epidemic Among Youth in Urban Nepal: A Cross-Sectional Study', 'Abstract\nIntroduction: Youth mental health is an emerging public health concern in Nepal, with limited evidence available on depression, anxiety, and loneliness among young people aged 15 to 24 years.\nObjective: This study investigated the prevalence of loneliness and psychological distress (depression, anxiety, and stress) among youths aged 15–24 years residing in Kathmandu Valley.\nMethodology: A cross‑sectional survey was conducted with 195 purposively and voluntarily recruited participants. Data were collected using validated Nepali versions of the De Jong Gierveld Loneliness Scale and the DASS‑21 and analysed descriptively and correlationally using Microsoft Excel.\nFindings: Overall, 67.7% of respondents experienced loneliness, with 17.9% reporting extreme levels. Social loneliness (70.3%) was more prevalent than emotional loneliness (58.9%). High rates of anxiety (70.3%) and depressive symptoms (60%) were observed, particularly among female participants.\nConclusion: The substantial prevalence of loneliness and psychological distress among Nepali youth highlights the urgent need for culturally responsive, youth‑centred mental health services and school‑based psychosocial interventions.', 'Jeevan Bhusal', 'hellowjeevan@gmail.com', 'Jeevan Bhusal (Grampians Health)*', 'Bhusal, Jeevan*', 'hellowjeevan@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Resume Jeevan BHUSAL Oct 2025.docx (32,309 bytes); v1_covered_fdd4387a-f1cc-4ff2-9316-ffa458329d09.pdf (552,486 bytes)', '2', '', '0', 'Shilpa Yohannan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shilpa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Kripa Bhatta \nDepartment of Family, Fairness and Housing, Victorian Government Australia', 'Paper Presentation')
