('54', 'kkup.06009@gmail.com', '56', '3/29/2026 1:14:04 PM +00:00', '3/29/2026 1:14:04 PM +00:00', 'AI Based Mental Health Monitoring System', 'Abstract—Mental health awareness has gained\nsignificant importance due to increasing stress,\ncognitive overload, and emotional challenges in\nmodern digital lifestyles. This paper presents an\nAI-based mental health monitoring system designed to\nsupport mental wellness through conversational\ninteraction, emotional assessment, and visual\nanalytics. The system analyzes user-generated text to\nidentify emotional patterns and computes wellness\nindicators such as cognitive state, emotional balance,\nstress level, and focus. The proposed system utilizes a\nbrowser-based chatbot interface and a\nJavaScript-based backend built on Node.js, where\nuser messages are transmitted via RESTful APIs and\nprocessed using a large language model accessed\nthrough an OpenRouter-compatible AI service. Unlike\nclinical diagnostic tools, the system is intentionally\ndesigned as a non-clinical mental wellness support\nplatform, emphasizing user privacy, ethical\ninteraction, and accessibility. Experimental results\ndemonstrate effective emotion categorization, wellness\nscoring, and intuitive dashboard-based visualization,\nhighlighting the system’s potential for daily mental\nwellness monitoring.', 'Kaushal Kishor', 'kkup.06009@gmail.com', 'Kaushal Kishor (Galgotias University)*', 'Kishor, Kaushal*', 'kkup.06009@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Reserch paper (1).pdf (3,381,609 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Anish K R (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Assistant Professor)', 'anish@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Ankit Tiwari', 'Paper Presentation'),
('55', 'baashkarcse@gmail.com', '57', '3/30/2026 2:57:23 PM +00:00', '3/30/2026 2:57:23 PM +00:00', 'Decision-Aware Precision Harvesting Using Geospatial Crop Intelligence', 'While precision agriculture has witnessed remarkable advances in the accuracy of crop stress condition detection reports, there remains a significant void between pixel-level diagnosis and operational implementation. Existing harvesting practices rely heavily on the assumption of uniformity across the field, indiscriminately stripping the entire crop regardless of yield potential or physiological stress resulting in resource wastage and reduced yields. This work proposes a Decision-Aware Precision Harvesting framework that connects the geospatial perception and machinery action. We present a physics-based Crop Health Index (CHI) that integrates optical (NDVI), synthetic aperture radar (SAR), and thermal data by incorporating into a Gaussian deviation model, so as to quantify crop-specific optimality. In contrast to static health mapping, our system uses a spatiotemporal velocity term (Delta_CHI) fused into the classification of field zones where the crops are either recovering or degrading, allowing dynamic differentiations for both harvest (immediate), yield priority wait, and exclusion zones. These decisions are mapped to a navigation cost surface, denoted as J_Cost, which informs a selective path optimizing algorithm that minimizes machinery overlap and soil compaction risk. Evaluated by means of scalability analysis on a breadth of farm configurations, results reveal that conditioning harvest operations with geospatial intelligence can mitigate fuel consumption and environmental impacts while maximizing the quality of harvested products.', 'Baskar M', 'baashkarcse@gmail.com', 'Baskar M (SRM institute of science and technology)*; Chaitanya Saradhi (SRM Institute of Science and Technology); Sadwika Reddy (SRM Institute of Science and Technology); Vetriselvi D (SRM Institute of Science and Technology); Harini K (SRM Institute of Science and Technology)', 'M, Baskar*; Saradhi, Chaitanya; Reddy, Sadwika; D, Vetriselvi; K, Harini', 'baashkarcse@gmail.com*; ce5012@srmist.edu.in; dd4882@srmist.edu.in; vetri.raja.nilaa@gmail.com; harinii.kolanchi@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ieee.pdf (10,298,096 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Shilpa Yohannan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shilpa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'baashkarcse@gmail.com	Baskar	M	SRM institute of science and technology	India		 \n\nce5012@srmist.edu.in	Chaitanya	Saradhi	SRM Institute of Science and Technology	India		 \n\ndd4882@srmist.edu.in	Sadwika	Reddy	SRM Institute of Science and Technology	India		 \n\nvetri.raja.nilaa@gmail.com	Vetriselvi	D	SRM Institute of Science and Technology	India		 \n\nharinii.kolanchi@gmail.com	Harini	K	SRM Institute of Science and Technology	India', 'Paper Presentation'),
('56', 'stephenthomas@karunya.edu.in', '58', '3/31/2026 9:30:36 AM +00:00', '3/31/2026 9:30:36 AM +00:00', 'LocalRAG++: Systematic Evaluation of Hybrid Retrieval, Cross-Encoder Reranking, and OCR-Aware Document Quality for Privacy-Preserving Offline RAG', 'Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems of\nten rely on cloud infrastructure, raising privacy concerns for\nsensitive document collections. We present LocalRAG++, a sys\ntematic evaluation of fully offline, privacy-preserving RAG re\ntrieval pipelines. Using a benchmark of 114 evaluation questions\nwith gold evidence annotations, we compare dense, sparse,\nand hybrid retrieval with optional cross-encoder reranking. We\nconduct an ablation over reranking candidate counts (c ∈\n{10,20,40,60,80}) to characterize the quality–latency tradeoff.\nResults show that hybrid retrieval with 20 reranking candidates is\nthe most practical operating point, improving Precision@1 from\n0.754 to 0.789 and EvidenceHit@10 from 0.930 to 0.974, while\nmaintaining a 901ms warm mean per-query latency. Increasing\ncandidates beyond 20 yields diminishing quality gains with\nsuperlinear latency growth. Beyond retrieval, we introduce an\nOCR-aware document quality detection layer that classifies page\nlevel text extractability using 18 deterministic heuristic features\nand five quality classes at both page and document levels.\nValidated against human labels with 100% pilot agreement, this\nlayer reveals that 9 of 11 evaluated documents exhibit mixed\nquality despite appearing predominantly textual, demonstrating\nthat retrieval failures in offline RAG are often attributable to\nupstream ingestion quality rather than retrieval logic alone. We\nalso report cold-start initialization time separately from steady\nstate latency to better reflect deployment behavior in resource\nconstrained, air-gapped environments. All components operate\nfully offline on commodity CPU hardware.', 'STEPHEN THOMAS', 'stephenthomas@karunya.edu.in', 'STEPHEN THOMAS (Karunya University)*', 'THOMAS, STEPHEN*', 'stephenthomas@karunya.edu.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'LocalRAG____Systematic_Evaluation_of_Hybrid_Retrieval__Cross_Encoder_Reranking__and_OCR_Aware_Document_Quality_for_Privacy_Preserving_Offline_RAG_new.pdf (439,664 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Shilpa Yohannan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shilpa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Salaja Silas\nProfessor\nKarunya University\nsalaja_cse@karunya.edu', 'Paper Presentation'),
('57', 'pankajjagadale28@gmail.com', '59', '4/1/2026 4:19:15 AM +00:00', '4/1/2026 4:19:15 AM +00:00', 'AN ANALYTICAL STUDY OF DATA SHARDING AND REPLICATION STRATEGIES IN DISTRIBUTED DATABASE SYSTEMS', 'The existing backend infrastructure is faced with critical scalability challenges that cannot be addressed by the traditional centralized database architecture. This paper describes an extensive analytical study that compares the strategies of data sharding and replication in a distributed database environment. By means of in-depth architectural analysis and mathematical modeling, we assess the hash-based, range-based, directory-based, and geo-sharding approaches in combination with primary-replica, multi-primary, and quorum-based replication schemes. The results of the analysis show that the best compromise between load balancing (95% load distribution, 99.99% availability) and read operations can be achieved by hash-based sharding in combination with asynchronous primary-replica replication for read-intensive applications, whereas quorum-based replication with range sharding is most suitable for write-intensive applications.', 'Pankaj Jagadale', 'pankajjagadale28@gmail.com', 'Pankaj Jagadale (AISSMS IOIT, Pune)*; Nikil Kadam (AISSMS IOIT, Pune); Swapnil Jogdankar (AISSMS IOIT, Pune)', 'Jagadale, Pankaj*; Kadam, Nikil; Jogdankar, Swapnil', 'pankajjagadale28@gmail.com*; nikilkadam578@gmail.com; swapniljog7887@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'research_paper.pdf (1,079,217 bytes)', '1', 'graph.pdf (101,101 bytes)', '1', 'Shilpa Yohannan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shilpa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Nikil Kadam, Student, AISSMS IOIT, Pune, nikilkadam578@gmail.com	 \n\nSwapnil Jogdankar, Student AISSMS IOIT, Pune, swapniljog7887@gmail.com	', 'Paper Presentation'),
('58', 'choudharimanasi01@gmail.com', '60', '4/1/2026 5:58:21 PM +00:00', '4/1/2026 5:58:21 PM +00:00', 'AI-Based Wearable Vision Assistance System for Visually Impaired Individuals Using Raspberry Pi:  A Hybrid Deep Learning and Sensor Fusion Approach', 'Visual impairment is one of the major health challenges facing the world, with over 285 million people affected globally. It drastically affects the movement of people in complex environments. Conventional devices such as white canes and guide dogs can only sense the environment to some extent and are unable to communicate semantic information about objects. In this paper, the authors propose a novel wearable vision assistance system using AI technology (AI-WVAS), which is based on the Raspberry Pi 4B platform. It is designed to sense obstacles and recognize objects using ultrasonic time-of-flight sensors and YOLO-based convolutional neural networks, respectively. It utilizes a hybrid model with priority-weighted decision engine-based processing. Time-of-flight-based distance calculation is done using the time-of-flight formula with the HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor. Object classification is done using the quantized YOLOv5-nano model. Audio feedback is given using the pyttsx3 text-to-speech engine with context-aware priority scheduling. From the experimental results, the proposed wearable vision assistance system achieves 96.4% accuracy in obstacle detection, 82.7% mAP@0.5 in object classification, 310 ms latency, and 4.2 W power consumption. It is one such technology that is capable of providing the best possible wearable vision assistance technology to people with visual impairments.', 'MANASI CHOUDHARI', 'choudharimanasi01@gmail.com', 'MANASI CHOUDHARI (AISSMS IOIT)*', 'CHOUDHARI, MANASI*', 'choudharimanasi01@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'PAPER.docx (365,222 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Deepa Rasheed (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences(Autonomous))', 'deepa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Sainath Chavan - Student - AISSMS IOIT - csainath449@gmail.com\nSuhasi Bari - Student - AISSMS IOIT - barisuhasi2005@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('59', 'a.ibrahim1@squ.edu.om', '61', '4/3/2026 10:45:22 AM +00:00', '4/3/2026 10:45:22 AM +00:00', 'Preparing Social Work Students for AI-Integrated Mental Health Practice: Education, Skills, and Ethical Readiness — Evidence from Sultan Qaboos University', 'Objectives: This study examines the preparation of social work students for AI-integrated mental health practice, with a particular focus on educational preparation, professional skill development, and ethical readiness. As artificial intelligence increasingly reshapes health and mental health services, social work education must respond by equipping future practitioners to engage critically, competently, and ethically with emerging technologies while preserving the profession’s human-centred orientation.\nDesign: A descriptive educational study was conducted with 48 social work students enrolled in the course Social Work in Mental and Psychological Health at Sultan Qaboos University. Data was collected through a structured questionnaire designed to assess students’ awareness of AI applications in health and mental health services, their attitudes toward the integration of AI in future practice, and their perceptions of the educational, professional, and ethical requirements associated with technology-enhanced service delivery.\nModel: The study was informed by a three-dimensional readiness framework comprising educational readiness, professional skills readiness, and ethical readiness for AI-integrated mental health practice.\nResults: The findings revealed that students demonstrated a moderate level of awareness of AI applications in health and mental health services. Most participants expressed cautious attitudes toward the integration of AI into future practice, emphasizing concerns related to confidentiality, data protection, algorithmic bias, and the potential erosion of human interaction in service delivery. At the same time, students identified a clear need for curriculum enhancement, practice-oriented training, and stronger ethical guidance to support the responsible and professionally appropriate use of AI in social work settings.\nConclusion: The study indicates that although social work students are receptive to learning about AI-integrated mental health practice, their readiness remains emergent rather than fully developed. Strengthening social work education through targeted curricular content, applied learning opportunities, and ethically grounded training is essential to preparing graduates for evolving practice environments and ensuring a balanced integration of innovation and professional values.\nKeywords: Artificial intelligence; social work education; mental health practice; ethical readiness; professional skills; social work students; Sultan Qaboos University.\n', 'Ahmed Ibrahim', 'a.ibrahim1@squ.edu.om', 'Ahmed Ibrahim (College of Arts and Social Sciences, Sultan Qaboos University)*', 'Ibrahim, Ahmed*', 'a.ibrahim1@squ.edu.om*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Preparing Social Work Students for AI-Integrated Mental Health Practice.docx (15,230 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Anish K R (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Assistant Professor)', 'anish@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Prof Dr. Ahmed Thabet Helal Ibrahim\nDepartment of Sociology and Social Work, College of Arts and Social Sciences, Sultan Qaboos University, Oman\na.ibrahim1@squ.edu.om\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('60', 'dhanushkumar04824@gmail.com', '62', '4/4/2026 3:31:41 PM +00:00', '4/4/2026 3:31:41 PM +00:00', 'ATM Simulation with Invisible Keypad', 'ATMs and POS systems are still susceptible to\nphysical and cryptographic attacks, mainly because of the use\nof PINs with low entropy. This paper describes the design\nand development of a comprehensive ATM simulation platform\nto counter shoulder surfing, camera-based observation attacks,\nand cryptographic attacks. The proposed system includes an\ninvisible and dynamically rearranged digital keypad, spatially\naware ultrasonic proximity detection, and zero-knowledge proof\nauthentication. Instead of the usual card slot, the system includes\nan RFID contactless token, and it makes the entry of credentials\ninvisible using a hybrid spatial-frequency optical technique,\nmaking it difficult to track visually or through motion. In\naddition, the system uses the Schnorr Identification Protocol\nto ensure that credentials are never sent or stored on central\nservers, thus eliminating the possibility of offline dictionary\nattacks following a breach. To test the feasibility of the system,\na local hardware node with asynchronous IoT telemetric alerts\nwas simulated. Security and usability analyses demonstrate that\nthe comprehensive framework effectively resists current physical\nobservation attacks and cryptographic attacks while maintaining\na high transaction rate.', 'Dhanush P', 'dhanushkumar04824@gmail.com', 'Dhanush P (Velammal college of Engineering and Technology )*', 'P, Dhanush*', 'dhanushkumar04824@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ATM Simulation with Invisible Keypad.pdf (1,644,364 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Anu Mohan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'anumohan@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'M. SAKTHIVEL\nDepartment of ECE\nVelammal College of Engineering & Technology\nMadurai, India\nmsv@vcet.ac.in\n\nBethana Muneeswar M\nDepartment of ECE\nVelammal College of Engineering & Technology\nMadurai, India\nbethanamuneeswar823@gmail.com\n\nM. Madana gopal\nDepartment of ECE\nVelammal College of Engineering & Technology\nMadurai-625009, India\nlone.wolf388431@gmail.com\n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('61', 'siddharthsharma653@gmail.com', '63', '4/5/2026 4:17:10 PM +00:00', '4/5/2026 4:17:10 PM +00:00', 'MRH : MIXED REALITY HOLOGLASSES', 'Today\'s society is divided into two groups: those who can afford technology and those who can\'t. We want to make technology accessible to the average person with our suggested solution and shown prototype. We\'re all familiar with augmented reality and virtual reality, but what if we could merge both into our everyday lives? This is mixed reality, or MR, which runs about 40,000 Rupees for one piece of equipment. Here is our answer, which can be offered for the allocated amount of 8000 Rupees and executed. The main purpose of our project is to use scanning technology to create a 3D virtual representation of any 2D or 3D item that can be zoomed in and out using a smart glove. Interacting with the 3D virtual object that is seen on the headset is made feasible by such a glove. We may state It may be thought of as a particular kind of mouse that allows users to operate a 3D model with only a few hand motions. There are many models that can transport you to virtual reality, such as gaming systems, or include augmented reality, such as Pokemon Go, but as of yet, there is no device that can enable the construction of 3D virtual models as well as their depiction in real time. There is a great deal of research being done in this area to investigate the potential, but they are still not concentrating on developing an inexpensive model.\nKeywords: Mixed reality, MR, AR, VR, Smart Gloves, 3D virtual object.\n', 'Siddharth  Sharma', 'siddharthsharma653@gmail.com', 'Siddharth  Sharma (Dronacharya college of engineering)*', 'Sharma, Siddharth *', 'siddharthsharma653@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Research MRH.docx (4,333,981 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Anu Mohan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'anumohan@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Non', 'Paper Presentation'),
('62', 'darjansingh2003@gmail.com', '64', '4/7/2026 8:38:40 AM +00:00', '4/7/2026 8:38:40 AM +00:00', 'Hybrid Machine Learning Model for Early Disease Prediction Using Healthcare Big Data', 'In the modern healthcare domain, early detection of diseases plays a crucial role in improving patient outcomes and reducing mortality rates. This project proposes an intelligent hybrid machine learning framework for early disease prediction using healthcare big data. The model integrates multiple machine learning algorithms, including Random Forest and Logistic Regression, to enhance prediction accuracy and reliability.\nThe system utilizes diverse biomedical datasets such as patient history, laboratory test results, and vital signs to identify patterns and correlations associated with various diseases. Data preprocessing techniques such as normalization, missing value handling, and feature selection are applied to improve model performance. The hybrid approach combines the strengths of individual models to reduce overfitting and increase generalization capability.\nExperimental results demonstrate that the proposed model achieves higher accuracy compared to traditional single-model approaches. This system can assist healthcare professionals in making timely and informed decisions, enabling early diagnosis and effective treatment planning. The proposed solution contributes to the advancement of predictive healthcare analytics and supports the development of intelligent medical decision support systems.', 'DARJANSINGH N', 'darjansingh2003@gmail.com', 'DARJANSINGH N (PSNA COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY )*; JEEVANANDHAM M (PSNA COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY); HARIHARAN S (PSNA COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY); GOKULNATH M (PSNA COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY); AYYANAPRABU J (PSNA COLLEGE OF ENGINEERING AND TECHNOLOGY)', 'N, DARJANSINGH*; M, JEEVANANDHAM; S, HARIHARAN; M, GOKULNATH; J, AYYANAPRABU', 'darjansingh2003@gmail.com*; jeevanandhamkmp3@gmail.com; hariharansankar06@gmail.com; gokulnathmanivel@gmail.com; ayyanaprabu@psnacet.edu.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', 'Anu Mohan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'anumohan@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'GOKULNATH M, Student, PSNA College of Engineering and Technology, gokulnathmanivel@gmail.com\n\nHARIHARAN S, Student, PSNA College of Engineering and Technology, hariharansankar06@gmail.com\n\nJEEVANANDHAM M, Student, PSNA College of Engineering and Technology, jeevanandhamkmp3@gmail.com\n\nAYYANA PRABU J, Assistant Professor, PSNA College of Engineering and Technology, ayyanaprabhu@psnacet.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('63', 'tamanna.mehman@mountsinai.org', '65', '4/7/2026 8:06:51 PM +00:00', '4/7/2026 8:06:51 PM +00:00', 'Navigating Silence, Stigma, and Survival: Culturally Responsive Approaches to Gender-Based Violence in Diverse Communities in the Digital Age', 'Objectives:\nAs a South Asian Muslim clinician and daughter of immigrants, my work is shaped by navigating silence and stigma around sexual and gender-based violence (GBV). This presentation explores how social workers can engage more meaningfully with survivors from culturally diverse backgrounds, particularly BIPOC communities. It examines how culture, faith, migration, gender, and increasingly, artificial intelligence (AI), influence disclosure, help-seeking, and healing.\n\nDesign:\nThis work draws from clinical practice in GBV prevention, response, and education, alongside community engagement and reflective practice. Integrating survivor narratives, psychoeducation, and anti-oppressive, trauma-informed frameworks, it highlights both professional and lived perspectives. It also considers how survivors may engage with AI tools for psychoeducation, anonymous disclosure, and early support-seeking.\n\nModel:\nThe “Silence, Stigma, Survival” model captures the layered realities survivors navigate- unspoken norms, fear of shame within communities, and resilience. It emphasizes trust-building, identity-affirming interventions, and culturally attuned care, rather than a “one size fits all” modality. In a digital context, AI may help interrupt silence by increasing anonymity and access, but can also reinforce stigma through bias, lack of cultural nuance, and misinformation.\n\nResults:\nCulturally grounded approaches in working with survivors of GBV, whether through prevention, education, or response require fostering trust to increase disclosure. Community education that acknowledges culture and places it at the forefront opens dialogue where silence once prevailed. AI tools show potential to provide low-barrier support, yet present concerns about bias, inaccuracy, and ethical use.\n\nConclusion:\nSocial work must adapt to evolving help-seeking landscapes by addressing silence and stigma while critically engaging AI’s role. Culturally responsive, trauma-informed approaches are essential to ensure innovation expands access without reproducing harm and centers prevention as a core element of a world free of GBV.\n', 'Tamanna Mehman', 'tamanna.mehman@mountsinai.org', 'Tamanna Mehman (The Mount Sinai Hospital)*', 'Mehman, Tamanna*', 'tamanna.mehman@mountsinai.org*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '2027 SW Conference Abstract.docx (15,812 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Deepa Rasheed (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences(Autonomous))', 'deepa@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'N/A', 'Paper Presentation'),
('64', 'medhabasu2000@gmail.com', '66', '4/8/2026 9:54:18 AM +00:00', '4/8/2026 9:54:18 AM +00:00', 'Reconceptualizing Trauma and Agency in Sex Work: Towards a nuanced Trauma-Informed Feminist Social Work Framework', 'Trauma-informed practice has become central to social work, especially in work with marginalized populations such as sex workers. While it has advanced more ethical and sensitive care, dominant approaches often rely on narrow, victim-centred understandings of trauma that emphasize harm while overlooking agency. In sex work contexts, this can reinforce stereotypes and obscure the complex ways individuals navigate their lives.\nObjectives\nThis paper examines prevailing approaches to trauma-informed practice in sex work settings, focusing on their reliance on victim-centred frameworks. It aims to reconceptualize trauma as a subjective, context-dependent experience while foregrounding the interconnectedness of trauma and agency, and to develop a more balanced, context-sensitive framework.\nDesign \nThe study adopts a conceptual and analytical approach, drawing on interdisciplinary literature from social work, public health, and feminist theory. It combines a review of existing trauma-informed models with the author’s experiential insights from coursework, research, and fieldwork conducted during a Master’s programme in social work.\nModel\nThe paper proposes a nuanced trauma-informed framework integrating vulnerability with resilience, strategy, and negotiation. It situates trauma within structural conditions such as stigma, criminalization, and economic precarity, while incorporating an indigenous feminist social work perspective grounded in local knowledge systems and lived realities.\nResults\nThe analysis shows that dominant approaches often position sex workers as passive victims, overlooking the complexity of lived experiences. It highlights how trauma and agency are deeply interconnected, with individuals actively navigating risk, constraint, and opportunity.\nConclusion\nThe paper advances a more balanced understanding of trauma-informed practice that acknowledges both structural vulnerability and individual agency. It calls for moving beyond deficit-based models toward collaborative, ethically grounded, and context-sensitive approaches that better inform care, advocacy, and service provision in sex work settings.\nKeywords: Trauma-informed practice; social work; sex work; agency; resilience; structural vulnerability; feminist social work; indigenous frameworks; harm reduction; stigma\n', 'MEDHA BASU', 'medhabasu2000@gmail.com', 'MEDHA BASU (DEPARTMENT OF SOCIAL WORK, UNIVERSITY OF DELHI)*; MEENU ANAND (DEPARTMENT OF SOCIAL WORK, UNIVERSITY OF DELHI)', 'BASU, MEDHA*; ANAND, MEENU', 'medhabasu2000@gmail.com*; meenuanand75@rediffmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Rajagiri Conference Abstract.pdf (288,072 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Soon Noi GOH (Changi General Hospital)', 'soon_noi_goh@cgh.com.sg', '', '', '', '', 'Medha Basu, Doctoral Research Scholar, Department of Social Work, University of Delhi. \nPh: 9163306077\nEmail: medhabasu2000@gmail.com\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('65', 'santhidran@utar.edu.my', '67', '4/11/2026 6:25:36 AM +00:00', '4/11/2026 6:25:36 AM +00:00', 'Mindful Communication as a Pathway to Emotional Resilience and Compassionate Care in Healthcare Professionals', 'Objectives:\nThis study aims to explore how mindfulness-based interventions (MBIs) cultivate mindful communication among healthcare professionals, focusing on attentional regulation, emotional resilience, and compassionate care.\n\nDesign:\nA qualitative study using Interpretative Phenomenological Analysis (IPA) will be conducted with 15–25 healthcare professionals who complete a 4-week Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT) course in June 2026. Semi-structured interviews will be carried out 2–4 weeks post-training to capture lived experiences of mindful communication in clinical and interpersonal contexts.\n\nModel:\nThe study integrates the Professional Quality of Life (ProQOL) framework with Barrett’s Theory of Constructed Emotion to analyze how attentional training and emotional regulation translate into communication practices. This dual lens highlights how mindful communication reduces reactive patterns, fosters compassion satisfaction, and strengthens team cohesion.\n\nExpected Results:\nIt is anticipated that participants will report increased capacity to pause before responding, deeper listening, and greater empathy in patient and colleague interactions. The study expects to identify facilitators (structured practice, peer support) and barriers (time constraints, organizational culture) to embedding mindful communication in healthcare routines.\n\nConclusion:\nBy embedding communication-focused training within MBIs, healthcare organizations can foster resilience, reduce burnout, and promote compassionate care. This study will contribute to the global dialogue on integrating mindfulness into healthcare systems by emphasizing communication as both a skill and a relational practice.\n\nKeywords:\nMindful communication; healthcare professionals; mindfulness-based intervention; emotional resilience; compassion satisfaction; qualitative study\n', 'Santhidran Sinnappan', 'santhidran@utar.edu.my', 'Santhidran Sinnappan (UTAR)*', 'Sinnappan, Santhidran*', 'santhidran@utar.edu.my*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Mindful Communication as a Pathway to Emotional Resilience and Compassionate Care in Healthcare Professionals.pdf (94,140 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Soon Noi GOH (Changi General Hospital)', 'soon_noi_goh@cgh.com.sg', '', '', '', '', 'Dr. Sabbitan Balakrishnan (Co-Principal Investigator)\nPracticing Medical Doctor (Psychiatrist)\nHospital Sultan Haji Abdul Ahmad Shah Temerloh, Pahang, Malaysia\ndrsabbitan@moh.gov.my\n\nDr. Thurkadevi Munikanan\nPracticing Medical Doctor (Psychiatrist)\nHospital Miri Sarawak, Malaysia\nthurkadevi@moh.gov.my\n\nDr. Maartandan Suppiah\nAssistant Professor\nCollege of Arts and Sciences\nUniversiti Utara Malaysia, Malaysia\ns.maartandan.suppiah@uum.edu.my\n\nChung Suet Ching\nPost-graduate student\nInstitute of Postgraduate Studies (IPSR)\nUniversiti Tunku Abdul Rahman, Malaysia\ntracy97@1utar.my\n \n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('66', 'devmalhotra1023@gmail.com', '68', '4/13/2026 6:15:25 AM +00:00', '4/13/2026 6:15:25 AM +00:00', 'Study on the Effect of Physical Activity on the Mental Health of College Students', 'The psychological wellbeing of college students has\nturned into an ever-increasing problem in the global community,\nas the percentage of stress, anxiety, and depression cases is\nrising because of the pressure in their academic life and changes\nin lifestyle and social issues. Physical activity is also generally\nconsidered an effective non-pharmacological treatment of psychological well-being. The research paper assumes the connection\nbetween physical activity and mental health in college students\nand the extent to which the form, intensity, and frequency of\nactivity affect the most important mental health outcomes. It is\nsuggested to use a mixed-method research design, which implies\nthe combination of both quantitative survey data and qualitative\ninformation to obtain a complete picture of the phenomenon.\nThere are standardized measures like the International Physical\nActivity Questionnaire (IPAQ) and Depression Anxiety Stress\nScales (DASS-21) which are used to gauge the levels of physical\nactivity and mental health outcomes. It is hoped that the results\nwill indicate a strong negative correlation between physical\nactivity and psychological distress, and the presence of intervening variables, namely, the quality of sleep and psychological\nresilience. The research is relevant to the current literature as\nit fills the gaps in the literature on causal correlations and\nmultidimensional analysis of physical activity. The findings have\npractical implications to institutions of higher learning in coming\nup with specific intervention measures and facilitating the active\nlifestyles among the students to improve their well-being.\nIndex Terms—Physical activity, mental health, college students,\ndepression, anxiety, stress, well-being, IPAQ, DASS-21, resilience,\nsleep quality, mixed methods approach\n', 'Dev Malhotra ', 'devmalhotra1023@gmail.com', 'Dev Malhotra  (Chandigarh University )*; Avinav Avinav (Chandigarh University); Bhupinder Singh (Chandigarh University)', 'Malhotra , Dev*; Avinav, Avinav; Singh, Bhupinder', 'devmalhotra1023@gmail.com*; ku2004avinav@gmail.com; bhupinder.e12856@cumail.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'devcns.pdf (92,621 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Anu Mohan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'anumohan@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', '\ndevmalhotra1023@gmail.com	Dev	Malhotra	Chandigarh University (Student)		 \n\nku2004avinav@gmail.com	Avinav	Avinav	Chandigarh University(Student)		 \n\nbhupinder.e12856@cumail.in	Bhupinder	Singh	Chandigarh University(Supervisor)', 'Paper Presentation'),
('67', 'paulfayazpaul@gmail.com', '69', '4/14/2026 7:34:27 AM +00:00', '4/14/2026 7:34:27 AM +00:00', 'Adverse Childhood Experiences, Trauma, Substance Use, and Mental Health Among Eviction-Affected Homeless Adults in Delhi, India: A Cross-Sectional Study', 'Background: In urban India, state-led eviction and demolition drives targeting unauthoized colonies have emerged as a significant structural driver of homelessness. Individuals displaced from informal settlements often lack the financial resources to secure alternative housing, resulting in immediate street homelessness or relocation to temporary shelters. Such forced displacement may compound pre-existing vulnerabilities, including adverse childhood experiences (ACEs), trauma exposure, substance use, and untreated psychiatric conditions. Despite increasing demolition drives in Delhi, the mental health consequences among eviction-affected adults remain underexplored. Objectives: To examine the prevalence and relationship of ACEs, trauma-related symptoms, substance use, and psychiatric morbidity among adults rendered homeless following eviction from unauthorized colonies in Delhi, India. Material and Methods: A cross-sectional descriptive study was conducted among 118 adults who became homeless after eviction and were residing on the streets or in temporary shelters in Delhi. Participants were recruited using convenience sampling. Structured face-to-face interviews, Adverse Childhood Experiences (ACE) scale, the Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test (AUDIT), the Drug Abuse Screening Test (DAST), the PTSD Checklist for DSM-5 (PCL-5), and the NIMHANS screening tool for psychological problems were administered. Results: The findings show that the mean age of the participants was 37.9 years, with 44.1% male and 55.9% female. In the study, 75.4% reported high levels of childhood adversity, and PTSD was identified in 27.8% of participants. Depression symptoms (94.9%) and overall General psychological problems (72.9%) were prevalent. Alcohol use assessment revealed that 46.6% had some degree of alcohol-related problems, including low risk (16.9%), harmful use (12.7%), and dependence (16.9%). Drug use problems were present in 33.9% of participants, with 24.6% reporting moderate to substantial use. Multiple regression analysis demonstrated that ACE scores and overall psychiatric symptom burden were significant predictors of PTSD severity, jointly accounting for 41.9% of the variance in PCL-5 scores. Conclusion: Adults rendered homeless following eviction from unauthorized colonies in Delhi experience a pronounced convergence of early-life adversity, trauma exposure, substance use, and psychiatric morbidity. These findings highlight the mental health consequences of forced displacement and underscore the urgent need for integrated, trauma-informed, and community-based mental health services embedded within post-eviction rehabilitation and homelessness response systems in urban India.\n\nKeywords: Eviction, Unauthorized colonies, Homelessness, Adverse childhood experiences, Trauma; Substance use, Mental health, PTSD, & India.\n\n\n', 'Fayaz Paul', 'paulfayazpaul@gmail.com', 'Fayaz Paul (Rajagiri College of Social science )*', 'Paul, Fayaz*', 'paulfayazpaul@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '1', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract Paul F.A.docx (16,214 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Hadas Rosenne (Hadassah Medical Center)', 'hadasr@hadassah.org.il', '', '', '', '', 'Dr. Fayaz Ahmad Paul, Assistant Professor, Department of Social Work, Rajagiri College of Social Sciences Kalamassery, Cochin, Kerala, India. Email ID: paulfayazpaul@gmail.com\n\nDr, Arif Ali, Associate Professor, Department of Psychiatric Social Work, Institute of Human Behaviour and Allied Sciences, New Delhi, India. Email: arifalipsw@gmail.com\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('69', 'ritwik.mund@gmail.com', '71', '4/15/2026 4:29:57 PM +00:00', '4/15/2026 4:29:57 PM +00:00', 'Social Systems and Academic Coping: A Study of Undergraduate Students in the University of Delhi', 'In India, college students experience academic stress due to pressure from examinations, family dynamics and relationships, peer interactions, and the institutional environment. Students encountering such stressors adopt coping strategies, and coping among college students comprises adaptive, problem-focused, emotion-focused, and avoidant strategies. The latter is associated with students with a higher incidence of stress. Currently, the interventions emerging from research studies mostly emphasise biological or medical perspectives.\n\nObjectives: The study seeks to explore the coping strategies of undergraduate students at the North Campus of the University of Delhi in the context of academic stress through their interactions with social systems. The study will engage with students’ lived experiences of academic stress influenced by various ecological levels. \n\nDesign: The study will use a qualitative design based on the Strengths provided in the Values in Action framework. The data will be collected through semi-structured interviews utilising criterion sampling strategies.\n\nModel: The study would be situated within Bronfenbrenner’s Ecological Systems Theory, examining the role of families, communities, peer groups, and policies in contributing to academic stress and students’ coping strategies. \n\nResult: The findings would highlight the influence of ecological factors, from personal and academic spaces, on stressors and coping strategies beyond the individual experiences. Further, the study would likely underline social factors and their effectiveness in impacting coping choices among college students. \n\nConclusion: The paper will engage with the gaps of the biomedical lens and emphasise a socially informed, contextual approach to addressing academic stress and coping strategies in higher education.\n\nKeywords: Social systems; Strengths; Academic Stress; Coping strategy; Higher education', 'Ritwik Mund', 'ritwik.mund@gmail.com', 'Ritwik Mund (Department of Social Work, University of Delhi)*; Sankar Ganesh S (Department of Social Work, University of Delhi)', 'Mund, Ritwik*; Ganesh S, Sankar', 'ritwik.mund@gmail.com*; sankar4302@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract_ Social Systems and Academic Coping_ A Study of Undergraduate Students in Delhi University (1).pdf (83,639 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Anu Mohan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'anumohan@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Sankar Ganesh S, PhD Scholar, Department of Social Work, University of Delhi, sankar4302@gmail.com		', 'Paper Presentation'),
('70', 'daithyasudan30@gmail.com', '72', '4/15/2026 6:04:44 PM +00:00', '4/15/2026 6:04:44 PM +00:00', 'DESIGN AND DEVELOPMENT OF IOT ENABLED PCM BASED SMART PERSONAL COOLING JACKET', 'Increased temperatures lead to reduced human efficiency, health, and endurance. The current paper discusses the development of an intelligent personal cooling jacket incorporating Phase Change Materials (PCMs) along with nano-structured inorganic surface treatment. PCM packages having melting temperature between 18 and 22°С are placed inside the jacket to store excess body heat, thus creating favourable conditions for sustaining optimal microclimate.\nThermal resistance properties were increased with the help of uniform distribution of nano-coating layer on fabric surfaces. In particular, morphological features of the material\'s surface along with dispersed particles increase the stability of the jacket and delay the process of thermal degradation. Nano-coating layer demonstrated strong adhesion with fabric layer without compromising its elasticity as was proved by micro-structural tests.\nIn experiment analysis, the developed jacket showed reduction of skin temperature on average for 3 to 5°C with an extended cooling period ranging from 60 to 90 minutes under increased temperatures.\n', 'Daithyasudan L R', 'daithyasudan30@gmail.com', 'Daithyasudan L R (Thiagarajar College of Engineering)*', 'L R, Daithyasudan*', 'daithyasudan30@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract for the conference.docx (40,754 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Anu Mohan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'anumohan@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'G.R. Aakash, Student (Dept of Mechatronics), Thiagarajar college of Engineering, Madurai and aakashgr@student.tce.edu.\nR. Gopikrishna, Student (Dept of Mechatronics),  Thiagarajar college of Engineering, Madurai and gopikrishna@student.tce.edu.\nDr.G. Kumaraguruparan, Professor and Head (Dept of Mechatronics), Thiagarajar college of Engineering, Madurai and ,  gkgmech@tce.edu.', 'Paper Presentation'),
('71', 'jacinta.chavulak@monash.edu', '73', '4/16/2026 9:54:39 PM +00:00', '4/16/2026 9:54:39 PM +00:00', 'Alternatives to restrictive interventions: the contribution of social work mental health clinicians in crisis settings', 'Emergency and crisis mental health settings are complex environments in which social workers practising as mental health clinicians balance least restrictive care with access to healthcare. Despite being active members of multidisciplinary crisis mental health teams, the experiences of social workers in these settings remain under-documented. Drawing on over 100 years of combined work experience in clinical mental health settings, the social work research team aims to explore how social workers find alternatives to restrictive practices in emergency and crisis mental health care.\nThis qualitative study employed semi-structured reflexive interviews with social workers employed as mental health clinicians in emergency departments and crisis assessment teams across multiple countries. Using a participatory research model, participants were also invited to engage in data analysis, adding an additional layer of reflection and meaning-making informed by clinical expertise.\nPreliminary findings show that emergency psychiatry social work involves high clinical acuity, rapid decision making, and intensive reflexive practice. Social workers use diverse strategies to minimise restrictive interventions, which includes deep collaboration with service users, families, and service systems. Practice in this specialised area is highly complex, with clinicians providing leadership within dominant medical models. Attention to power alongside the thoughtful application of theoretical frameworks is central to this work. Significant challenges were identified, including resource constraints, risk averse organisational cultures, moral distress, and inconsistent recognition of social work expertise in emergency settings.\nThese findings hold important implications for mental health policy, workforce development, and the recognition of social work leadership in mental health care. It demonstrates how clinician-led research and co-analysis ensure practice knowledge is captured and used to inform future research and leadership. The voices of the workforce are integral in providing a safer mental health system for all. \n', 'Jacinta  Chavulak', 'jacinta.chavulak@monash.edu', 'Jacinta  Chavulak (Monash University)*; Terry Smyth (Royal Melbourne Hospital); Philip Mendes (Monash University); Melissa Petrakis (Monash University)', 'Chavulak, Jacinta *; Smyth, Terry; Mendes, Philip; Petrakis, Melissa', 'jacinta.chavulak@monash.edu*; terry.smyth@mh.org.au; philip.mendes@monash.edu; melissa.petrakis@monash.edu', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Workforce Development, Wellbeing, and Leadership', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ICSWHMH 2027 abstract.docx (16,865 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Josephine Nongmaithem (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'josephine@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'As above ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('72', 'arpit.singh.cs28@iilm.edu', '74', '4/17/2026 9:28:45 AM +00:00', '4/17/2026 9:28:45 AM +00:00', 'Password Encryption and authentication System', 'Unauthorized access to user accounts via compromised or inadequately secured credentials persists as a primary threat in contemporary cybersecurity. Traditional password authentication systems employ static hashing without contextual consideration, rendering them susceptible to credential stuffing, brute-force attacks, and replay attacks. This paper introduces A.E.G.I.S (Adaptive Encrypted Guard for Identity and Security), a risk-aware, multi-layer authentication framework integrating bcrypt-based password hashing, device fingerprinting, IP-based risk assessment, Time-based One-Time Password (TOTP) step-up authentication, and a MySQL-backed audit trail. The system employs a trusted-context model inspired by modern identity providers, requiring a second factor only when a login originates from an unrecognized IP address or device. Experimental testing on a synthetic workload of 10,000 login requests demonstrates a 97.3% trusted-context hit rate, a mean lockout activation at 2.8 failed attempts, and 100% audit event capture, confirming that A.E.G.I.S significantly reduces the attack surface for brute-force and credential-stuffing attacks while preserving low latency for returning users.', 'ARPIT SINGH', 'arpit.singh.cs28@iilm.edu', 'ARPIT SINGH (IILM UNIVERSITY)*; Shivam Awasthi (IILM University); PAWNI GUPTA (IILM UNIVERSITY); Akshat Kumar Singh (IILM university); Shivam  Goyal (IILM University); Ankit Raj (IILM University)', 'SINGH, ARPIT*; Awasthi, Shivam; GUPTA, PAWNI; Singh, Akshat Kumar; Goyal, Shivam ; Raj, Ankit', 'arpit.singh.cs28@iilm.edu*; shivam.awasthi@iilm.edu; pawni.gupta.cs28@iilm.edu; akshat.singh.cs28@iilm.edu; shivam.goyal.cs28@illm.edu; ankit.raj.cs28@iilm.edu', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'AEGIS_IEEE_Format.docx (18,863 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Josephine Nongmaithem (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'josephine@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Arpit Singh,Student,IILM University, arpit.singh.cs28@iilm.edu, \nShivam Awasthi,Student,IILM university,shivam.awasthi@iilm.edu,\nAkshat Kumar Singh,Student,IILM University,akshat.singh.cs28@iilm.edu,\nPawni Gupta,Student,IILM University,pawni.gupta.cs28@iilm.edu,\nShivam Goyal,shivam.goyal@illm.edu,\nAnkit Raj,Student,IILM Universitry, ankit.raj.cs28@iilm.edu\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('73', 'michal.kl1504@gmail.com', '75', '4/18/2026 8:44:54 PM +00:00', '4/18/2026 8:44:54 PM +00:00', 'An Ecological Model of Recognition: Diagnosis as a Relational Process Among Survivors of Childhood Sexual Abuse', 'Objectives: This study explores how adult women survivors of childhood sexual abuse (CSA), diagnosed with CPTSD and/or BPD, understand their abuse, interpret psychiatric diagnoses, and link the two within broader processes of recognition and meaning-making.\nDesign: A qualitative study was conducted using Interpretive Description methodology combined with Reflexive Thematic Analysis. Semi-structured interviews were carried out with 19 adult women survivors of CSA (ages 22–55), recruited through social media groups, all holding formal diagnoses of CPTSD and/or BPD.\nModel: We propose a socioecological framework that extends Kelley et al.\'s (2022) model of traumatic stress to the process of recognition, conceptualizing psychiatric diagnosis as a relational and symbolic object negotiated across intrapsychic, familial, and institutional domains.\nResults: Findings revealed an overarching process of making sense of abuse and its consequences, encompassing four interrelated themes: (1) memory, doubt, and silence; (2) internal struggles over whether the abuse was “real”; (3) familial validation and invalidation; and (4) institutional responses that either connected or failed to connect trauma to its consequences. Psychiatric diagnoses functioned as relational frameworks shaping self-perception, stigma, and validation. Trauma-based diagnoses (e.g., CPTSD) often facilitated recognition and coherence, whereas BPD diagnoses were more frequently associated with stigma and misrecognition.\nConclusion: Recognition is a non-linear, dynamic, and context-dependent process. Clinically, the findings highlight the importance of trauma-informed diagnostic communication and systemic practices that integrate trauma into care by “connecting the dots” across services. The study contributes to understanding how complexity and diversity are managed in mental health social work, supporting more relational and context-sensitive approaches.\nKeywords: childhood sexual abuse; CPTSD; BPD; psychiatric diagnosis; recognition; qualitative research', 'Michal Klein', 'michal.kl1504@gmail.com', 'Michal Klein (Tel Hai University of Kiryat Shmona)*', 'Klein, Michal*', 'michal.kl1504@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract_ICSWHMH2027_Final (MK).pdf (185,595 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Danishwar Dar (Rajagri College of Social Sciences)', 'danishwar@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Moshe Farchi\nAssociate Professor\nDepartment of Social Work, Tel-Hai University of Kiryat Shmona and the Galilee, Kiryat Shmona, Israel\nmoshefar@telhai.ac.il\nNoam Meir\nGraduate Student\nDepartment of Social Work, Tel-Hai University of Kiryat Shmona and the Galilee, Kiryat Shmona, Israel\nnoamonet@gmail.com\nMaya Peled Avram\nLecturer\nDepartment of Social Work, Tel-Hai University of Kiryat Shmona and the Galilee, Kiryat Shmona, Israel\nmayaavr@telhai.ac.il', 'Paper Presentation'),
('74', 'madhualathuru@gmail.com', '76', '4/19/2026 2:38:23 AM +00:00', '4/19/2026 2:42:59 AM +00:00', '“Positive Mental Health Outcomes Associated with the COVID-19 Pandemic: A Narrative Review”', 'The COVID-19 pandemic has undoubtedly been a catastrophic event, causing serious adverse effects on a global scale, from socio-economic difficulties to millions of fatalities. The pandemic has significantly impacted mental healthcare provision, introducing both challenges and positive changes. Numerous studies highlighted these challenges, while reports of positive changes were relatively scarce. The primary objective of this narrative review was to explore and emphasise the positive mental health outcomes that arose indirectly from the pandemic. We reviewed 47 research studies published between 2019 and 2023 regarding positive mental health outcomes associated with the COVID-19 pandemic using electronic databases such as PubMed, Scopus, ProQuest, Google Scholar, and web-based news reports. We employed a deductive thematic analysis strategy to extract data from the literature. We organized the study\'s findings into broader themes: mental health awareness and help-seeking behavior, mental wellbeing, reductions in tobacco use, strengthened social relationships, and acceleration in digital mental health. The objective of emphasising the positive aspects of the pandemic is not to minimise the detrimental consequences. Instead, highlight the opportunities and lessons this pandemic has brought. By doing so, we can improve the future of mental health.', 'Madhu A S', 'madhualathuru@gmail.com', 'Madhu A S (Government First Grade College, Vamadapadavu)*', 'A S, Madhu*', 'madhualathuru@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Narrative review Manuscript.docx (127,254 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Josephine Nongmaithem (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'josephine@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Kalyanasundaram Janaki Raman (Corresponding author)\nAdditional Professor, Department of Psychiatric Social Work, National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences, Bengaluru, Karnataka, India\nEmail ID: janakitrichy@gmail.com \nORCID:  0000-0002-9614-5858\n\nThennarasu Kandavel\nProfessor, Department of Biostatistics, National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences, Bengaluru, Karnataka, India\nEmail ID: kthenna@gmail.com\nORCID:  0000-0001-8797-8834\n\nAjit Bhalchandra Dahale\nProfessor, Department of Psychiatry, National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences, Bengaluru, Karnataka, India\nEmail ID: ajitdahale@gmail.com \nORCID: 0000-0002-9833-2542 ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('75', 'ks5543@srmist.edu.in', '78', '4/20/2026 5:37:53 AM +00:00', '4/20/2026 5:37:53 AM +00:00', 'Proactive Driver Drowsiness Detection Using 3D CNN   and Adaptive Alerting ', 'Drowsiness among drivers is one of the leading causes of road accidents especially in long distance driving and during the night where fatigue slowly diminishes alertness and reaction time. The majority of current driver monitoring systems are based on frame-based processing and fixed threshold detection, which prove to be ineffective in terms of capturing the gradual nature of drowsiness and tend to produce slow or unreliable notifications.\nThe current paper describes a Proactive Driver Drowsiness Detection System with 3D Convolutional Neural Networks (3D CNN) and Adaptive Alerting that is able to treat fatigue as a time-varying and constantly changing phenomenon. The proposed system examines sets of driver facial images as a way of learning spatio-temporal patterns including duration of eye closure, blinking behavior and head movements. A continuous risk score of drowsiness, R(t), is approximated to be the level of fatigue of the driver at any given point in time, and thus, the rising trends in drowsiness can be detected early.In order to enhance strength, a temporal attention system is included to pay attention to the most informative frames in each sequence to minimize the influence of noise and irrelevant variations. Depending on the perceived level of risk, an adaptive alerting system is used with progressive warnings that will progressively escalate to more intense audio or voice warning with an increase in fatigue.\nThe results of the experiments prove that the suggested system has the accuracy level of about 85% and can offer a more stable detection and earlier warnings than the traditional methods. Its lightweight and real-time nature makes it appropriate to be used in real-life implementation in intelligent transportation systems and advanced driver assistance systems.\n', 'Kapilan S', 'ks5543@srmist.edu.in', 'Kapilan S (SRM University)*', 'S, Kapilan*', 'ks5543@srmist.edu.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Final ieee driver - Copy.pdf (445,114 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Martina Rajan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'martina@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Kapilan.S, Student, SRM University , ks5543@srmist.edu.in\nPraneeswaran.S, Student, SRM University, ps7173@srmist.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('76', 'aniketjha0013@gmail.com', '79', '4/22/2026 8:12:15 AM +00:00', '4/22/2026 8:12:15 AM +00:00', 'AI-Powered Mental Wellness Assistant for Early Intervention using Hybrid Semantic Retrieval and Safety-Aware Response Generation', 'Stress, anxiety, and loneliness are on the rise, but\ntimely access to mental health care is limited due to social stigma,\nexcessive costs of treatment, and the lack of qualified specialists.\nArtificial intelligence can provide a scalable, accessible avenue\nof mental wellness intervention at an early stage. The paper\nintroduces a more advanced mental wellness chatbot that builds\non classical TF-IDF and supervised classification to add sentence\ntransformer embeddings to support deep semantic retrieval, a\nhybrid multi-stage filtering and safety layer to ensure quality of\nresponses, and a context-awareness module to monitor emotional\npatterns throughout a conversation. It is based on a Python\nFlask client-server architecture and HTML/CSS/JS frontend. The\nsystem is assessed based on 38 assessed test cases rated by human\nassessors on three dimensions (relevance, empathy, and safety) on\na 1–5 Likert scale. The proposed system has mean scores of 3.6,\n3.7, and 4.7 respectively, which are improvements of +0.9, +1.3,\nand +0.6 over a TF-IDF baseline retrieval model. Findings affirm\nthat hybrid semantic retrieval with safety-conscious generation\nsignificantly enhances the quality and consistency of responses, as\nwell as their suitability in the application of early mental health\nsupport.', 'Aniket Jha', 'aniketjha0013@gmail.com', 'Aniket Jha (Galgotias University)*; Ravi Sajwan (Galgotias University)', 'Jha, Aniket*; Sajwan, Ravi', 'aniketjha0013@gmail.com*; ravisaj28092003@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'paper.pdf (192,068 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Danishwar Dar (Rajagri College of Social Sciences)', 'danishwar@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Aniket Jha\nUndergraduate Student\nDepartment of Computer Science\nGalgotias University, India\nEmail: aniketjha0013@gmail.com,\nRavi Sajwan\nUndergraduate Student\nDepartment of Computer Science\nGalgotias University, India\nEmail: ravisaj28092003@gmail.com,\nDr. S.Karpaga Selvi\nProfessor\nDepartment of Computer Science\nGalgotias University, India\nEmail: karpagaselvi.s@galgotiasuniversity.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('77', 'srinivasa.msw283@gmail.com', '80', '4/23/2026 10:53:25 AM +00:00', '4/23/2026 10:53:25 AM +00:00', 'BRIDGING EVIDENCE AND LIVED REALITIES: SOCIAL WORK INTERVENTIONS FOR DALIT WOMEN’S HEALTH IN KALABURAGI REGION', 'This study examines the intersection of structural inequality, caste-based marginalization, and gendered health disparities among Dalit women. Drawing on primary data collected from 130 respondents across rural and peri-urban communities, the research situates lived experiences within broader public health and social justice frameworks. The findings reveal persistent barriers to healthcare access, including economic precarity, discrimination within medical institutions, limited health literacy, and inadequate state outreach. The study critically engages with the gap between policy-driven evidence and everyday health realities, demonstrating that standardized health interventions often fail to account for caste-based vulnerabilities and localized socio-cultural dynamics. Using a mixed-methods approach, the research highlights how social determinants, such as sanitation, nutrition, reproductive autonomy, and mobility, shape health outcomes in complex ways. Importantly, the paper foregrounds the role of social work as a transformative practice. It argues for context-sensitive, community-driven interventions that integrate advocacy, participatory health education, and institutional accountability. Models of intervention discussed include grassroots mobilization, culturally responsive health communication, and collaborative engagement with public health systems. The study concludes that bridging the divide between empirical evidence and lived realities requires a reorientation of social work practice toward intersectionality, rights-based approaches, and sustained engagement with marginalized communities. Such efforts are essential for achieving equitable health outcomes among Dalit women in the region.', 'Dr.Srinivasa D', 'srinivasa.msw283@gmail.com', 'Dr.Srinivasa D (Central University of Karnataka)*', 'D, Dr.Srinivasa*', 'srinivasa.msw283@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'BRIDGING EVIDENCE AND LIVED REALITIES- Kerala International Conference.docx (13,284 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Martina Rajan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'martina@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Pramita Nayak\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('78', 'cchen@g2.usc.edu.tw', '81', '4/25/2026 7:55:48 AM +00:00', '4/25/2026 8:15:33 AM +00:00', 'Enhancing Assessment Competence in Medical Social Work: An Action Research on Curriculum Innovation in Health Education', '(Objectives) This study examines the effectiveness of curriculum innovation in enhancing assessment competence among medical social work students, focusing on needs assessment, goal setting, and assessment reasoning. Medical social workers are required to address the complex health needs of patients and their families by conducting comprehensive assessments based on multiple sources of information, while also facilitating healthcare teams’ understanding and responses to service users. Accordingly, this study further investigates how interdisciplinary and collaborative teaching approaches support students in integrating diverse information, identifying client problems, and formulating appropriate intervention goals in practice contexts.\n(Design) This study adopts an action research approach involving 60 third-year students enrolled in a “Medical Social Work” course. A cyclical process of instructional design, implementation, and refinement was conducted. Data were collected through reflective worksheets, assessment forms, and humanoid diagrams, and analyzed using triangulation to examine students’ development in assessment-related competencies.\n(Model) The instructional model includes curriculum restructuring, interdisciplinary collaboration, teacher–student co-creation, case-based practice, optimization of teaching materials and assessment tools, revised evaluation methods, and collaboration with field practitioners to strengthen contextual learning and professional integration.\n(Result) Findings indicate that interdisciplinary and collaborative teaching enhances students’ ability to integrate information, improve needs assessment and goal-setting skills, and develop coherent assessment logic and intervention plans.\n(Conclusion) This study proposes a feasible model for improving assessment education in medical social work, demonstrating that interdisciplinary and collaborative pedagogies facilitate competency integration and support the transfer of learning into practice.\nKeywords: Medical Social Work; Assessment Competence; Action Research; Interdisciplinary Learning; Teacher–Student Co-creation', 'Chun-Yi Chen', 'cchen@g2.usc.edu.tw', 'Chun-Yi Chen (SHIH CHIEN)*', 'Chen, Chun-Yi*', 'cchen@g2.usc.edu.tw*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Enhancing Assessment Competence in Medical Social Work_ An Action Research on Curriculum Innovation in Health Education.pdf (74,961 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Hui-i Yang, Licensed Social Worker\nDepartment of Social Work, Taipei Tzu Chi Hospital,\nBuddhist Tzu Chi Medical Foundation, Taiwan\nhuiiyang627@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('79', 'sarvikae1@gmail.com', '82', '4/26/2026 12:14:55 PM +00:00', '4/26/2026 12:14:55 PM +00:00', 'Enhancing Smart Agriculture with 5G and Explainable AI', 'A hybrid smart agriculture crop yield forecasting model is proposed by combining feature analysis and classification methods. We use the XLNet deep learning model, based on the transformer architecture, to gather important context information from high-dimensional agricultural data related to soil, weather, and crop factors. To boost performance, we fine-tune a subset of features using an improved barnacle mating optimization algorithm to find the best one. The resulting feature vectors are then fed into a SVM, which analyzes the extracted features to determine the best separation hyperplane.. Performance comparison with Random Forest and hybrid XLNet-Random Forest models shows that the proposed XLNet+SVM framework achieves better precision and less error rates. In addition, interpretable AI techniques e.g., SHAP, are used to both enhance transparency by recognising the most important environmental and soil input features influencing predictions. The model provides a trade-off between the prediction accuracy and interpretation, which is adequate for sustainable precision agriculture', 'Sarvika E', 'sarvikae1@gmail.com', 'Sarvika E (Velalar college of Engineering and Technology)*; Malar P (Velalar College of Engineering and Technology); Thanigaiyarasan I (Velalar College of Engineering and Technology); Myvizhi V (Velalar College of Engineering and Technology); Sangameshwari M (Velalar College of Engineering and Technology)', 'E, Sarvika*; P, Malar; I, Thanigaiyarasan; V, Myvizhi; M, Sangameshwari', 'sarvikae1@gmail.com*; malar.prof24@gmail.com; thanigai353@gmail.com; myvizhivijayavelu06@gmail.com; msangameshwari57@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Smart agri paper1-plag checked (1).docx (167,336 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Martina Rajan (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'martina@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', '\nThanigaiyarasan I – Student – Velalar College of Engineering and Technology – thanigai353@gmail.com\nMyvizhi V – Student – Velalar College of Engineering and Technology – myvizhivijayavelu06@gmail.com\nSangameshwari M – Student – Velalar College of Engineering and Technology – msangameshwari57@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('80', 'prachi.mittal@outlook.com', '83', '4/26/2026 1:24:45 PM +00:00', '4/26/2026 1:24:45 PM +00:00', 'Inclusiveness Rating for Indian One-Stop Centres: A UNFPA Idea for addressing Gender-based Violence', 'In Dec 2022, United Nations Populations Fund (UNFPA) organized an idea contest on the topic of “preventing and addressing gender-based violence”. The winning entry was “Inclusiveness Rating”, a mathematical index that could be used to make India’s One-Stop Centres (OSCs) more inclusive for persons with disabilities who are disproportionately a more frequent target of gender-based violence. In this paper, an overview of the inclusiveness rating idea submission is presented.\nGender-based violence (GBV) is a big problem in many countries, including India. And while people of all genders fall victim to it, the female and transgender population is particularly vulnerable. Furthermore, persons with disabilities (PWDs), physical and psychological, are even more prone to GBV because the society i.e. the people usually discriminate against them and social institutes are generally not designed in a very inclusive manner. \nOSCs in India, also nicknamed Sakhi centres (“Companion”), aim to provide various kinds of help to the victims of GBV, e.g. a shelter, medical support, legal counselling, psychological counselling etc. But like other social institutes, they also fall short on inclusiveness of their design, including the infrastructure, personnel and the services offered.\nThe idea of inclusiveness rating (IR) tries to address the above problem by assessing the different aspects of OSC design from an inclusiveness point of view. As such, the IR concept can be applied to OSCs for inclusion of any group of people. But in this paper, only the assessment from the point of view of PWDs is presented. First the general concept of IR is presented and then its specific application to Indian OSCs and their inclusiveness for PWDs.', 'Prachi Mittal', 'prachi.mittal@outlook.com', 'Prachi Mittal (Independent Researcher)*', 'Mittal, Prachi*', 'prachi.mittal@outlook.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Inclusiveness Index for Indian One-Stop Centres.docx (534,812 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Haripriya  Venugopal (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'haripriya@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'None', 'Paper Presentation'),
('81', '23211a66j4@bvrit.ac.in', '84', '4/26/2026 3:44:26 PM +00:00', '4/26/2026 3:44:26 PM +00:00', 'Mental Health Distress and Emotion Drift Analysis Using Transformers', '. Social media platforms have become vital sources for understanding mental health concerns, since users often share experiences\nThese are experiences related to stress, anxiety, and emotional well-being.\nThis paper proposes a transformer-based approach for finding mental\nhealth dis- tress and analyzing emotional changes over time using largescale Red- dit data. First, a labeled mental health dataset is used to train\na DistilBERT-based classification to identify different categories of Mental health distress includes substance-related issues, early life fac- Tors,\nand psychological stressors. After training, this model is then applied\nto multi-year, unlabeled Reddit posts. to perform large-scale distress\nprediction. Further, an emotion classi- fication model based on transformer architecture is used to extract the dominant emotion expressed\nin each post. Aggregating these emotions across While the proposed system makes predictions across months and years, temporal emotion drift\nanalysis, highlighting long-term emotional pat- Terns associated with discussions about mental health. Results show that the transformer model\nis effective in processing informal and noisy social media text while capturing both distress cate- gories, and emotional trends. This framework\nprovides a scalable solution for for mental health analysis and can support future research in population- Level mental health monitoring and\nearly identification of emotional dis- tress.\n', 'VIVEKSAI CHEELA', '23211a66j4@bvrit.ac.in', 'VIVEKSAI CHEELA (bvrit)*; suddala rahul (bvrit); vishnu  thammineni (bvrit); lavanya b (bvrit); mounika m (bvrit)', 'CHEELA, VIVEKSAI*; rahul, suddala; thammineni, vishnu ; b, lavanya; m, mounika', '23211a66j4@bvrit.ac.in*; 23211a66g3@bvrit.ac.in; 23211a66j3@bvrit.ac.in; lavanya.b@bvrit.ac.in; mounika.m@bvrit.ac.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'final_conference_paper_with_appendix.pdf (420,251 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Haripriya  Venugopal (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'haripriya@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'lavanya b,assistant professor,lavanya.b@bvrit.ac.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('82', 'ADT23SOCB1558@students.mituniversity.edu.in', '85', '4/27/2026 6:01:09 AM +00:00', '4/27/2026 6:01:09 AM +00:00', 'HealHive: A Secure Anonymous AI-Driven Mental Health Support Platform', 'The occurrence of mental disorders is on the rise in India; however, a significant portion of the population does not approach any professionals owing to factors like stigma, confidentiality issues, affordability concerns, and unavailability of trustworthy therapists. Although several online platforms have come into existence for dealing with such challenges, they fail to ensure anonymity, intelligent therapist matchmaking, and safety precautions.\n\nThis study suggests HealHive, an AI-driven, anonymous, and secure online platform aimed at helping users deal with their mental issues using advanced technology. The system uses NLP for the evaluation of the user\'s responses, classifies the severity level of their mental disorder, and recommends further courses of action. In case of low-risk patients, the AI takes charge, while high-risk patients are handed over to verified therapists via intelligent matchmaking services.\n\nAnonymity and security are two of the key concerns for users of online systems, and therefore, HealHive uses anonymized verification, encryption of communications, and intelligent moderation based on AI for preventing any unsafe activities. Through comparative analysis with other systems, the advantages of the model under consideration become apparent.', 'Satya Barik', 'ADT23SOCB1558@students.mituniversity.edu.in', 'Satya Barik (MIT ADT University)*', 'Barik, Satya*', 'ADT23SOCB1558@students.mituniversity.edu.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'HealHive_Research_Paper 10.56.57.pdf (888,009 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Haripriya  Venugopal (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'haripriya@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Sachin Hiranwale\nAsst. Professor\nDepartment of Information Technology\nMIT School of Computing, MIT Art Design and Technology University\nPune, India\nsachin.hiranwale@mituniversity.edu.in. \n\nSwaraj Shelar\nStudent\nDepartment of Information Technology\nMIT School of Computing, MIT Art Design and Technology University\nPune, India\nADT23SOCB1616@mituniversity.edu.in\n\nSuchit Parte\nStudent\nDepartment of Information Technology\nMIT School of Computing, MIT Art Design and Technology University\nPune, India\nADT23SOCB1564@mituniversity.edu.in \n\nVansh Asnani  \nStudent                        \nDepartment of Information Technology\nMIT School of Computing, MIT Art Design and Technology University\nPune, India\nADT23SOCB1573@mituniversity.edu.in ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('83', 'nidhilal@hotmail.com', '86', '4/27/2026 4:00:42 PM +00:00', '4/27/2026 4:02:52 PM +00:00', 'Innovative Integrative Medicine Techniques to treat Patients', 'Innovative Integrative Medicine Techniques to treat Patients\nSharing stories can be powerful. Narratives provide expression and better understanding of who we are. As humans, we relate to stories more than facts or numbers. As physicians, we have our patient anecdotes, which, when shared, tell our listeners about our interactions and allow learners to understand, interpret and empathize. As we introspect and think of various perspectives, we reflect on decisions made, those that were not made, and understand lessons learned. Our experiences teach us not to repeat unintentional mistakes; if captured and shared, they can also teach others not to make them at all. Shared and hearing stories will allow our audience to look towards the future with greater empathy, support, and resiliency.\nThrough our discussion we use Narratives, Art and Music as forms of expression and storytelling. \n\nThese benefits are for both the story teller and the listeners, which subsequently benefits patients, current and future.\nThe goals for learners and listeners are to: \n1.	develop more informed and culturally sensitive interprofessional providers;\n2.	share a provider perspective that may not be heard otherwise and may not be same as the patient perspective;\n3.	improve patient care by increasing listeners reflective skills;\n4.	enable learners to understand, interpret, and empathize with human actions; and\n5.	revisit challenging and educational patient experiences and understand lessons learned along the way, thereby increasing resiliencies and well-being.\n', 'Nidhi Lal', 'nidhilal@hotmail.com', 'Nidhi Lal (Boston Medical Center)*', 'Lal, Nidhi*', 'nidhilal@hotmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'narrative workshop .docx (18,192 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Haripriya  Venugopal (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'haripriya@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'none', 'Paper Presentation'),
('84', 'krishandevojha44@gmail.com', '87', '4/29/2026 11:29:01 AM +00:00', '4/29/2026 11:29:01 AM +00:00', 'An NLP-Based Machine Learning Framework for Strong Spam Email Classification: A Comparative Study of Classical and Deep Learning Methods', 'The number of spam emails has been increasing \nexponen ally and con nues to be a huge cybersecurity risk, resul ng \nin lost money, privacy, and produc vity in the en re global \ncommunica on systems. Old-fashioned rule based filters have proven \nto be very weak in dealing with more advanced spam campaigns \nwhich use obfusca on techniques, social engineering and adap ve \ncontent crea on. The paper introduces a complete framework of \nnatural language processing and machine learning to classify spam \nemails automa cally, which is essen al in the crea on of adap ve \ncontent-based spam detec on systems. The proposed methodology \ninvolves a mul-stage preprocessing pipeline, which involves \ntokeniza on, stop-word removal, stemming and TF-IDF vectoriza on \nusing n-gram analysis to iden fy meaningful seman c and syntac c \nfeatures in raw email text. There is a compara ve analysis of five \nclassifica on algorithms, Mul nomial Naive Bayes, Support Vector \nMachine with Radial Basis Func on kernel, Random Forest, Long \nShortTerm Memory networks, and Bidirec onal Long Short-Term \nMemory networks. Benchmark Enron-Spam dataset of 10,000 \nbalanced email samples are experimented. Empirical evidence \nindicates that the Random Forest classifier using TF-IDF features has a \nbe er performance of 98.7 percent accuracy, 0.98 precision, 0.99 \nrecall, and a false posi ve rate of 0.01, which is be er than deep \nlearning methods when working with rela vely small data sets. The \nproposed system is able to run at 50 milliseconds per email on average \ninference me and can be deployed in real me. The study provides a \nrepeatable evalua on system, the most effec ve feature-algorithm \ncombina ons to detect spam, and a benchmark to conduct future \nstudies on adversarial robustness in the face of genera ve AI-based \nspam.', 'Krishan Ojha', 'krishandevojha44@gmail.com', 'Krishan Ojha (Chandigarh University)*', 'Ojha, Krishan*', 'krishandevojha44@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Krishan_Research_Paper.pdf (907,389 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Haripriya  Venugopal (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'haripriya@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Sudhanshu Ranjan \nSudhansu3710@gmail.com \nDept. of CSE \nChandigarh University \nMohali, Punjab , India ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('85', 'vkpatil8810@gmail.com', '88', '4/29/2026 7:18:41 PM +00:00', '4/29/2026 7:28:46 PM +00:00', 'Smart Healthcare System Using QR with UID and Role-Based Access', ' It is quite important to consider the benefits\nassociated with the use of smart cards in cases where\nunexpected events happen, and the type of medical\nattention is determined by the experts. One of the most\nsignificant problems that developing countries face today\nis health care, which calls for the implementation of IT\nsolutions to overcome existing barriers. It can also be used\nfor keeping an integrated health record and simplifying the\nmedication process, as well as improving the efficiency of\nservices delivery. The proposed Care Card solution\nprovides for assigning a unique identifier (UID) to patients\nregistered with the system. In other words, UID becomes\nthe primary key for accessing patients\' medical records.\nData security measures include providing for encryption,\nauthentication, and authorization. The first two measures\naim at ensuring data protection, whereas the latter one\nhelps to prevent unauthorized access to confidential\ninformation by different users. The system can\naccommodate several users, including patients, physicians,\npharmacists, and hospital staff members who will use\ncustomized interfaces. QR code will be incorporated into\nthe application to provide for quick identification of\npatients and their data in case of emergency situations.', 'VIVEK PATIL', 'vkpatil8810@gmail.com', 'VIVEK PATIL (VIIT,Pune)*', 'PATIL, VIVEK*', 'vkpatil8810@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Professional Practice, Interventions, and Models of Care', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'carecard_research.pdf (434,495 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Haripriya  Venugopal (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'haripriya@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Aditya Admane\naditya.22320001@viit.ac.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('86', 'kajalsingh1620@gmail.com', '89', '4/30/2026 6:11:26 AM +00:00', '4/30/2026 6:14:22 AM +00:00', 'ACADEMIC PRESSURE AND COPING STRATEGIES AMONG ADOLESCENTS: A STUDY IN BAGHPAT DISTRICT, UP', 'Adolescence is a key developmental stage during which individuals form a lens through which their understanding of themselves and their environment builds. Adolescents mental health during this period significantly influences their well-being in later life. However, most of the existing research has mainly been conducted in Western countries or in large urban settings. There is comparatively limited research that focuses on adolescents living in rural and semi-urban areas of North India. \nThe present study focuses on studying concerns of the students in Classes IX–XII in selected schools of Baghpat district of Uttar Pradesh. The study aimed to understand their experiences of academic pressure and the coping methods. It aims to identify the factors contributing to stress among the adolescents, analyse the disturbances brought by stress in daily life, and explore the coping strategies of adolescents to deal with stress. \nDescriptive cross sectional research design was adopted. Data was collected through a questionnaire having both close-ended and open-ended questions. The sample size is of 100 students who are studying in class IX-XII from rural and semi-urban academic institution in Baghpat district, UP.\nThe findings of the study shows that many students experience various types of stress related to their studies, school environment, and the expectations placed on them by their parents and teachers. It was also found that many students know how to cope with their stress. Although some students adopt active coping and some respond with anger.\nHence, the study highlights the need for supportive educational environment and effective mental health interventions by understanding the sources of stress among adolescents. the findings of this study can be used by teachers, school administrators, parents, and social workers for better academic performance and overall well-being of adolescents by promoting healthy coping strategies.\n\nKeywords- adolescents, coping strategies, academic pressure, stress.\n', 'Kajal  Singh', 'kajalsingh1620@gmail.com', 'Kajal  Singh (University of delhi)*', 'Singh, Kajal *', 'kajalsingh1620@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'abstract.docx (20,040 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Haripriya  Venugopal (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'haripriya@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Primary author- Kajal Singh, Phd scholar, Delhi school of social work, university of Delhi, University Road, Delhi', 'Paper Presentation'),
('87', 'imraghu0805@gmail.com', '90', '4/30/2026 2:34:06 PM +00:00', '4/30/2026 2:38:11 PM +00:00', 'AQI Guardian: A Personalized Mobile Health Application for Real-Time Air Quality Prediction and Automated Guardian Alert System', 'Air pollution is a global health crisis responsible for approximately 8.1 million deaths annually (State of Global Air, 2024). Individuals suffering from respiratory conditions such as asthma, Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD), and cardiovascular disorders are disproportionately vulnerable to hazardous air quality. Despite the availability of general-purpose AQI monitoring applications, there exists a\nsignificant gap in personalized, location-specific air quality prediction systems that proactively alert the guardians or caregivers of at-risk\npatients before exposure occurs. This paper presents AQI Guardian, a cross-platform mobile health (mHealth) application built using React\nNative and Expo, designed to address this gap. The system performs: (1) real-time AQI monitoring using GPS-based coordinate-level data from\nthe OpenWeatherMap Air Pollution API, (2) next-hour AQI prediction using a Moving Average algorithm on a locally persisted time-series window, (3) AQI-aware safe route planning with micro-local variation modeling along travel paths, and (4) automated SMS-based emergency\nalerts to registered guardians when air quality reaches hazardous thresholds. The novel contribution lies in the integration of predictive analytics with a guardian notification protocol, transforming a passive monitoring tool into an active health protection system for respiratory-sensitive populations.', 'Raghu R', 'imraghu0805@gmail.com', 'Raghu R (Panimalar Engineering College)*; Arun  A (Panimalar Engineering College); Punithan J (	Panimalar Engineering College); Meda Eswar (Panimalar Engineering College)', 'R, Raghu*; A, Arun ; J, Punithan; Eswar, Meda', 'imraghu0805@gmail.com*; drarun.srm@gmail.com; punithan06232005@gmail.com; meshwar220@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'AQI_Guardian_Conference_Paper .pdf (196,993 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Haripriya  Venugopal (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous), Kalamassery)', 'haripriya@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Mr. A. Arun M.E., (Ph.D), Panimalar Engineering College\nRaghu R, Panimalar Engineering College\nPunithan J, Panimalar Engineering College\nMeda Eswar, Panimalar Engineering College', 'Paper Presentation'),
('88', 'anuragkumar0824@gmail.com', '91', '5/1/2026 11:25:40 AM +00:00', '5/1/2026 11:25:40 AM +00:00', 'Conversational AI for Detecting and Supporting Individuals with Mental Health Issues Using Hybrid AI and Tiered Clinical Safety Guardrails', 'Abstract—Conversational AI can be a way to provide mental\nhealth services when they are not easily available or take too\nlong to access like when the internet is slow or a lot of data is\nneeded to train models.Recently natural language processing has.\nMade conversational AI systems faster but these systems still have\nproblems like overestimating how well they can analyze feelings\nand not being good at finding risks.\nThe system we are proposing which is a mix of approaches\nwill help counsel and diagnose users based on their mental health\nrisk level.Our system can analyze emotions by looking at what\npeople say and using machine learning. It can also detect crises\nby looking at the conversation history.We break down the risk\nanalysis into three parts: risk, medium risk and high risk.Our\nearly results show that our mixed approach works better than\nthe system especially in different risk situations and it shows that\nwe need a conversational AI system for mental health that can\nexplain itself.\nThe mental health services can be delivered through AI when\nmental health services are not readily available.Our hybrid\napproach will help users according to their health risk level.The\nsystem performs emotion analysis through mental health sen-\ntiment analysis. It uses a rule-based crisis detector.The risk\nanalysis is broken down into three stages for health: low risk,\nmedium risk and high risk.Our preliminary results show that the\nhybrid approach is better than the baseline system for health risk\nscenarios.The conversational AI system for health is a good way\nto provide mental health services.\nWe need an AI system, for mental health that can explain\nitself like our hybrid approach.\nIndex Terms—Chatbots (conversational AI), Mental Health,\nRisk Triage, Sentiment Analysis, Safety Guardrails, CBT, Ex-\nplainabilit', 'Anurag kumar', 'anuragkumar0824@gmail.com', 'Anurag kumar (kiet group of institutions ghaziabad)*', 'kumar, Anurag*', 'anuragkumar0824@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'demy_research_paper (14).pdf (407,923 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'SHYBA BABU (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'shybababu@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'aryan ,ashish,aryak', 'Paper Presentation'),
('89', 'saibavith07@gmail.com', '92', '5/4/2026 9:29:33 AM +00:00', '5/4/2026 9:29:33 AM +00:00', 'NUTRA: ADAPTIVE AI-DRIVEN PERSONALIZED NUTRITION MONITORING WITH DYNAMIC METABOLIC THRESHOLD CALIBRATION', 'Personalized nutrition is a critical component of preventive healthcare, yet conventional dietary recommendation systems often rely on static guidelines that fail to adapt to individual metabolic variability and lifestyle dynamics. This paper presents NUTRA, an adaptive AI-driven framework for personalized nutrition monitoring that integrates real-time physiological data with intelligent analytics to deliver context-aware dietary recommendations. The proposed system employs machine learning models to analyze user-specific parameters such as activity levels, dietary intake, biometric signals, and historical health patterns. A key contribution of this work is the introduction of a dynamic metabolic threshold calibration mechanism, which continuously adjusts nutritional baselines based on evolving metabolic responses, thereby improving recommendation accuracy over time. The architecture combines wearable sensor integration, cloud-based data processing, and a feedback-driven optimization loop to ensure scalability and adaptability. Experimental evaluation using simulated and benchmark health datasets demonstrates improved precision in nutrient recommendation and enhanced responsiveness to metabolic changes compared to static rule-based systems. The proposed approach highlights the potential of adaptive AI techniques in advancing personalized healthcare and promoting long-term wellness through data-driven nutrition management.', 'S Sai BavithTej', 'saibavith07@gmail.com', 'S Sai BavithTej (SRM Institute of Science & Technology, Tiruchirapalli Campus)*', 'Sai BavithTej, S*', 'saibavith07@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'IEEE research paper .pdf (825,090 bytes); IEEE_Conference_Template__2_ (2).pdf (935,269 bytes)', '2', '', '0', 'SHYBA BABU (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences)', 'shybababu@rajagiri.edu', '', '', '', '', 'Name :Yaswanth kumar S, email:ys4790@srmist.edu.in\nName:. Shashank Reddy.A , email:ar4647@srmist.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('90', 'sachinshamvab@gmail.com', '93', '5/7/2026 10:41:29 AM +00:00', '5/7/2026 10:43:18 AM +00:00', 'Social sufferings in Russia and Ukraine warfare: A necropolitical analysis of Nepali citizens exposed in Sordid Trafficking.   ', 'Although Nepal has officially remained uninvolved in the Russia–Ukraine war, economic hardship and unemployment have compelled many Nepali citizens to migrate through informal international routes, often facilitated by human traffickers, ultimately placing them in active war zones. This phenomenon has produced complex social, legal, and emotional realities, where Nepali citizens continue to lose their lives under precarious and poorly documented circumstances. Families are frequently left without reliable information regarding the status of their loved ones, creating prolonged uncertainty and psychological distress. Returnees report inadequate medical care for the wounded, while families of the deceased face significant legal and bureaucratic barriers in repatriating bodies and claiming compensation. From a mental health perspective, these conditions generate layered forms of suffering, including ambiguous loss, prolonged grief disorder, trauma, anxiety, and depression among affected families and survivors. The absence of confirmed death, the dispersal or mishandling of bodies, and the lack of culturally appropriate funeral rites further intensify emotional trauma and disrupt traditional mourning practices. Such experiences not only individualize distress but also produce collective psychosocial consequences within communities. While International Humanitarian Law (IHL) provides a framework for the respectful treatment and identification of the dead, its practical limitations in this context reveal significant gaps. Drawing on critical policy discourse analysis (CPA) and humanitarian forensics, this paper examines these structural and ethical shortcomings, highlighting the urgent need for integrated legal and mental health frameworks. It advocates for policies that address not only material and legal restitution but also psychosocial support systems to mitigate the enduring mental health burdens faced by Nepali families affected by this transnational conflict.', 'Sachn  Ghimire', 'sachinshamvab@gmail.com', 'Sachn  Ghimire (Tribhuban University )*', 'Ghimire, Sachn *', 'sachinshamvab@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Russia and Ukraine_ mental health _Sachin Ghimire_Kerala.docx (15,049 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Shinto Joseph (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shintot@gmail.com', '', '', '', '', 'Single Author', 'Paper Presentation'),
('91', 'tonshen.chuang@gmail.com', '94', '5/8/2026 6:19:38 AM +00:00', '5/8/2026 7:02:49 AM +00:00', 'Defining Supervisory Competencies in Medical Social Work: A Consensus-Based Framework for Workforce Development in Taiwan.', 'Objectives: This study aims to identify the essential professional competencies required of medical social work supervisors in hospital settings in Taiwan. Given the limited availability of structured supervisory training and evidence-based competency frameworks, the study further seeks to develop a contextualized, consensus-based framework to inform supervisory training, workforce development, and quality improvement in hospital-based social work services.\nDesign: A four-stage mixed-methods design was employed to develop and validate the supervisory competency framework. The study integrated qualitative exploration, expert consensus, contextual refinement, and quantitative assessment to ensure that the resulting framework reflected both professional standards and local hospital-based practice realities.\nModel: The model was developed through four sequential stages: first, semi-structured interviews using the Critical Incident Technique were conducted to identify foundational supervisory competencies; second, a Delphi survey with academic and practice experts was used to establish consensus on the clarity and importance of competency indicators; third, focus groups with senior practitioners were conducted to refine and contextualize the indicators for hospital-based practice; and fourth, a nationwide quantitative survey of hospital social work supervisors was conducted to examine the perceived importance and frequency of each competency.\nResult: The study identified four major domains of supervisory responsibility in hospital social work and eight key task objectives. For each task objective, specific behavioral indicators were delineated, along with the corresponding knowledge, skills, and professional attitudes required for effective supervision. The resulting framework reflects both expert consensus and the practical demands of hospital-based social work supervision in Taiwan.\nConclusion: This study develops a consensus-based and contextually grounded competency framework for medical social work supervision in Taiwan. By clarifying the core competencies required of hospital social work supervisors, the framework provides a practical foundation for supervisory training, workforce development, and service quality improvement in hospital-based social work.', 'Tung-Hsien Chuang', 'tonshen.chuang@gmail.com', 'Jen-Chia Chang (Graduate Institute of Technological and Vocational Education, National Taipei University of Technology); Tung-Hsien Chuang (Medical Social Work Association. Taiwan (R.O.C.) / Graduate Institute of Technological and Vocational Education, National Taipei University of Technology)*', 'Chang, Jen-Chia; Chuang, Tung-Hsien*', 'jc5839@ntut.edu.tw; tonshen.chuang@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Workforce Development, Wellbeing, and Leadership', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Defining Supervisory Competencies in Medical Social Work A Consensus-Based Framework for Workforce Development in Taiwan.docx (17,831 bytes); Defining Supervisory Competencies in Medical Social Work A Consensus-Based Framework for Workforce Development in', '2', '', '0', 'Shinto Joseph (Rajagiri College of Social Sciences (Autonomous))', 'shintot@gmail.com', '', '', '', '', 'Jen-Chia Chang \nProfessor, Graduate Institute of Technological and Vocational Education, National Taipei University of Technology, Taiwan.\nEmail: jc5839@ntut.edu.tw', 'Paper Presentation'),
('93', 'shir.dt@gmail.com', '96', '5/8/2026 10:15:33 AM +00:00', '5/8/2026 10:15:33 AM +00:00', 'Learning from Institutional Failure: Trauma and Loss Among Bereaved Families of Women Soldiers', 'The study explores the emotional toll and mental burden experienced by bereaved families in the context of the broader implications of institutional failure and delayed communication. The literature on the trauma and loss experienced by bereaved families of women soldiers killed during military service in combat or combat-related activities is particularly sparse. The current study thus aims to extend the knowledge base on trauma, loss, and war experiences to include the voices of bereaved family members of women soldiers and to explore the chain of events informing the relationship between bereaved families, the state, and the military. The study presents a qualitative analysis of personal interviews with 23 bereaved parents and siblings of women soldiers who were killed in a multiple-casualty event while serving in the Israel Defense Forces (IDF) during the Hamas attack on Israel on October 7, 2023. Three main health-related themes emerged from the analysis: the absence of information provided by the military to families during the multiple-casualty event; flaws in the identification of bodies; and chaos and misinformation regarding the status of casualties. Emotional responses common to all the bereaved families included devastation, confusion, anger, a sense of insult, and feelings of betrayal by the military and the state. \nThe painful evidence we present can provide empirical, theoretical, and practical lessons to be learned from the severe institutional mistakes made during a military event involving mass killing. By examining this case study and analyzing how such lessons can be applied, both theoretically and practically, to the experiences of health systems around the globe, we aim to contribute to safeguarding the well-being and mental health of bereaved families.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n', 'Shir Daphna-Tekoah', 'shir.dt@gmail.com', 'Shir Daphna-Tekoah (Ashkelon Academic College)*; Ayelet Harel (Ben-Gurion University   )', 'Daphna-Tekoah, Shir*; Harel, Ayelet', 'shir.dt@gmail.com*; ayeleths@bgu.ac.il', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Moral Trauma and Loss Among Families of Women Combat-Support Soldiers.docx (15,099 bytes)', '1', 'Ecological Moral Injury among Healthcare Professionals in a VUCA World Implications for Medical Social Work.pdf (78,004 bytes)', '1', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Prof. Ayelet Harel, Faculty of Humanities and Social Sciences,\nConflict Management and Resolution Program\nand The Department of Politics and Government\nBen-Gurion University of the Negev, Israel', 'Paper Presentation'),
('94', 'psahoo24@gmail.com', '97', '5/8/2026 1:10:55 PM +00:00', '5/8/2026 1:10:55 PM +00:00', 'Impact of Mining Operations on the Health and Nutritional Status of Local Communities in Odisha, India', 'Introduction: Mining operations have numerous negative impacts on the periphery that cause loss of ancestral occupation and livelihood, damage to forest, agriculture and common property resources, pollute the local environment and affect the overall socio-economic life of people living in the region. Nutrition and health status of the mining workers and local people are two major indicators affected significantly by mining activities, which are inadequately compensated by the companies.\nData and Methods: The present exploratory study tries to assess the effects of mining operations on the nutrition and health of mine workers and local people living in the mining periphery of one of the major mining districts (Keonjhar) of Odisha, India. The primary study used a mixed-methods approach to collect cross-sectional data from 257 households (1295 population) living in the core mining regions, using a structured interview schedule. The data were collected from eight villages and operational mines under five blocks of Keonjhar.\nResults: The study found that mining operations have affected the nutritional intake and health of the mine workers and their community. Poverty, loss of access to forest and productive agricultural land, poor communication, and distance to market were major factors for inadequate nutrition. Diseases caused by environmental issues, i.e., fever, cold, cough, and occupational illnesses like lung problems, asthma, chest pain, joint pain, weakness, and skin disease, were the most reported diseases in the mine areas.\nConclusion: Mining operations have a critical negative impact on the mining workers, which damages overall health and nutrition by causing numerous socio-economic and environmental hazards. The profit motive of mining companies causes non-compliance with the law, guided by the Mines Act and the DGMS for healthcare and nutrition in the mining locality.\n\nKeywords: Nutrition, Body Mass Index (BMI), Disease, Healthcare, Mining, Infrastructure, Mining Workers.', 'Pradeep Sahoo', 'psahoo24@gmail.com', 'Pradeep Sahoo (Centurion University of Technology and Management)*', 'Sahoo, Pradeep*', 'psahoo24@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract_BMI and Mining_Sahoo et al.docx (17,459 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', '2.	Ranjan Kumar Mohanty\nXavier Institute of Management, XIM University, Bhubaneswar, 751013, Odisha, India\nE-mail: ranjanmohanty85@gmail.com\nOrcid ID: 0000-0002-4902-3209\n3.	Himanshu Sekhar Rout*\nDepartment of A. & A. Economics, Utkal University, Bhubaneswar, Odisha, India\nPopulation Research Centre Bhubaneswar, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India, New Delhi, India\nE-mail: hsrout@utkaluniversity.ac.in\nOrcid ID: 0000-0001-8445-340X\n4.	Kirtti Ranjan Paltasingh\nDepartment of Economics, Ravenshaw University, Cuttack, Odisha, India\nE-mail: kirttiecon@gmail.com\nOrcid ID: 0000-0001-7431-6137', 'Paper Presentation'),
('95', 'apple820644@gmail.com', '98', '5/11/2026 7:34:09 AM +00:00', '5/11/2026 7:35:53 AM +00:00', 'Problematic internet-related experiences and social support as predictors of depressive mood', 'Objectives:\nThis study examined the relative associations of problematic internet-related experiences and perceived social support with depressive mood in a nationally representative Taiwanese sample (N = 4,904).\n\nDesign:\nCross-sectional secondary data analysis.\n\nModel:\nHierarchical regression was used. Variables were entered in three blocks: demographics, problematic internet-related experiences, and perceived social support.\n\nResult (Preliminary):\nPerceived social support was the strongest protective factor (β = -.282, p < .001), followed by problematic internet-related experiences as a significant risk factor (β = .232, p < .001). Age (β = -.128, p < .001) and female gender (β = .079, p < .001) were also significant. The final model explained 16.0% of the variance in depressive mood (adjusted R² = .159).\n\nConclusion:\nSocial support plays a more important role than problematic internet-related experiences in explaining depressive mood in the general population. Strengthening social connection and addressing maladaptive internet-related behaviours should be considered in mental health strategies.', 'chengwei hsieh', 'apple820644@gmail.com', 'chengwei hsieh (national chi nan university)*', 'hsieh, chengwei*', 'apple820644@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'HSIEH_ICSWHMH2027_StudentSeed.docx (15,257 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'no', 'Paper Presentation'),
('96', 'M.Rawat@sms.ed.ac.uk', '99', '5/11/2026 9:13:32 AM +00:00', '5/11/2026 9:13:32 AM +00:00', 'Building resilience and coping strategies: Learning from rituals and culture to improve mental health care ', 'Background:\nIn India, mental illness is a major contributor to the overall disease burden, particularly affecting structurally disadvantaged groups. Despite this burden, the majority of the population has limited or no access to mental health services, and there is a severe shortage of mental health professionals. Furthermore, many communities around the world do not understand mental distress solely through the perspective of biomedical categories. Global mental health and anthropological discourse has long argued that understanding mental distress must be situated within a broader process of meaning-making that includes the social and cultural determinants of health. Early critiques of psychiatry also highlighted the limitations of universal diagnostic frameworks derived from the Global North, particularly when applied to different cultural contexts. Therefore, it is important to analyse the local meaning-making process to better understand how it functions as a strong care mechanism within communities and further learn ways to make mental health policies culturally relevant.\n\nThis study analyses the phenomenon of deity worship and divine possession, also known as mediumship or shamanism, in the Johar Valley of Uttarakhand. The worship of deities holds special significance in people\'s lives, and they are considered divine protectors or bringers of justice. In Munsyari, people worship two types of deities: village deities and family deities. Although these deities are classified into two groups, their role in the community is largely the same: ensuring the well-being of their devotees. The aim of the study is to understand how deity worship ensures well-being in the communities and the role of mediums who act as their vessels in ensuring this process is accomplished. This study also explores the power dynamics within ritual spaces to shed further light on understanding how healing occurs amidst the interplay of social identities.\n\nMethods: \nThe study is based on 13 months of ethnographic fieldwork conducted in the Johar Valley of Uttarakhand, specifically in Munsiyari. Using an anthropological approach, the research combined participant observation, informal conversations, and semi-structured interviews with mediums and ritual healers, biomedical practitioners, frontline health workers, and community members. This approach is particularly valuable for studying mental health, as it enables an in-depth understanding of how distress is locally experienced, interpreted, and managed within everyday social relations, moral worlds, and systems of belief. Unlike survey-based or purely clinical methods, anthropological investigation captures the interplay between kinship, ritual practices, and structural constraints such as limited healthcare access. In doing so, it provides critical insights into how care is actually practiced on the ground, highlighting the need for culturally informed and context-sensitive mental health interventions.\n\nResults: \nFindings indicate that beliefs in deities remain central to how distress is understood and managed in the region. Rituals of deity worship are performed for various distress like, good harvesting, untimely deaths, sudden or unknown illness, unexplained symptoms, and practices involving deity worship constitute key therapeutic response. These practices are not merely symbolic but actively shape experiences of care by fostering social cohesion, reinforcing kinship ties, and providing culturally meaningful frameworks for interpreting suffering. Participants described how such rituals generate a sense of belonging and emotional support, which contributes to coping and recovery during periods of uncertainty and hardship.\nAt the same time, study reveals that distress is often interpreted as a consequence of disrupted relations with deities. Any failure in ritual obligation, such as forgetting to offer what was promised, are believed to result in misfortune, including sudden illness. Mediumship plays a crucial role in diagnosing these disruptions as mediums are consulted to identify the underlying causes of distress and prescribe appropriate ritual remedies.\n\nConclusion: \nOverall, experiences of mental distress are situated within a dense therapeutic landscape in which biomedical care, ritual healing, kinship networks, and cultural beliefs intersect, rather than operate as separate or competing domains. The study approach ritual, therefore, not merely as a religious or a site of recovery or healing, but as a living field where social identities intersect and transform. I argue that it is within this interaction that healing occurs through divine intervention while new social ties are formed or existing ones are strengthened. \n', 'Meenal Rawat', 'M.Rawat@sms.ed.ac.uk', 'Meenal Rawat (University of Edinburgh )*', 'Rawat, Meenal*', 'M.Rawat@sms.ed.ac.uk*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'NA', 'Paper Presentation'),
('97', 'itakofman@gmail.com', '100', '5/12/2026 3:30:01 PM +00:00', '5/12/2026 3:30:01 PM +00:00', 'Promoting Equity in Mental Health Care: A Systematic Review of Psychiatric Rehabilitation Utilization and Retention', 'Objectives\nPsychiatric rehabilitation services (PRS) are central to recovery, community integration, and quality of life (QOL) among individuals with severe mental illness (SMI). Grounded in human rights and equal opportunity, PRS aim to ensure equitable access to community participation. Despite proven effectiveness, utilization and retention remain low, with disparities among minority and underserved groups. This review synthesizes empirical findings on factors influencing PRS use and retention, guided by Andersen’s Behavioral Model of Health Service Use (BMHSU\nDesign \nFollowing PRISMA guidelines, a search across PubMed, PsycINFO, Web of Science, and Scopus yielded 12,566 records published up to June 2024. After screening and quality assessment, 16 empirical studies were included. Studies spanned the United States, Europe, Asia, and the Middle East. Data were thematically analyzed using BMHSU domains. \nResults \nThree domains were central to PRS utilization and retention: Healthcare system-flexible, user-centered, and well-staffed services supported retention, while rigid, under-resourced programs and transport barriers led to dropout. Environmental-stigma, discrimination, lack of culturally adapted services, and regional disparities restricted equity, particularly among marginalized groups. Individual: Predisposing-positive attitudes and hope enhanced retention; minority status and low motivation reduced use. Enabling-family support and trust-based client–provider relationships promoted engagement. Need-psychiatric symptoms, comorbidities, and hospitalization history shaped service use. \nConclusion \nPolicies must reduce disparities, expand culturally responsive services, and strengthen workforce sustainability. Addressing stigma, building therapeutic alliances, and engaging families and communities are vital for continuity and empowerment. Findings highlight the need for rights-based, multilevel interventions that promote recovery, QOL and affirm PRS as a human right. The findings also underscore the important role of social work and community mental health systems in promoting equitable, recovery-oriented, and culturally responsive PRS\n', 'ita kofman', 'itakofman@gmail.com', 'ita kofman (\'Bar Ilan\' university)*; Rena  Bina (\'Bar Ilan\' university); Haya Itzhaky (\'Bar Ilan\' university)', 'kofman, ita*; Bina, Rena ; Itzhaky, Haya', 'itakofman@gmail.com*; Rena.bina@biu.ac.il; Haya.Itzhaky@biu.ac.il', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Ita Kofman abstract 1.docx (18,766 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Rena Bina, PhD, Head of clinical rehabilitation track, School of Social Work, Bar-Ilan University, Ramat Gan, Israel\nRena.bina@biu.ac.il\nand\nHaya Itzhaky, Prof., Head, School of Social Work, Jerusalem Multidisciplinary College (JMC), and Professor Emerita, Bar-Ilan University\nHaya.Itzhaky@biu.ac.il\n\n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('99', 'gsanhueza@luc.edu', '102', '5/13/2026 2:47:54 PM +00:00', '5/13/2026 2:47:54 PM +00:00', 'Promoting Prison Reform through Collaborative Research: a case study in Chile', 'Prison systems in Latin America currently present various problems that include severe overcrowding, high levels of violence, scarce access to reintegration programs, and high recidivism, among others. In this precarious context, the effective reintegration of incarcerated people becomes very problematic and unlikely since, upon leaving prison, people face various barriers that are combined with a series of accumulated disadvantages that were amplified by the experience of incarceration. However, a somewhat forgotten prerequisite for effective reintegration processes is the development of evidence-based policies that rely on good data at different levels of the criminal justice chain. To address this problem, our team partnered with the Chilean prison service in Chile and gathered data on various indicators that were relevant for prison life and rehabilitation, creating an integrated visualization prototype. Thus, this article summarizes the design, implementation process and main results of this collaborative initiative carried out between 2022-2023, including various examples of the displayed prototype. We employed a mixed-methods approach that combined the use of surveys and administrative data, with more than 110 qualitative interviews, along with geocoded material, and an analysis of the prison-related normative context. Both the prototype of integrated visualization and the various research products derived from this project are generating useful information for prison administrators and decision makers to enhance the chances for a better reintegration of incarcerated people in Chile. Finally, we discuss possibilities and limitations of this case study considering the US context and the Smart Decarceration Initiative.', 'Guillermo Sanhueza', 'gsanhueza@luc.edu', 'Guillermo Sanhueza (Loyola University Chicago)*', 'Sanhueza, Guillermo*', 'gsanhueza@luc.edu*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', 'Elisa Gordon (The Mount Sinai Hospital)', 'elisa.gordon@mountsinai.org', '', '', '', '', '2. Mark Bunnell, MSW Loyola University Chicago. Independent therapist. email: mbunnell@luc.edu \n\n3. Liza Zúñiga, PhD student, University of Chile. email: liza.zuniga.c@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('102', 'amanda.hyne@mountsinai.org', '105', '5/13/2026 3:04:09 PM +00:00', '5/13/2026 4:18:58 PM +00:00', 'Self-Compassion and Acceptance: Coping With Inflammatory Bowel Disease During Difficult Times', 'Inflammatory bowel diseases impact more than just intestinal lining, as patients with diagnoses of Crohn’s Disease or ulcerative colitis are more likely to experience symptoms of anxiety or depression. The connection between mental health and disease status has been well documented, making the need for patient support around adaptive coping critical to wellbeing. The social work role can be utilized to help patients with inflammatory bowel diseases develop greater self- acceptance, build social networks to reduce isolation, and cope with disease related anxiety more resiliently with less shame. Understanding psychologist Kristin Neff’s self-compassion construct is integral to helping patients with chronic illness navigate feelings of shame, unworthiness, and embarrassment when facing symptoms such as bowel incontinence, urgency, and drastic weight changes. Neff’s three principles of self-compassion, including self-kindness, mindfulness, and shared humanity, can help patients increase psychological flexibility in adjustment while managing the unpredictable stressors and social stigmatization they often face, especially during periods of active disease. Self-compassion has been correlated with reduced distress and better mental health outcomes in patients with chronic health conditions. Thus, application of this framework within treatment modalities is beneficial for clinical social work with patients diagnosed with inflammatory bowel diseases. ', 'Amanda Hyne', 'amanda.hyne@mountsinai.org', 'Amanda Hyne (Mount Sinai Hospital)*', 'Hyne, Amanda*', 'amanda.hyne@mountsinai.org*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Professional Practice, Interventions, and Models of Care', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'N/A', 'Paper Presentation'),
('104', 'liraz1785@gmail.com', '107', '5/14/2026 8:40:38 AM +00:00', '5/14/2026 8:43:12 AM +00:00', 'Help-Seeking and Treatment Satisfaction Among Evacuated and Non-Evacuated Populations from War Zones', 'Background\nFollowing the terrorist attack in southern Israel on October 7, 2023, and the ensuing war, certain communities were evacuated to hotels across the country, while other populations remained in their homes despite extensive exposure to traumatic events. Exposure to combat and political violence is associated with psychological distress, including symptoms of Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD), anxiety, and depression. The help-seeking model posits that receipt of mental health treatment is influenced by internal factors (e.g., distress levels, gender, and attitudes toward treatment) and external factors (e.g., service availability and cultural norms; Saint Arnault, 2009).\nResilience Centers were established in Israel to provide immediate response to populations living under the ongoing threat of terror and war. These centers are grounded in a trauma-informed early intervention approach, emphasizing high availability and accessible care alongside community work aimed at strengthening personal and social resilience. During the Iron Swords War, Resilience Centers operated under several models adapted to the specific population served.\nObjectives\nThis study examined the extent of help-seeking at Resilience Centers among evacuees from Sderot and residents of the \"Mishor HaGefen\" neighborhood in Ofakim, as well as the variables predicting such help-seeking. In addition, the study assessed recipients\' evaluations of various aspects of the therapeutic interventions provided by the Resilience Centers.\nMethod\nThe study is based on two cross-sectional surveys. The first was conducted three weeks after the attack (November–December 2023) among the evacuated population of Sderot, relocated to hotels throughout the country, and included 810 residents (77.4% women). The Resilience Center provided services within the hotels or via online treatment. The second survey was conducted eight months after the October 7 attack (June–August 2024) among residents of Ofakim. The current study focuses on a sub-sample from a neighborhood directly affected by the events of October 7 but not evacuated (\"Mishor HaGefen\"), comprising 387 respondents (66.3% women). The Resilience Center operated within the neighborhood itself. Data were collected via an online questionnaire distributed through various methods in each sample.\nMeasures\nParticipants were asked a single item regarding help-seeking at the Resilience Center (sought help / did not seek help). Additional measures included direct and indirect exposure (presence at a terror event, direct life threat, injury, witnessing injury or death, property damage), Acute Stress Disorder (ASD; assessed using the NSESS), and in the Ofakim sample PTSD (ITQ), anxiety (GAD-7), and depression (PHQ-9). Among those who received treatment at the Resilience Center, satisfaction with various aspects of the intervention was assessed using a scale developed specifically for this study.\nResults\nApproximately 46% of participants in the Sderot sample and 32% in the Mishor HaGefen (Ofakim) sample reported seeking treatment at the Resilience Centers. Among the evacuated population, gender (female), parenthood, and high levels of acute stress symptoms but not the degree of exposure to terror and war events per se were significant predictors of help-seeking. Among the non-evacuated population, help-seeking was weakly correlated with exposure levels and moderately correlated with elevated symptoms of PTSD, anxiety, and depression. Across both samples, help-seeking was thus associated with the subjective distress experienced by residents.\nIndividuals who received treatment at the Resilience Centers in both samples provided high ratings across the various dimensions of the therapeutic framework. Satisfaction scores were not associated with demographic characteristics or levels of psychological distress.\nConclusions\nThe findings indicate that individuals accurately identify the distress they are experiencing and are aware of their need for help. From the perspective of service recipients a central vantage point in treatment outcome evaluation Resilience Center interventions were highly valued. Future research should examine the long-term effects of these interventions, as well as additional mechanisms shaping patterns of psychosocial service utilization in the context of mass trauma exposure.\nKeywords:help-seeking, Resilience Centers, mass trauma, evacuation, PTSD, acute stress, treatment satisfaction, Israel', 'Liraz cohen-biton', 'liraz1785@gmail.com', 'Liraz cohen-biton (Ashkelon Academic College)*', 'cohen-biton, Liraz*', 'liraz1785@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '2', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Liraz Cohen Biton(PhD) is a Lecturer and Senior Faculty Member, Faculty of Social Work, Ashkelon Academic College; Liraz1785@gmail.com\n\nMiriam Schiff (PhD) is a Professor, Zena Harman Chair in Social Work, and the Dean of the School of Social Work and Social Welfare at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem; miriam.schiff@mail.huji.ac.il\n\nRami Benbenishty is a Professor emeritus at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Israel AND  Andrés Bello University, Santiago, Chile; ramibenben@gmail.com\n\nOhad Gilbar is a Senior Lecturer at the School of Social Work at the Hebrew university of Jerusalem; ohad.gilbar@mail.huji.ac.il\n‏\nRuth Pat-Horenczyk, Ph.D., is a Full Professor at the School of Social Work and Social Welfare at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem; horenczyk@mail.huji.ac.il', 'Paper Presentation'),
('105', 'kavita.nimhans@gmail.com', '108', '5/15/2026 5:57:15 AM +00:00', '5/15/2026 6:02:09 AM +00:00', 'EFFECTIVENESS OF GENDER SENSITIZATION TRAINING FOR CHILDREN IN CONFLICT WITH LAW: A QUASI-EXPERIMENTAL STUDY', 'Background: Children form an important part of society, and some of them enter into conflict with the law as a result of several psychosocial vulnerabilities. Lack of age-appropriate learning and an unhealthy social environment, which become a part of children’s socialisation, contribute to their entry into the legal system. Studies have shown that crimes against women rose to around 15.3% in 2021 as compared to the previous year. This necessitates the need for gender sensitization among children as a part of their learning processes, especially for children in conflict with the law, since some of them would have also indulged in violence against females.  \nMethodology: This study was conducted as a part of the project ‘NIMHANS-Government of Karnataka collaborative project on Gender Sensitization for children in observation homes and special homes and Perpetrator training in prisons\', by the Department of Psychiatric Social Work, NIMHANS, in collaboration with the Government of Karnataka. The aim of the study was to understand the effectiveness of gender sensitization training for 30 children in conflict with the law residing in the Special Home in Bengaluru. Gender sensitization training was conducted for the children over 6 sessions following a pre-assessment. A structured questionnaire consisting of 15 items and the Gender Roles Attitude Scale (GRAS) were used for the pre-post assessments. Descriptive and inferential statistics were used to analyse the data. \nResults: The mean age of the children was 18.4. The results show that the children’s knowledge and understanding and their attitude towards gender roles have improved after the training program across majority of the domains. \nConclusion: The study shows the need for gender sensitization training among children in conflict with the law. It also demonstrates the way forward for more scientific and systematic inquiry into this area.\n', 'Kavita Jangam', 'kavita.nimhans@gmail.com', 'Kavita Jangam (NIMHANS)*', 'Jangam, Kavita*', 'kavita.nimhans@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'abstract for Rajgiri Int conf.docx (14,808 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'E. Aravind Raj\nAbhishikta Naik \nSunu Merla CJ\nXavior B', 'Paper Presentation'),
('106', 'josmathews@gmail.com', '109', '5/15/2026 6:24:02 AM +00:00', '5/15/2026 6:24:02 AM +00:00', 'Community Resilience: Theoretical Insights', '                                       ABSTRACT\nResilience, defined and analysed across individuals, ecosystems, and social contexts, including community contexts, refers to the ability to withstand adversity, emergencies, and negative conditions. It is a dynamic state that develops over time. Conceptualisation of resilience at the ecological, social system, and psychological levels aims to elucidate its dynamics and explain the mechanisms that underlie its development and maintenance at the levels at which it is understood. This conceptual paper seeks to offer a socio-psychological explanation of the development and maintenance of community resilience. The behavioural explanation of community resilience draws upon social psychological theories and prevailing social and ecological views. The analysis of these theories proceeds along the levels of the individual, the group, and the larger community, which can be labelled micro-, meso-, and macro-oriented, respectively. Individual theories of resilience draw upon individual psychological resources, including cognitive, affective, motivational, and personality-centred resources, such as attachment theory, cognitive theory, positive psychology, social learning theory, and related ones. In the same vein, social psychological theories such as group development, social identity theory, social exchange theory, interpersonal theories, and related processes of group relations, to a greater extent, explain the development of community resilience. At the macro level, social processes, cultural norms, and social structures shape the emergence of community resilience. The key argument of the paper centres on the levels of community resilience that provide a holistic understanding, leading to greater clarity.\nKeywords: Resilience, Community resilience, Psychological theories, Social-psychological theories\n\n', 'jose mathews', 'josmathews@gmail.com', 'jose mathews (Chinmaya Viswa Vidyapeeth)*', 'mathews, jose*', 'josmathews@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'abstract.docx (15,769 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Single author', 'Paper Presentation'),
('107', 'alyvredenburgh@gmail.com', '110', '5/16/2026 11:58:35 AM +00:00', '5/16/2026 11:58:35 AM +00:00', 'Does Belonging Reduce Depression? A Cross-Sectional Study of Social Connection, Loneliness, and Depression', 'Background: The interplay between social connection, loneliness, and depression remains an important area of inquiry for community mental health. While research has established associations between social factors and psychological well-being, the relative contributions of friendship quality, ffinancial stress, and religious identity — and the mechanisms linking them — are less well understood in community samples.\nMethods: A cross-sectional online survey was administered by WeBelong Institute (N = 178; Mage = 38.6; 55.6% female). Participants completed the PHQ-9, a four-item loneliness scale, a seven-item belonging and wellbeing scale, a four-item friendship quality scale, and measures of financial worry, religiosity, and social network size. Multiple regression and bias-corrected bootstrap mediation analyses (10,000 iterations) were conducted.\nResults: Loneliness was the strongest independent predictor of depression (r = .61, p < .001), accounting for 37.3% of variance in PHQ-9 scores. Financial worry showed a significant dose-response relationship with depression (η² = .137). Mediation analyses revealed that loneliness fully mediated the relationship between overall connection and depression (proportion mediated = 101%), and substantially mediated the friendship quality–depression relationship (73.3%). Religious/spiritual identity was associated with significantly higher belonging (d = .47) but not lower depression. Men reported larger social networks than women, but no gender differences emerged in friendship quality, loneliness, or depression.\nConclusions: Loneliness functions as the primary psychological pathway through which social connection influences depression. Interventions targeting felt loneliness — rather than social network size alone — may be most effective. Financial worry represents a partially independent pathway to depression that social support alone does not fully address.\nKeywords: belonging, loneliness, depression, friendship quality, financial stress, mediation, PHQ-9\n', 'aly VREDENBURGH', 'alyvredenburgh@gmail.com', 'aly VREDENBURGH (WeBelong Institute)*', 'VREDENBURGH, aly*', 'alyvredenburgh@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Aly Vredenburgh, CEO of WeBelong Institute, alyvredenburgh@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('108', 'marissa.rheiner@gmail.com', '111', '5/18/2026 1:28:37 AM +00:00', '5/18/2026 1:28:37 AM +00:00', 'Addressing Antisemitism-Related Social Disconnection in Clinical Practice: A Relational-Cultural Approach', 'Rates of antisemitism have steadily been on the rise over the last decade, with contemporary antisemitism having roots in anti-Israel opinions or disagreements over the Israeli-Palestinian conflict. Using the International Holocaust Remembrance Alliance’s definition of antisemitism, this presentation explores how antisemitic incidents manifest in physical, written, and media-based forms and how they target individuals, their property, or their places of worship. This presentation positions antisemitism not only as a systemic form of oppression, but as an interpersonal trauma that has documented impacts to the social connectivity of Jewish individuals. An overview of the literature within this presentation demonstrates patterns of social avoidance, social isolation, identity concealment, and feelings of rejection In Jewish individuals associated with antisemitic experiences. This presentation will seek to name these phenomena as Antisemitism-Related Social Disconnection (ASRSD) and explore how it can be addressed within clinical treatment settings. This presentation positions Relational-Cultural Theory and approaches grounded in it as a method of addressing the social disconnection emerging within this population. This presentation explores how to utilize Relational-Cultural Theory to situate the problem within the larger sociopolitical and cultural context, and how to explore controlling and relational images that lead to disconnection. This presentation will explore strategies to promote a growth-fostering relationship with the Jewish client experiencing ASRSD within the clinical space, to use the therapeutic relationship as a discrepant relational image, and how to express mutuality in order to promote relational resilience. In addition, this presentation will explore how to utilize established and validated measures within clinical treatment approaches to help quantify the relational work being done within the therapeutic space. Overall, this theory-based practice approach promotes an equitable and socially just treatment approach for the Jewish population experiencing ASRSD.', 'Marissa Garfunkel', 'marissa.rheiner@gmail.com', 'Marissa Garfunkel (Simmons University)*', 'Garfunkel, Marissa*', 'marissa.rheiner@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Marissa Garfunkel\nSimmons University\nmarissa.garfunkel@simmons.edu\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-0889-9469', 'Poster Presentation'),
('109', 'compassionateresistance@yahoo.com', '112', '5/19/2026 3:25:44 AM +00:00', '5/19/2026 3:25:44 AM +00:00', 'Development and Delivery of JEDI and Anti-Oppression Concepts in Entry-Level Health Professional Education ', 'This study is based on findings from a two-year qualitative study involving the development and facilitation of SOWK210: Justice, Equity, Diversity, & Inclusion [JEDI] in Social Work at Colorado Mesa University. This academic institution educates approximately 9,400 undergraduate students in Grand Junction, Colorado; a politically conservative community in a rural part of the state [United States of America]. \n\nIn creating neutrality when discussing dichotomized and uncomfortable subjects, this semester-long class content was created based on theoretical pedagogy. Previously known as a Bachelor of Social Work class with a title and subject matter involving working with “Diverse Populations”, former BSW Director of Colorado Mesa University, Professor Aisha Chapra Sander formulated the foundational change of this class into creating space for students to have brave, opposing conversations.  \n\nAdditionally, when creating the outline and content for the class, the core values from the NASW [National Association of Social Workers] were continuously referenced. The National Association of Social Workers is a historical and powerful social work organization with core values of: service, social justice, dignity and worth of a person, the importance of human relationships, integrity, and competence (National Association of Social Workers [NASW], 2021). Additionally, the 2021 NASW Code of Ethics update emphasized the importance of professional self-care, which is another topic that was discussed, as well as practiced, with the students throughout the class (NASW, 2021). \n\nIt is no surprise, the past year has been incredibly challenging in the United States from an inclusive standpoint. The current political administration has greatly contested the concepts of social justice, equity, diversity, and inclusion, as well as the social work profession as a whole. It is because of the Council on Social Work Education and their mandate that BSW students gain education on “Advancing Humans Rights and Social, Economic, and Environmental Justice (Competency 3) that this class and its content was never brought into question (Council of Social Work Education [CSWE], 2022). \n\nAnother reason this extremely controversial class was given its autonomy involves one of the most important realizations gained from teaching this class; the is the vast importance of creating a nonjudgemental, brave educational space for students to have what has lovingly become known as “spicy conversations”. They were encouraged to talk about topics people and communities often shy away from, such as culturally responsive pedagogy, imperialism, equality in queer rights, compassionate resistance, discrimination, respectful dissension, and cultural appropriation. Through their assignments and discussions, the students learned how to present their viewpoints, often opposing, with dignity and respect. \n\nThis is a qualitative summation teaching two years of an incredibly dynamic class that focuses on the successes and challenges with the creation, as well as execution of assignments. For example, each of the last two semesters, the students created Social Media JEDI Reels. This assignment overall was the most unpredictable in the syllabus and turned out to be one of the most impactful. In a time when two-thirds of the global population uses social media and the United States is continuously facing a heightened level of fascism hegemony, this assignment gave the students the opportunity to present their viewpoints and process through incredibly relevant current topics.  \n\nThe United States of America and the whole world is really on a journey of learning about the past so it can inform the future, as well as about racial and cultural dynamics. The paper will also focus on the overall conclusions gained for successful facilitation of classes with subject matters such as this, as well as application on a global level. The invitation is for everyone to adopt a growth-oriented mindset so that people can learn, as well as unlearn, to advocate for a more equitable, collaborative, inclusive process when it comes to how humans carry ourselves, educate others, steward these precious lands, and lead dynamic communities.   ', 'Kelsey Worfler', 'compassionateresistance@yahoo.com', 'Kelsey Worfler (Brave Spaces Health & Wellness)*; Kelsey Worfler (Colorado Mesa University)', 'Worfler, Kelsey*; Worfler, Kelsey', 'compassionateresistance@yahoo.com*; kworfler@coloradomesa.edu', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ICSWHMH Abstract-Worfler (1).docx (23,922 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'None', 'Paper Presentation')
