('110', 'jeezesarasaji@gmail.com', '113', '5/19/2026 5:07:23 AM +00:00', '5/19/2026 5:10:17 AM +00:00', 'Integrating Mental Health in Policing: Global Policy Perspectives and Lessons for India', 'Policing as a profession has evolved and transformed over time due to changing patterns in crime. The changes have demanded increased psychological burden on police personnel\'s mental health and well-being due to exposure to traumatic events, stress, and occupational risks; as a result, they experience depression, anxiety, and post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), which are higher in law enforcement officers compared to the general population. From an Indian lens, it worsens the situation by adding inadequate structural and institutional reforms.\nObjectives:\nThis study aims to examine mental health and well-being policy frameworks in policing systems across different countries and assess their relevance for strengthening mental health support within the Indian policing system. \nDesign:\nThe study adopts a comparative policy analysis of mental health and well-being frameworks in selected countries, including the United States, United Kingdom, Canada, and Australia. Policy documents and scholarly literature are reviewed to identify key approaches and support mechanisms implemented within police organizations.\nResult and Conclusion:\nThe paper identifies the need to integrate similar mental health frameworks into the Indian policing system, which are practised in different countries.  Institutionalising psychological services, peer-support mechanisms, and trauma-informed policies can improve officer well-being, reduce burnout, and enhance the overall effectiveness, accountability, and sustainability of policing institutions in India.\n \nKeywords: Police mental health, policing policy, global practices, occupational well-being \n\n', 'Jeeze Saji', 'jeezesarasaji@gmail.com', 'Jeeze Saji (Central University of Gujarat )*; Sony Kunjappan (Central University of Gujarat)', 'Saji, Jeeze*; Kunjappan, Sony', 'jeezesarasaji@gmail.com*; sony@cug.ac.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Workforce Development, Wellbeing, and Leadership', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Mental Health and Policing.docx (26,037 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Prof. Sony Kunjappan, Professor and Head of Department, Department of Studies in Social Management, Central University of Gujarat. sony@cug.ac.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('111', 'farhancyra@gmail.com', '114', '5/19/2026 9:20:12 AM +00:00', '5/19/2026 9:20:12 AM +00:00', 'Awareness or Distortion? Social Media\'s Dual Impact on Mental Health Perceptions Among Adolescents and Young Adults: A Narrative Review', 'We live in an era in which social media has fundamentally reshaped how people access information, form opinions, and make sense of the world around them. The digital platforms – particularly short-form video platforms like Instagram Reels and YouTube Shorts – have become the dominant environment for information seeking in contemporary life, from politics to parenting, from health to identity. Mental health is no exception. For adolescents and young adults today, these platforms are not supplementary sources of mental health knowledge — they are often the first, and sometimes the only, source.\nThis shift is not merely technological. Drawing on Castells\' theorisation of the network society and Bronfenbrenner\'s ecological systems framework, foundational to social work\'s person-in-environment tradition, this paper argues that short-form social media has emerged as a social institution in the fullest sociological sense. It socialises. It constructs norms. It distributes authority over what it means to be mentally well or unwell. Unlike the institutions that preceded it — family, education, religion — this one is algorithmically governed, commercially driven, and clinically unaccountable.\nUnqualified creators dominate mental health content on these platforms, consistently pathologising normative emotional experiences, misrepresenting diagnostic criteria, and normalising self-diagnosis. Platform algorithms compound this by amplifying emotionally resonant content regardless of clinical accuracy, trapping vulnerable users in recursive loops where distress generates further distress. Every sadness risks becoming a disorder. Every symptom is a diagnosis.\nThis paper does not argue against social media’s mental health awareness; its role in reducing stigma is real and must be acknowledged. The argument is more precise: the same architecture that produces awareness also produces distortion, and these two effects cannot be separated. Through a narrative review of peer-reviewed literature, institutional reports, and observational studies published between 2019 and 2025, drawing across communication studies, clinical psychology, public health, and social work, this paper maps that dual effect critically. A narrative review was chosen deliberately — the literature is interdisciplinary, methodologically varied, and not yet mature enough for meta-analytic synthesis. What this moment requires is not a count of findings but a critical reading of them — one capable of holding contradictions, tracing patterns, and constructing a coherent argument from evidence that does not yet speak in one voice.\nIt has direct implications for social work. The person-in-environment framework has always held that psychosocial context is inseparable from mental health. Social media has now become a psychosocial environment — shaping self-understanding, help-seeking behaviour, and how young people present to practitioners. This paper calls on social work practice, education, and policy to treat clients\' social media environments as clinically relevant factors—and to engage seriously with the unregulated institutional space that quietly does what practitioners are trained to do, but without the training, ethics, or accountability.\nThe institution has changed. The practice must follow.\nKeywords: Social media, Mental health misinformation, Adolescents, Young adults, Self-diagnosis, Algorithmic amplification, Short-form video, Mental health perceptions, Person-in-environment, Social institution, Pathologisation, Mental health literacy, Social work practice, Narrative review\n', 'Muhammed Farhan C K', 'farhancyra@gmail.com', 'Muhammed Farhan C K (University of Delhi)*', 'C K, Muhammed Farhan*', 'farhancyra@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ICSWHMH Abstract.docx (16,351 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Sole-Authored', 'Paper Presentation'),
('112', 'dthomas@socialwork.du.ac.in', '116', '5/20/2026 8:31:19 AM +00:00', '5/31/2026 3:03:47 PM +00:00', 'Covid 19 Outbreak and Community Engagement: A study of Community Health Workers in Kerala', 'This article explores the extent of community engagement through the functioning of Community Health Workers (CHWs) during the Covid 19 outbreak in Kerala. It elaborates upon how CHWs addressed various social issues during a pandemic crisis by involving in community initiatives and their challenges in practice. The case study approach was used to obtain a holistic description from six respondents, comprising ASHA (Accredited Social Health Activist) workers and Kudumbashree health volunteers who were selected through purposive sampling. A semi-structured interview schedule was utilised and the researcher conducted telephonic interviews as the data collection phase happened during the second wave of the COVID-19 outbreak. \n\nThe study showcases the prominent social issues during the outbreak from hunger to elderly care, contextualised the community engagement phenomenon and also emphasized the role of health workers which aided in understanding the collaborative networks formed for infection control/preparedness and outreach practices. It also highlights the challenges faced by Women health workers such as burnout, lack of social security/permanency of work, difficulty in home visits for ASHA’s and no wages or incentives in the case of Kudumbashree workers in health interventions, thus advocating for policy measures at state/central government levels to address their labour rights. ', 'Dennis Thomas', 'dthomas@socialwork.du.ac.in', 'Dennis Thomas (University of Delhi)*; Dr. Shivani Chauhan  Barooah (Tata Institute of Social Sciences, Guwahati)', 'Thomas, Dennis*; Barooah, Dr. Shivani Chauhan ', 'dthomas@socialwork.du.ac.in*; shivani.barooah@tiss.edu', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract-ICSWHMH_Dennis.docx (15,411 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Dr. Shivani Chauhan Barooah\nAssistant Professor\nGuwahati Campus,\nAssistant Professor, Centre for Community Organisation and Development Practice-Guwahati, School of Social Work - Guwahati Campus\n\nChairperson, Centre for Labour Studies and Social Protection, School of Social Sciences and Humanities\nEmail id: shivani.barooah@tiss.edu', 'Paper Presentation'),
('113', 'orlysa@bgu.ac.il', '117', '5/20/2026 9:42:01 AM +00:00', '5/20/2026 10:09:14 AM +00:00', 'Sustained Use of Cognitive-Behavioural Skills and Reduced Depressive Symptoms Among Older Adults Two Years After Skill Acquisition', '\nBackground: The COVID-19 pandemic placed older adults at heightened risk for depression due to prolonged uncertainty, social isolation, and fear of illness. Brief cognitive-behavioural interventions (CBIs) may alleviate psychological distress during crises by reducing depression and loneliness and enhancing perceived social support; however, little is known about the long-term maintenance of skills acquired through such interventions. Guided by Taylor’s biopsychosocial model, the present study examined the sustained use of CBIs skills acquired during a brief intervention and their association with lower depressive symptoms nearly two years later.\nMethods: This study constituted the fourth wave of a longitudinal investigation initiated in Israel in March 2020. Data were collected between January and February 2022, during the fifth wave of the COVID-19 pandemic. Participants were 54 community-dwelling adults aged 65 years and older who completed all previous assessments. Measures included the use of CBI skills acquired during the intervention, depressive symptoms, loneliness, and perceived social support.\nFindings: Approximately 70% of participants continued to use cognitive skills several times per week, including positive cognitive restructuring and positive self-talk. Approximately half of the participants continued to practice behavioural techniques, such as breathing exercises. Depression levels were significantly lower at T3 compared with baseline levels. Perceived social support declined significantly over time, whereas loneliness levels remained stable. Greater use of acquired CBI skills predicted lower levels of depression at T3, even after controlling for demographic variables and baseline depression.\nDiscussion and Implications: The findings suggest that skills acquired through brief CBIs may remain beneficial over time and help reduce depressive symptoms among older adults during prolonged crises. The decline in perceived social support highlights the ongoing emotional burden experienced during the pandemic. These results support integrating brief CBIs into routine care for older adults and encouraging the continued use of these skills.\n', 'Orly Sarid', 'orlysa@bgu.ac.il', 'Orly Sarid (Ben Gurion University of the Negev)*; Stav Shapira (Ben-Gurion University of the Negev); Daphna Yeshua-Katz (Ben-Gurion University of the Negev)', 'Sarid, Orly*; Shapira, Stav; Yeshua-Katz, Daphna', 'orlysa@bgu.ac.il*; stavshap@bgu.ac.il; yeshuad@bgu.ac.il', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Professional Practice, Interventions, and Models of Care', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract  india.docx (17,234 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', '1	The Spitzer Department of Social Work, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Beer-Sheva, Israel; orlysa@bgu.ac.il; yeshuad@bgu.ac.il\n2	Goldman Sonnenfeldt School of Sustainability and Climate Change, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Beer-Sheva, Israel;(OS) and (SS)\n3	The School of Public Health, Faculty of Health Sciences, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Israel;stavshap@bgu.ac.il \n', 'Poster Presentation'),
('114', 'priscaliak@uj.ac.za', '118', '5/22/2026 10:02:55 AM +00:00', '5/22/2026 10:02:55 AM +00:00', 'Enhancing Psychological Wellbeing Through Supportive Supervision: Perspectives of Child Protection Social Workers in South Africa', 'Social workers operate within emotionally demanding environments, making supportive supervision essential for promoting their psychological well-being and professional functioning. Supportive supervision aims to reduce the risk of stress and burnout while enhancing practitioners’ ability to cope with the demands of their work. In the absence of adequate support, newly qualified practitioners, particularly those in the child protection sector, are especially vulnerable to stress and burnout. This paper examines the perceived benefits and challenges of supportive supervision in social work practice. Adopting a qualitative research approach, the study explored social workers’ experiences of supportive supervision within a child protection organisation in South Africa. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with 28 social workers in the Western Cape, and the data were analysed thematically. The findings revealed that when the supportive function of supervision is implemented in a structured and consistent manner, it fosters harmonious and caring relationships between supervisors and supervisees, thereby helping to prevent stressful work-related experiences. Supportive supervision was further found to enhance employee engagement and contribute positively to practitioners’ overall job experience. Although child protection social workers were identified as requiring substantial emotional and professional support, participants reported receiving limited supportive supervision. Nevertheless, supportive supervision was perceived as making child protection work more manageable and sustainable. Concerns were raised that supervision within social work practice has increasingly shifted away from educational and supportive functions towards predominantly managerial and administrative responsibilities. The study therefore recommends that supervisors prioritise supportive supervision in order to promote the mental well-being, professional retention, and overall effectiveness of social workers.', 'Priscalia Khosa', 'priscaliak@uj.ac.za', 'Priscalia Khosa (University of Johannesburg)*', 'Khosa, Priscalia*', 'priscaliak@uj.ac.za*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Workforce Development, Wellbeing, and Leadership', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'No co-authors', 'Paper Presentation'),
('115', 'angela.r.savage@jsums.edu', '119', '5/23/2026 5:36:11 PM +00:00', '5/23/2026 5:36:11 PM +00:00', 'Bridging Global Innovations and Community Wisdom: Advancing Culturally Grounded Child Welfare and Mental Health Social Work Through International Partnerships', 'As health and mental health systems across the globe confront increasing social inequities, workforce shortages, intergenerational trauma, and growing behavioral health needs, social workers are uniquely positioned to create culturally responsive and community-centered interventions that strengthen families and communities (National Association of Social Workers [NASW], 2021; World Health Organization [WHO], 2022). This presentation explores how global collaboration, community engagement, and culturally grounded child welfare practices can improve outcomes for vulnerable children, youth, and families, particularly in historically underserved and marginalized communities (Bronfenbrenner, 1979; Ungar, 2021).\nDrawing from experiences within Title IV-E child welfare workforce development initiatives in Mississippi and broader international perspectives in social work education, this presentation highlights innovative strategies that integrate trauma-informed care, community partnerships, simulation-based learning, and culturally responsive mental health practices into social work education and workforce preparation (Council on Social Work Education [CSWE], 2022; Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration [SAMHSA], 2023). The presentation examines how grassroots approaches and collaborative educational models can bridge gaps between academic institutions, public agencies, and communities to strengthen the health and mental health workforce (Acri et al., 2021).\nSpecial attention is given to the role of Historically Black Colleges and Universities (HBCUs) and community-based partnerships in preparing culturally competent practitioners capable of addressing complex mental health and child welfare challenges (Gasman & Commodore, 2014). Participants will also explore how international partnerships foster cross-national learning, promote culturally informed practice frameworks, and support sustainable workforce development initiatives in health and mental health social work (Healy, 2008).\nThrough discussion and case-based examples, attendees will gain practical strategies for implementing community-centered and culturally grounded interventions that advance equity, strengthen mental health systems, and improve child and family well-being across diverse populations.\n', 'Angela Savage', 'angela.r.savage@jsums.edu', 'Angela Savage (Jackson State University)*', 'Savage, Angela*', 'angela.r.savage@jsums.edu*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Workforce Development, Wellbeing, and Leadership', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Tyriesa Howard, MSW Program Chair, Jackson State University tyriesa.howard@jsums.edu \n\nCandace Riddley, BSW Program Chair, Jackson State University\ncandace.l.riddley@jsums.edu ', 'Poster Presentation'),
('116', 'noari@hadassah.org.il', '120', '5/25/2026 7:46:20 AM +00:00', '5/25/2026 7:46:20 AM +00:00', 'Short-Term Intervention in Internal Medicine Wards: Shaping Professional Identity ', 'Objectives: This presentation outlines the fundamental principles of short-term intervention in Internal Medicine wards, specifically as applied to the professional socialization and training of newly qualified social workers.\n\nDesign: As the head of a social work unit in a big hospital, I supervise social workers entering the healthcare system for the first time. Internal Medicine wards—often described as the \"core of the hospital\"—serve as the primary entry point for these workers. A significant challenge in supervision is bridging the perceived gap between long-term psychotherapy and short-term medical social work intervention. Social workers who are unfamiliar with the nature of hospital social work, frequently view these domains as dichotomous, where the former is idealized as \"true\" clinical work and the latter is dismissed as merely technical or administrative. My role is to change this perception, guiding them to recognize and value the therapeutic potential inherent in brief, acute-care interventions.\n\nModel: The presentation highlights key clinical skills essential for the socialization of new medical social workers:\n\n•	Rapid Assessment: The ability to swiftly and accurately identify the complex needs of patients and families in high-pressure environments.\n•	Dynamic Rapport: Establishing meaningful therapeutic relationships with diverse populations (socio-economically and ethnically) during acute crises within dynamic settings.\n•	Integration of Task and Process: Reconceptualizing technical procedures as therapeutic process.\n\nResult: Internalizing the therapeutic nature of acute-care social work remains a supervisory challenge in the Internal Medicine unit. Implementing these core principles facilitates a more distinct professional identity.\n\nConclusion: Most social workers in Internal Medicine wards eventually understand that their intervention requires a variety of clinical skills in order to address a variety of patients and families. Recognizing these interventions as \"professional\" in the highest sense is vital for providing comprehensive care to patients and their families.\n\n\n', 'Noa Rivlin', 'noari@hadassah.org.il', 'Noa Rivlin (Hadassah Medical Center)*', 'Rivlin, Noa*', 'noari@hadassah.org.il*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'none', 'Paper Presentation'),
('117', 'hadasr@hadassah.org.il', '121', '5/25/2026 8:47:18 AM +00:00', '5/25/2026 8:47:18 AM +00:00', 'Collaborative Social Work in Health Care Research Group: Lessons from over 20 years of partnership', 'Objectives: To present the 20-year evolution, core working principles, and challenges of a collaborative research partnership between an academic school of social work and a hospital social work department.\nDesign: Established in 2004 by the School of Social Work at the Hebrew University and the Department of Social Work Services at Hadassah Hospital, this partnership promotes practice-based research. Both parties contribute their distinct strengths: hospital social workers provide clinical expertise and grounded field questions, while academic researchers offer theoretical, methodological, and publishing expertise. The initiative is co-managed and funded by both departments.\nModel: The group evolved from a modest initiative into an established research site. To date, over 45 diverse research projects have been conducted. The group gradually expanded from local collaborations to multi-centered national and international studies involving local and foreign hospitals and universities. Dissemination also evolved from clinicians’ participation in conferences to full partnership in peer-reviewed journal publications.\nResult: The model highlights key organizational realities:\n•	Competing Demands and Pacing: Clinicians face heavy workloads where patient care takes precedence. This delays the planning and development of new research projects, in contrast to academic pressures to submit grant applications and publish rapidly.\n•	The Research Coordinator as a Bridge: A dedicated field coordinator position is crucial. Serving as an institutional liaison who translates the clinical realities of the hospital to academic researchers and vice versa.\nConclusion: The model has merit. However, maintaining and enhancing the group’s achievements requires patience and ongoing funding, both of which are challenging to sustain. This model can serve as an example for other medical social work departments worldwide.\n', 'Hadas Rosenne', 'hadasr@hadassah.org.il', 'Hadas Rosenne (Hadassah Medical Center)*; Nurit Seidel Levy (Hadassah Medical Center); Noa Shemsh (Hadassah Medical Center); Miriam Schiff (Paul Baerwald School of Social Work and Social Welfare, The Hebrew University of Jerusalem)', 'Rosenne, Hadas*; Seidel Levy, Nurit; Shemsh, Noa; Schiff, Miriam', 'hadasr@hadassah.org.il*; nuritzl@hadassah.org.il; noa@hadassah.org.il; miriam.schiff@mail.huji.ac.il', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '2', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Nurit Seidel Levy, Head of the Department of Social Work Services, Hadassah Medical Center, Nuritzl@hadassah.org.il\n\nNoa Shemeh, Head of the Department of Social Work Services, Hadassah Mt. Scopus, Hadassah Medical Center, Noa@hadassah.org.il\n\nMiriam Schiff, Dean, Paul Baerwald School of Social Work and Social Welfare, The Hebrew University of Jerusalem, miriam.schiff@mail.huji.ac.il', 'Paper Presentation'),
('118', 'arifalipsw@gmail.com', '122', '5/25/2026 9:51:36 AM +00:00', '5/25/2026 9:55:45 AM +00:00', 'Beyond the Streets: A Multi-Stakeholder Exploration of Structural Determinants, Service Gaps, and Recovery Pathways among Homeless Persons with Mental Illness in Delhi', 'Background: Homelessness among individuals with mental illness is a significant public health and social concern in urban India, especially in metropolitan settings such as Delhi. Gaining insight into the psychosocial causes, service needs, and systemic challenges from multiple stakeholders\' perspectives is crucial for designing effective and culturally relevant interventions.\nAim: This study aimed to explore stakeholder perspectives regarding the psychosocial factors contributing to homelessness, the healthcare and rehabilitation needs of homeless persons with mental illness, and the barriers affecting access to care and support services in Delhi.\nMaterials and Methods: A qualitative research approach was adopted using Focus Group Discussions (FGDs) conducted at the Institute of Human Behaviour and Allied Sciences (IHBAS), Delhi. A total of five FGDs, involving 46 participants, were conducted. Participants included psychiatrists, clinical psychologists, psychiatric social workers, social workers, NGO personnel, police officials, legal professionals, and shelter-home staff who were directly involved in the care and rehabilitation of homeless individuals with mental illness. Purposive sampling was used for participant selection. The discussions were audio-recorded, transcribed verbatim, translated into English, and thematically analyzed using Braun and Clarke’s framework with the assistance of MAXQDA software.\nResults: The findings revealed that homelessness among persons with mental illness is shaped by interconnected and recurring factors such as poverty, migration, family conflict, substance use, stigma, and social exclusion. Stakeholders highlighted several unmet healthcare and psychosocial needs, including psychiatric treatment, access to food and shelter, emotional support, rehabilitation, and long-term care. Major barriers to service access included lack of identity documents, financial difficulties, stigma and discrimination, fragmented services, and inadequate follow-up care. Participants identified community outreach, multidisciplinary collaboration, shelter-based rehabilitation, and family reintegration as important pathways toward recovery and social inclusion.\nConclusions: The study underscores the importance of moving beyond short-term rescue approaches toward comprehensive, recovery-oriented, and rights-based models of care for homeless persons with mental illness. Strengthening intersectoral coordination, improving continuity of care, and enhancing accessibility of mental health and social welfare services are essential to address homelessness and mental illness in urban India effectively.', 'Arif Ali', 'arifalipsw@gmail.com', 'Arif Ali (IHBAS, Delhi)*; Jahanara  Gajendragad (IHBAS, Delhi); Deepak  Kumar (IHBAS, Delhi); Rajinder  Dhamija (IHBAS, Delhi)', 'Ali, Arif*; Gajendragad, Jahanara ; Kumar, Deepak ; Dhamija, Rajinder ', 'arifalipsw@gmail.com*; jahan_ara_2000@yahoo.com; srivastav.deep@gmail.com; dhamijark@gov.in', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Beyond the Streets.docx_Abstract.docx (14,464 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Dr.Jahanara M Gajendragad Professor & Head, Department of Psychiatric Social Work, Institute of Human Behaviour and Allied Sciences, New Delhi, India. Email: jahan_ara_2000@yahoo.com\n\nDr.Deepak Kumar, Professor & Head, Department of Psychiatry, Institute of Human Behaviour and Allied Sciences, New Delhi, India.  Email: srivastav.deep@gmail.com\n \nDr. Rajinder K Dhamija, Director, Department of Neurology, Institute of Human Behaviour and Allied Sciences, New Delhi, India.  Email: dhamijark@gov.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('119', 'amarmor@staff.haifa.ac.il', '123', '5/25/2026 4:11:42 PM +00:00', '5/27/2026 8:02:46 PM +00:00', ' Navigating Ethical and practical Dilemmas in Mandatory Reporting of Child Sexual Abuse Involving LGBTQ+ Youth', 'In the present proposal, we present an ongoing study examining ethical and clinical dilemmas that arise in the context of mandatory reporting (MR) of suspected child sexual abuse involving LGBTQ+ adolescents. MR laws are a central part of child protection systems worldwide and require professionals to report suspected child abuse. While designed to protect children, these laws can also create difficult ethical and clinical dilemmas, especially when working with vulnerable populations such as LGBTQ+ adolescents who are not openly identified.\nThis study explores how professionals deal with disclosures of child sexual abuse involving LGBTQ+ youth in the Israeli context, where mandatory reporting obligations are broad and often leave limited room for discretion. The study is in its early stages and draws on qualitative questionnaires completed by professionals in mental health and healthcare settings, including social workers, psychologists, and physicians working in hospital and community settings, including emergency and psychiatric units.\nThe questionnaires focus on three areas: professionals’ understanding of the legal framework and its nuances; accounts of real cases and dilemmas from practice; and responses to clinical vignettes presenting borderline or ambiguous situations. Together, these allow an examination of both reported practice and underlying professional reasoning.\nEarly findings point to ongoing tension between legal obligations and the clinical relationship with adolescents. Participants describe situations in which reporting may expose young people to family rejection, forced disclosure of sexual orientation, stigma, or withdrawal from care. Some also report that adolescents avoid or limit disclosure because of fear of mandatory reporting procedures.\nOverall, the study aims to map how professionals currently navigate these situations in practice and how they make sense of protection, risk, and trust in their clinical decision-making. It highlights the gap between formal legal requirements and the realities of work with LGBTQ+ youth in sensitive clinical contexts.\nUltimately, the study raises a broader question for child protection systems: how can professionals uphold safeguarding duties without undermining the trust that makes disclosure possible in the first place? The study also hopes to contribute to ongoing thinking about mandatory reporting frameworks, and whether a more flexible, context-sensitive approach could better balance protection with clinical judgment in complex cases that require multi-layered responses rather than a strictly rule-based application of law.\n\n', 'Amitai Marmor', 'amarmor@staff.haifa.ac.il', 'Amitai Marmor (University of Haifa)*; Assaf Vital (Clalit Health Services ); Hadas Bloemendal (Hebrew University of Jerusalem)', 'Marmor, Amitai*; Vital, Assaf; Bloemendal, Hadas', 'amarmor@staff.haifa.ac.il*; VitalAsaf@gmail.com; hadas.bloemendal@mail.huji.ac.il', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Dr Assaf Vital- Resident physician. ֿVitalAsaf@gmail.com\n\nHadas Bloemendal- Ph.D candidate, The Hebrew University of Jerusalem- hadas.bloemendal@mail.huji.ac.il', 'Paper Presentation'),
('120', 'typteng@g2.usc.edu.tw', '124', '5/27/2026 6:51:29 AM +00:00', '5/27/2026 6:51:29 AM +00:00', 'Exploring the natural science integrates socio-emotional learning interventional program promote children\'s social-emotional competence', 'Stable socio-emotional development in young children lays the foundation for their mental health. This study explores the impact of intervening natural science activities with socio-emotional learning (SEL) on young children\'s socio-emotional competences and natural science concepts.\nResearch Objectives:\n1.	To design and implement a curriculum that integrates socio-emotional learning concepts into natural science activities.\n2.	To compare the differences in socio-emotional competence and natural science concepts before and after children\'s participation in natural science activities.\nMethodology, Subjects, and Instruments: This study used a quasi-experimental research design, with the theme of \"water\" in natural science as the core curriculum. The subjects were preschool children from two childcare centers in Taipei City, one serving as the experimental group and the other as the control group. The study conducted a comprehensive analysis using qualitative and quantitative data: qualitative data included the coding analysis of children\'s learning outcomes and transcripts of audio-visual teaching records. The statistical analysis including: descriptive, paired samples t-tests, independent samples t-tests, and non-parametric tests by using SPSS.\nResults: This study designed and implemented 20 lessons that integrated natural science activities with socio-emotional learning concepts, each lasting 30-40 minutes. Quantitative results showed that there was no significant difference in the control group, while the experimental group showed significant improvements in socio-emotional skills and water science cognitive abilities after the lessons.\nConclusion and Suggestions: This study shows that integrating SEL into natural science activities can simultaneously promote children\'s water science concepts and socio-emotional development, and can serve as a reference for the future promotion of SEL as the base of children’s mental health.\n\nKey words: children’s mental health, social-emotional learning, natural science interventional program\n\n', 'Teresa Teng', 'typteng@g2.usc.edu.tw', 'Teresa Teng (Shih Chien University)*', 'Teng, Teresa*', 'typteng@g2.usc.edu.tw*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '2026055-abstract-India-ICSWHMH.docx (16,817 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', '●	First author: Teresa Y. P. Teng- Professor, Department of Family Studies and Child Development, Shih Chien University, Taipei, Taiwan\n●	Co-authors: Jo-Hsin Lai, Hsiao-Yuan Wang, Ting-I Lin, Xun-Ling Fang-Senior students of Department of Family Studies and Child Development, Shih Chien University, Taipei, Taiwan\n●	Corresponding author: Chen, B.C.- Assistant Professor,\nDepartment of Child Care and Education, Chang Gung University of Science and Technology, Taoyuan, Taiwan\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('121', 'mirikp@ariel.ac.il', '125', '5/27/2026 10:50:46 AM +00:00', '5/27/2026 10:50:46 AM +00:00', 'When childbirth occurs under displacement: maternal well-being during prolonged war', 'Objectives: Childbirth under conditions of displacement and prolonged war-related stress presents a unique psychological challenge. Women giving birth under prolonged crisis conditions may simultaneously cope with disrupted safety, repeated displacement, ongoing uncertainty, and concerns for their families and communities. However, the psychological mechanisms linking perceived childbirth risk to maternal well-being under such conditions remain insufficiently understood. This study explored the roles of self-compassion and intolerance of uncertainty in understanding maternal psychological well-being among women exposed to prolonged war-related stress.\nDesign: A retrospective cross-sectional study was conducted among women who gave birth while temporarily displaced under prolonged war-related conditions in Israel.\nModel: Ninety-six women completed validated measures of perceived childbirth risk, self-compassion, intolerance of uncertainty, and psychological well-being. A moderated mediation model (PROCESS Model 7) was tested while controlling for maternal age, income, and objective obstetric risk.\nResult: Higher perceived childbirth risk was associated with lower self-compassion, which in turn was associated with lower psychological well-being. This indirect pathway was significant primarily among women with high intolerance of uncertainty levels. Among women with low or moderate intolerance of uncertainty, perceived childbirth risk was not significantly associated with psychological well-being, suggesting greater resilience under prolonged stress conditions. \nConclusion: Self-compassion emerged as an important internal resource associated with maternal well-being under prolonged stress, whereas intolerance of uncertainty appeared to heighten vulnerability under conditions of ongoing uncertainty and displacement. The findings highlight the importance of trauma-informed and compassion-oriented support for women giving birth during prolonged crisis conditions. Early identification of women characterized by high intolerance of uncertainty may help clinicians and social workers identify mothers who could be at elevated psychological risk during periods of prolonged adversity.\n', 'Miri Kestler Peleg', 'mirikp@ariel.ac.il', 'Miri Kestler Peleg (School of Social Work, Ariel University)*', 'Kestler Peleg, Miri*', 'mirikp@ariel.ac.il*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ABSTRACT.docx (15,299 bytes); title page.docx (21,695 bytes)', '2', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Miri Kestler-Peleg, Senior Lecturer, School of Social Work, Ariel University, Israel, mirikp@ariel.ac.il.\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0002-0006-4448\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('124', 'karendurham2@gmail.com', '128', '5/27/2026 4:13:20 PM +00:00', '5/27/2026 4:13:20 PM +00:00', 'Integrating Perinatal Mental Health into Master’s-Level Social Work Education: Preparing Practitioners for Equitable, Collaborative, and Culturally Responsive Care', 'Perinatal mental health is a critical yet often underemphasized area of health and mental health social work education. While social workers frequently encounter pregnant, postpartum, and parenting individuals across clinical, medical, community, child welfare, substance use, and policy settings, many graduate-level training programs provide limited structured preparation for recognizing and responding to perinatal mental health concerns. This gap has important implications for health equity, access to care, early intervention, and the development of a workforce equipped to address the complex intersections of mental health, family systems, reproductive health, trauma, culture, and social determinants of health.\n\nThis presentation argues that perinatal mental health education should be intentionally integrated into master’s-level social work curricula as part of broader preparation for health and mental health practice. Framed within the conference theme of managing change and embracing diversity, the presentation will examine how perinatal mental health education aligns with several urgent priorities in social work: advancing health equity, strengthening population mental health, supporting trauma-informed and culturally responsive care, preparing students for interdisciplinary collaboration, and building workforce capacity for emerging practice needs.\n\nThe presentation will offer a curriculum-informed model for embedding perinatal mental health content into existing master’s-level social work training rather than treating it as a narrow specialty topic. Key areas of focus include perinatal mood and anxiety disorders, postpartum rage and emotional dysregulation, reproductive grief and loss, birth trauma, substance use, intimate partner violence, infant and family well-being, stigma and shame, and culturally responsive engagement with diverse families. Attention will also be given to the ways structural inequities, racism, poverty, rurality, migration, disability, and access barriers shape perinatal mental health risk, help-seeking, diagnosis, and treatment experiences.\n\nThe presentation will translate this content into practical teaching strategies for social work educators and field instructors. Examples may include case-based learning, simulated interprofessional consultation, screening and referral exercises, policy and systems mapping, reflective practice activities, and assignments that help students connect micro, mezzo, and macro dimensions of perinatal mental health. Rather than positioning perinatal mental health as only relevant to maternal health specialists, this presentation will demonstrate its relevance across social work roles in hospitals, community mental health, schools, child and family services, behavioral health, public health, integrated care, and policy advocacy.\n\nPractice and educational implications include the need for stronger competency development in perinatal mental health assessment, culturally responsive engagement, interdisciplinary referral pathways, ethical decision-making, trauma-informed intervention, and advocacy for inclusive systems of care. By preparing future social workers to recognize and respond to perinatal mental health needs, master’s-level programs can contribute to earlier identification, reduced stigma, stronger family support, and more equitable access to care. This presentation ultimately positions perinatal mental health education as a vital component of future-facing social work training and a meaningful pathway for strengthening health and mental health systems across diverse global contexts.', 'Karen Durham', 'karendurham2@gmail.com', 'Karen Durham (University of Kentucky)*', 'Durham, Karen*', 'karendurham2@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Education, Training, and Future Directions for Social Work', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', 'Miriam Schiff (Hebrew University Of Jerusalem)', 'miriam.schiff@mail.huji.ac.il', '', '', '', '', 'None', 'Paper Presentation'),
('126', 'jennifer.harrison@wmich.edu', '130', '5/27/2026 11:43:28 PM +00:00', '5/27/2026 11:43:28 PM +00:00', 'Enhancing Motivation for Adolescents and Adults in Mental Health Settings', 'Practitioners may know the basics of motivation and may have incorporated it into their professional work for many years.  This session will review basic skills of motivational interviewing and quickly move on to applications in planning your next contact with an individual or family to assist them in advancing their change process and achieve their goals.  Case studies will be used to enhance the relevancy of your work in change processes.', 'Jennifer Harrison', 'jennifer.harrison@wmich.edu', 'Jennifer Harrison (Western Michigan University College of Health and Human Services)*', 'Harrison, Jennifer*', 'jennifer.harrison@wmich.edu*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Professional Practice, Interventions, and Models of Care', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Enhancing Motivation for Adolescents and Adults - IASSW 012027.pdf (1,347,244 bytes); Motivation Interviewing Professional Development for Plainwell Community Schools.docx (15,988 bytes)', '2', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'None', 'Paper Presentation'),
('127', 'mm525@st-andrews.ac.uk', '131', '5/28/2026 11:29:27 PM +00:00', '5/28/2026 11:29:27 PM +00:00', 'More Lonely Abroad? Loneliness Among Mid-life and Older Indian Immigrants in the UK Compared to Host and Origin Populations', 'Objectives: Loneliness is a major global public health concern, yet comparative evidence remains limited on how immigrants’ loneliness relates both to host-country populations and to non-migrants in countries of origin. This study compares loneliness among mid-life and older Indian immigrants in the UK with White British adults in the UK and Indians residing in India. This study examines (a) differences in loneliness prevalence across these three populations and (b) whether key determinants of loneliness operate differently across migration and national contexts.\n\nDesign: This study uses harmonised, nationally representative data from UK Household Longitudinal Study (UKHLS, Wave 9) and Longitudinal Ageing Study in India (LASI, Wave 1), including 82,231 individuals aged 45+. \n\nModel: Loneliness prevalence was compared across the three groups using pooled binary logistic regression models estimated on the combined LASI–UKHLS dataset. To assess contextual variation in correlates, we also estimated stratified logistic regression models for each group with the same predictors. Results are presented as average marginal effects (AMEs).\n\nResults: Despite relatively higher socioeconomic status, Indian immigrants in the UK reported the highest loneliness (45%), significantly more than White British (32%) and Indian-residing adults (33%). These differences persist after controlling for key demographic, socioeconomic, health, and social factors. Stratified analyses showed marked heterogeneity in loneliness vulnerability profiles. Poor self-rated health was the only consistent predictor across all groups. Frequent participation in social organisations was associated with lower loneliness only among White British adults. Conventional predictors, including old age, sex, education attainment, income, partnership, living arrangement, social organisation and religious participation, were not significantly associated with loneliness among Indian immigrants in the UK.\n\nConclusion: These findings suggest that loneliness in later life is deeply embedded in cultural norms, institutional contexts, and migration experiences. The study underscores the need for context-sensitive and cross-cultural approaches to understanding and addressing loneliness among ageing populations in increasingly multicultural societies.\n', 'Mengxing Joshi', 'mm525@st-andrews.ac.uk', 'Mengxing Joshi (International Institute for Population Sciences)*', 'Joshi, Mengxing*', 'mm525@st-andrews.ac.uk*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'NA', 'Paper Presentation'),
('128', 'sks.sharma.iitr@gmail.com', '132', '5/29/2026 3:44:45 AM +00:00', '5/29/2026 3:44:45 AM +00:00', 'Stress Resiliency as a Predictor of Cardiovascular Symptoms among Management Students ', 'In the age of artificial intelligence (AI), it is a perception among the management students that prospective job or employment market will shrink and therefore, these students are under high stress. Though stress is apart and parcel of human life especially during days of contemporary management education context, it is the human capability to bounce back and move in life further. However, many students do not have sufficient resiliency and as a consequence they develop early symptoms of cardiovascular diseases. In this context, the present research proposal will be an attempt to develop some AI model which can predict cardiovascular symptoms among management students through stress resiliency score obtained through questionnaire-based survey. Data will be collected through questionnaire-based survey among 250 management students of Kerala with the classification based on gender (male and female). Based on data analysis through appropriate statistical techniques, one AI product is expected to be developed and delivered in colleges and universities of India for external validation. If successful, it may help the students to take some preventive measures to boost their cardiovascular health. \n\nKeywords: Artificial Intelligence, Management Students, Stress Resiliency, Cardiovascular Disease Symptoms, India\n', 'Santosh Sharma', 'sks.sharma.iitr@gmail.com', 'Santosh Sharma (Rajagiri Business School)*', 'Sharma, Santosh*', 'sks.sharma.iitr@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'RP-AI.docx (13,608 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Satyansh Sharma\nVeeam (Bengaluru, India)\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('129', 'phildien88@gmail.com', '133', '5/29/2026 7:54:19 AM +00:00', '5/29/2026 7:54:19 AM +00:00', 'Invisible threat: Exploring the psychosocial impacts of asbestos exposure in DIY home renovators in Australia', 'Background: Despite Australia\'s 2004 national ban, legacy residential asbestos remains a persistent, non-occupational public health hazard. Existing global scholarship heavily favours epidemiological projections or post-diagnostic clinical data. As a result, complex health systems routinely overlook the acute, pre-diagnostic health and mental health burdens carried by do-it-yourself (DIY) home renovators. This creates a critical structural blind spot where environmental safety meets psychological wellbeing. Addressing it requires urgent social work intervention and cross-national learning.\nMethods: This study examines the lived experiences, mental health impacts, and structural determinants that shape awareness of asbestos exposure. It is grounded in Anti-Oppressive Practice and epistemic injustice theory. Using Critical Narrative Inquiry (CNI), the study analyses in-depth, semi-structured interviews with twelve Australian DIY renovators. These insights were examined through a localised, community-based lens. The analysis spanned five dimensions, including Positioning and Voice, to assess how citizens navigate fragmented health and regulatory systems.\nFindings: Driven by a commitment to social justice, the study exposes a pattern of structurally produced harm, termed Systemic Abandonment. Under this pattern, complex health and policy systems ration support conditionally. Care is activated only after an individual crosses into clinical disease. Critical reflection on this injustice reveals five mechanisms that threaten health equity and community safety. First, Retrospective Dread appears as chronic, unmanaged health anxiety sustained across decades of biological latency. Second, the Compliance Trap exposes a stark social justice failure, in which prohibitive market costs force unsafe practices despite accurate safety knowledge. Third, the Safety Class Divide reveals a wealth-gated distribution of preventative health measures. Fourth, the Information–Action Gap reflects the failure of institutional pathways to deliver supported compliance. Finally, Tactile Literacy highlights resilient, community-based practices that everyday citizens develop to protect themselves in the absence of institutional care. In addition, an entirely male sample points to a gendered structure within support networks built around outdated industrial health models.\nImplications: This research offers important cross-national learning for global public health systems managing post-industrial toxic legacies. The findings call for a radical social change agenda. To advance health equity and social justice, social work practitioners and policymakers must lead a shift from Static Information to Active Facilitation. This reform means building responsive, community-based practices. In particular, frameworks should include subsidised toxicity testing, co-funded material removal, and accessible waste infrastructure. Importantly, genuine systemic reform requires universally reclassifying pre-diagnostic mental health and psychosocial support within complex healthcare frameworks.\n', 'Phillip Dien', 'phildien88@gmail.com', 'Phillip Dien (University of Sunshine Coast)*; Vinathe  Sharma-Brymer (University of Sunshine Coast )', 'Dien, Phillip*; Sharma-Brymer, Vinathe ', 'phildien88@gmail.com*; vsharmabrymer@usc.edu.au', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ABSTRACT for conference.docx (30,802 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Name - Dr Vinathe Sharma-Brymer \nPosition - Senior Lecturer in Social Work and Program Coordinator for Bachelor of Human Services\nEmail - vsharmabrymer@usc.edu.au\nName - Dr Tina Lathouras \nPosition - Senior Lecturer in Social Work and Program Coordinator for Master of Social Work\nEmail - TLathouras@usc.edu.au', 'Paper Presentation'),
('130', '1538808328@qq.com', '134', '5/29/2026 9:28:40 AM +00:00', '5/29/2026 9:28:40 AM +00:00', 'Digital Technology or Task-Shifting? Comparing Two Pathways for Social Work in Older Adults‘ Mental Health in China and India， with Global Implications', 'China and India—the world‘s two most populous developing countries—are simultaneously confronting the twin challenges of unprecedented population aging and severe shortages of mental health resources. China has over 220 million people aged 65 and above, with depressive symptoms detected in 26.4% of older adults. India has approximately 150 million people aged 60 and above, with late-life depression prevalence estimated at 20%-30%. Both countries suffer from acute shortages of psychiatrists (China: 6.93 per 100,000 population; India: 2-3 per 100,000), and conventional social work systems remain grossly insufficient to meet the growing demand. Facing this common predicament, China and India have pursued two sharply contrasting pathways shaped by their distinct national contexts, technological endowments, and governance logics.\n\nThis paper adopts a comparative scoping review and policy analysis methodology to systematically examine and contrast national strategies and core practice models for social work in older adults’ mental health in China and India. Three key findings emerge:\n\n(1) The China pathway: digital technology-driven. Leveraging the world‘s largest internet user base and advanced digital infrastructure, China has positioned digital interventions as a national strategic tool. The 14th Five-Year Plan for Healthy Aging explicitly promotes “Internet + mental health”; multiple cities have launched smart social psychological service cloud platforms. A major national R&D project on “Intelligent Technologies for Mental Health Adjustment and Comfort of Older Adults,” led by the Institute of Psychology of the Chinese Academy of Sciences, is building an integrated “psychology + AI” assessment and intervention system. Advantages include high scalability and data-driven precision; disadvantages include the digital literacy gap and older adults’ mistrust of technology.\n\n(2) The India pathway: task-shifting-driven. Drawing on the WHO‘s “lay health worker” framework and Kerala’s long experience in community health, India has vigorously promoted task-shifting models involving non-professional volunteers and community workers. By training teachers, village health workers, and elderly self-help groups to provide basic psychological support and referral services, India has achieved community coverage at extremely low cost. Advantages include deep cultural embeddedness and reach to the most marginalized populations; disadvantages include variable service quality and weak professional supervision chains.\n\n(3) Both pathways face common challenges: sustainable financing mechanisms, standardized outcome evaluation, and adequate responses to stigma and structural violence.\n\nThis paper argues that the Chinese and Indian pathways are not mutually exclusive but rather deeply complementary. China could incorporate India’s community-based task-shifting mechanisms to reach populations left behind by the digital divide; India could draw on China‘s digital experience to enhance the efficiency and standardization of large-scale interventions. This “South-South” bidirectional learning framework offers a transferable strategic toolkit for other low- and middle-income countries confronting the dual crises of aging and mental health, while also contributing Eastern experiential knowledge to postcolonial theories of mental health social work.\n\nKeywords: Older Adults Mental Health; Digital Interventions; Task-Shifting; China-India Comparison; Community-Based Social Work', 'Yi Luo', '1538808328@qq.com', 'Yi Luo (GUANGDONG UNIVERSITY OF FOREIGN STUDIES )*', 'Luo, Yi*', '1538808328@qq.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Yi Luo', 'Paper Presentation'),
('131', 'jane.e.hamilton@uth.tmc.edu', '135', '5/29/2026 4:50:27 PM +00:00', '5/29/2026 4:50:27 PM +00:00', 'Bridging Hospital and Housing: A Mixed-Methods Evaluation of Psychiatric Respite for People Experiencing Homelessness with Serious Mental Illness', 'Research Objective:\nIn the United States, people experiencing homelessness experience disproportionately high rates of serious mental illness, co-occurring substance use disorders, and chronic medical conditions compared with the general population. Timely access to community-based behavioral health services, primary care, and permanent housing following psychiatric hospitalization is critical to improving outcomes and addressing structural determinants of health. This study evaluated whether an innovative post-psychiatric hospitalization respite program implemented in Houston, Texas, USA, improved access to permanent housing and reduced psychiatric readmissions, psychiatric emergency department visits, and criminal justice system involvement among adults experiencing homelessness. Implementation barriers and facilitators were also examined.\n\nStudy Design:\nA mixed-methods design was used. Quantitative analyses drew on merged electronic health record data from an academic safety-net psychiatric hospital and the local mental health authority in Houston, Texas, USA, including psychiatric hospitalizations, psychiatric emergency department visits, and jail bookings. Logistic regression analyses examined patient-level predictors of program completion and housing placement, with predictors selected based on significant group differences. Pre-post utilization and psychiatric hospitalization costs during the year following respite participation were compared between participants who obtained permanent housing and those who exited the program before housing placement. Implementation data were collected through two web-based Qualtrics surveys administered to psychiatric hospital and respite providers, as well as thirteen follow-up key informant interviews with providers and patients.\n\nPopulation Studied:\nThe study population included adults experiencing homelessness with serious mental illness (n=363) who were admitted to the Hospital to Home psychiatric respite program between April 28, 2020, and May 23, 2023, following discharge from an academic safety-net psychiatric hospital in Houston, Texas, USA, that serves approximately 10,000 patients annually.\n\nPrincipal Findings:\nOne-third of participants (n=111) completed the program and were placed into permanent housing, including permanent supportive housing (n=28) or rapid rehousing (n=83) through the local U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) Continuum of Care system. Among participants linked to permanent housing, there was a 78.8% reduction in the number of individuals hospitalized and a 74.2% reduction in total psychiatric hospitalizations within one year compared with participants who exited the program before housing placement (both p<0.001). Significant group differences were also observed in psychiatric emergency department visits (p=0.010) and jail bookings (p=0.002). Logistic regression analyses indicated that being unmarried and participation in cognitive behavioral therapy delivered by master’s-level social workers were associated with program completion and housing placement. Total psychiatric hospitalization costs during the year following respite participation were substantially lower among participants placed in permanent housing ($367,400) compared with those who were not housed ($1,610,653; p<0.001). Provider surveys identified strong community support for the program but highlighted limited respite bed capacity, particularly for women, as a key implementation barrier. Interviews emphasized the program’s recovery-oriented, trauma-informed approach, while prolonged housing wait times contributed to premature program exit.\n\nConclusions:\nPsychiatric respite is a promising transitional care model in the United States for supporting safe discharge, continuity of care, and access to permanent housing among people experiencing homelessness with serious mental illness. Further research is needed to assess long-term outcomes and scalability across U.S. health systems and communities.\n\nImplications for Policy or Practice:\nAs homelessness continues to increase across the United States, psychiatric respite programs represent an evidence-informed strategy for improving outcomes among a highly vulnerable population. These findings have implications for hospital discharge planning, cross-sector community partnerships, and policy investments that align U.S. health systems with housing and behavioral health solutions.', 'Jane Hamilton', 'jane.e.hamilton@uth.tmc.edu', 'Jane Hamilton (University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston)*', 'Hamilton, Jane*', 'jane.e.hamilton@uth.tmc.edu*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Final.MixedMethods.PsychiatricRespiteManuscript.2.12.2026.pdf (533,940 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', '(1) Jane Hamilton, PhD, MPH, LCSW-S, Associate Professor of Psychiatry, University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston, McGovern Medical School, Houston, Texas, USA, Jane.E.Hamilton@uth.tmc.edu\n(2) Bruno Kluwe-Schiavon, PhD, MPH, LCSW, Research Instructor, University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston, McGovern Medical School.Houston, Texas, USA. and and Assistant Professor at Postgraduate Program in Health Sciences, Universidade do Extremo Sul Catarinense, Criciúma, Brazil. Bruno.KluweSchiavon@uth.tmc.edu ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('133', 'devbhupa@gmail.com', '137', '5/30/2026 7:00:31 AM +00:00', '5/30/2026 7:00:31 AM +00:00', 'Socio-Economic Status and Wellbeing of the Adolescents in Lunglei District, Mizoram', 'Socio-Economic Status (SES) is a measure of an individual\'s, family\'s, and other sources of income, which predominantly determines an individual\'s position relative to others in society. It is often understood that low socio-economic status is associated with lower levels of education, health, and living conditions, which are the main indicators of SES. The present study is descriptive in design and cross-sectional. A mixed-methods approach is used to collect data through field surveys, focus group discussions, case studies, and the Participatory Rural Appraisal technique. Samples are selected from core and peripheral areas using a stratified disproportionate sampling technique. The quantitative findings revealed that the socio-economic condition of adolescents is favourable and, in terms of overall wellbeing, is good; however, future security and community belonging are comparatively weaker domains. With this backdrop, the present study examines socio-economic characteristics to assess wellbeing, examines the relationship between wellbeing and SES, and suggests policy measures for social work practice. The qualitative findings revealed that familial and peer interactions, as well as religious factors, played a supportive role in adolescents’ wellbeing. Besides that, there is an insignificant relationship between SES and wellbeing due to other influencing factors in the study samples.  In conclusion, the present study suggests policy interventions based on its findings to strengthen adolescents’ future security and community connectedness through school-based support services, career guidance, and youth participation programmes to improve their well-being.', 'C Devendiran', 'devbhupa@gmail.com', 'C Devendiran (Mizoram University)*', 'Devendiran, C*', 'devbhupa@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Prof.C.Devendiran, Prof. A. Muthulakshmi & Ruthi Lalnunthari.pdf (87,795 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'A. Muthulakshmi, Professor, Mizoram University, Aizawl-796 004 and muthumzu@gmail.com; Ruthi Lalnunthari, Former M.Phil., Mizoram University, Aizawl - 796 004, and ruthihniarcheng@gmail.com ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('134', 'rishitvinu21363@gmail.com', '139', '5/30/2026 6:21:37 PM +00:00', '5/30/2026 6:21:37 PM +00:00', 'The Politics of Inclusion: Migrant Mental Health Equity in Kerala', '     The research examines the divide between the formal policy and \"lived accessibility\" for 3.4 million interstate migrants in Kerala. It reviews the operationalisation of the Mental Healthcare Act (2017) and evaluates the equity in mental healthcare availability. Furthermore, it investigates the persistent systemic exclusion of migrant workers. It relies on qualitative meta-data analysis by observing the PRISMA 2020 principles and systematically analyses the policy reports and peer-reviewed publications between 2015 and 2025. It applies reflexive thematic coding and juxtaposes statutory provisions with ground-level outcomes. It utilises the Social Determinants of Health (SDH) and social justice lens. It appraises the institutional constraints, which includes unstable housing and linguistic obstacles, in producing \"distributive harm\" and inequitable healthcare access. It is initially found that Kerala’s programme ambitions surpass delivery capability. As a result, reported mental disorders grew from 272 to 400 per 100,000 between 2002 and 2018, while treatment availability continued to be insufficient with only 5.53 beds per 10,000 population. The initial findings recognise \"exclusion by design\", where 47.9% of workers endure psychological strain, but are left out as \"clinically distant\". This is due to various significant challenges, which could include language disparities and deficiency of healthcare mobility. Measures like \"Garima\" facilitate field-based assistance, but they stay sporadic and emergency-driven with inadequate institutionalisation. Therefore, legislation advances equity on paper, but it is not guaranteed in reality. Amidst this, true inclusion calls for \"responsive design\", which could embed transferable healthcare, linguistically friendly systems, specialised grievance channels, and oversight frameworks, for the labourers.\nKeywords: Migrant Mental Health Equity, Mental Healthcare Act 2017, Social Determinants of Health, Exclusion by Design, Kerala Migrant Welfare.', 'Rishit Vinu', 'rishitvinu21363@gmail.com', 'Rishit Vinu (Department of Political Science, St.John\'s College, Anchal, Kollam, Kerala)*', 'Vinu, Rishit*', 'rishitvinu21363@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract by Rishit Vinu - The Politics of Inclusion_ Migrant Mental Health Equity in Kerala.docx (8,851 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Rishit Vinu, Undergraduate Student (3rd Year), Department of Political Science, St. John’s College, Anchal, Kollam, Kerala (Affiliated to University of Kerala) | E-Mail: rishitvinu21363@gmail.com', 'Student Presentation Track: Seed Ideas'),
('135', 'efrat.luski@mail.huji.ac.il', '140', '5/30/2026 8:39:57 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 7:42:02 AM +00:00', 'Co-Designing National Workforce Policy in Health Social Work: Strengthening Professional Identity through Accreditation Pathway Development', '*Background and Objectives*\nHealth social work operates at the intersection of increasingly complex healthcare needs, widening health inequalities, and growing pressures on health systems (Browne, 2019; Craig & Muskat, 2013). Population ageing and workforce shortages have intensified concerns about service capacity (Khan et al., 2024). Despite expanding clinical, psychosocial, leadership, and care coordination responsibilities, there is limited evidence regarding professional development initiatives designed to address the evolving healthcare challenges and support the expansion of advanced social work roles (Xenakis, 2018). This study examines a national initiative led by the Israeli Ministry of Health to develop tiered accreditation and specialization pathways in health social work using a Policy Co-Design approach. The study addressed the following questions: (1) How does professional specialization in health social work emerge as a response to increasing health system complexity? (2) How can Policy Co-Design approach (Walker et al., 2023) contribute to the development, legitimacy, and implementation of specialization and accreditation frameworks in health social work?\n*Methods*\nThe initiative was led by the National Health Social Work Service within the Israeli Ministry of Health and involved six national core teams: one foundational accreditation team and five specialization teams focusing on child development and pediatric neurology, rehabilitation, mental health, primary care, and palliative care, pain, loss and bereavement.\nThe Policy Co-Design process followed three stages: (1) mapping practice realities and system challenges; (2) identifying gaps in knowledge, competencies, role boundaries, and professional authority; and (3) translating findings into accreditation frameworks, competency models, educational pathways, and authority structures.\nData sources included workshop transcripts, structured outputs, policy documents, expert consultations, and reflective field notes. Analysis combined inductive thematic analysis with deductive coding informed by the study objectives and Policy Co-Design stages.\n*Findings*\nDespite substantial differences across specialization domains, a consistent pattern of professional specialization emerged across all core teams. This led to the development of a cross-specialty model for professional specialization in health social work.\nParticipants described specialization as a response to growing health system complexity rather than as an isolated professional aspiration. Across all domains, discussions began with increasing case complexity, workforce shortages, service fragmentation, growing psychosocial needs, continuity-of-care challenges, and inequities in service access.\nAnalysis identified five interconnected layers of specialization: (1) recognition of increasing clinical and system complexity; (2) construction of a distinct professional identity; (3) definition of advanced competencies; (4) expansion of professional authority and regulatory recognition; and (5) development of structured accreditation and specialization pathways.\nParticipants described a shift from viewing health social work as a generalist profession focused primarily on support, mediation, and assistance. Instead, they increasingly perceived it as a distinct clinical-systemic specialty that requires advanced knowledge, practice-based competencies, and the ability to integrate the broader biopsychosocial needs of patients and families.\nCommon themes across domains included expanding responsibilities without corresponding authority, role ambiguity, and advanced expertise lacking formal recognition. Participants further described advanced practice as requiring integrated clinical, systemic, ethical, educational, and leadership competencies that often develop through practice rather than traditional academic education.\nThe Policy Co-Design process itself emerged as a significant finding. Bringing together frontline practitioners, academic experts, and policy leaders created a unique national space for reflection, collective knowledge production, and professional identity formation. Participants viewed the process as enhancing legitimacy, ownership, and engagement in professional change.\n*Conclusion*\nThe findings suggest that professional specialization in health social work emerges as a response to increasing health system complexity, creating demands for new professional identities, advanced competencies, expanded authority, and formal specialization pathways. The study proposes a cross-specialty model describing how the profession responds through identity formation, competency development, authority expansion, and professional regulation. Beyond advancing the profession, the model has the potential to strengthen workforce sustainability and service capacity, while offering a transferable framework for developing advanced practice pathways across health professions.\n', 'Efrat Lusky', 'efrat.luski@mail.huji.ac.il', 'Yael Goor Kol (Ministry of Health & Ben-Gurion University of the Negev; Israel); Efrat Lusky (the Hebrew University)*', 'Goor Kol, Yael; Lusky, Efrat*', 'yael.goor@moh.gov.il; efrat.luski@mail.huji.ac.il*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Workforce Development, Wellbeing, and Leadership', '', '2', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract.docx (23,249 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Yael Goor Kol, Ph.D., is Head of the Israeli National Social Work Service at the Ministry of Health and a Teaching Fellow at the School of Social Work, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev.\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('136', 'dtener@gmail.com', '141', '5/31/2026 6:49:08 AM +00:00', '5/31/2026 6:49:08 AM +00:00', 'Title: “How Are They Going to Carry This?”: Adolescents and Children as Recipients of Peer Sexual Abuse Disclosures', 'Background\nChildren and adolescents are often the first to hear their peers’ disclosures of sexual abuse, yet their experiences as recipients remain largely absent from professional and institutional responses. While services are typically oriented toward the survivor, peer recipients frequently describe carrying emotional, relational, and sometimes legal weight in the absence of structured recognition or support. Although existing literature on indirect or third-party disclosures has pointed to procedural uncertainty, it has not sufficiently attended to the experiences or the role of peer recipients within the disclosure process.\nObjective\nThis study aims to examine how professionals conceptualize child and adolescent recipients of peer disclosures—including perceived characteristics, assigned roles, and perceived impacts on recipients’ lives and their relationships with survivors—and how these conceptualizations inform case management decisions, institutional priorities, professional responses, and intervention practices.\nMethods\nQualitative study using six focus groups (40 participants) with multidisciplinary professionals in child advocacy center (CAC) and related systems: law enforcement/police, social workers, forensic interviewers, victim advocates, mental health providers, and educators. Discussions explored meaning-making, decision-making, and priorities following peer disclosures, including assessment, support, and follow-up for disclosure recipients.\nFindings\nEven when asked to place disclosure recipients at the center of the discussion, professionals often repositioned them at the margins, primarily in relation to the survivor. Participants described perceived personal characteristics such as loyalty, sensitivity, maturity, and social centrality that may render certain individuals more likely to be chosen as recipients of disclosures. Recipients were perceived as assuming multiple roles, including acting as secret keepers, providing emotional support to the victim, serving as witnesses to the disclosure, and managing peer-group dynamics. Professionals perceived consequences to the recipients included secondary and vicarious trauma, emotional strain, relational tension, and involvement in lengthy legal procedures without preparation. Support for recipients appeared to be inconsistent and variably applied. Caregivers and professionals did not always acknowledge recipients’ support needs, and guidance regarding appropriate forms of support was often limited. Educators frequently served as the primary point of contact, with responses predominantly oriented toward the victim, thereby rendering recipients’ experiences less visible.\nImplications \nFindings indicate a gap in trauma-informed responses: disclosure recipients are rarely systematically assessed, supported, or followed up. Clearer protocols and training are needed to recognize and respond to their specific support needs and reduce secondary trauma risk.\n\n', 'DAFNA TENER', 'dtener@gmail.com', 'DAFNA TENER (The Hebrew University of Jerusalem)*', 'TENER, DAFNA*', 'dtener@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '1', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Suha Daw, The Hebrew University of Jerusalem\ndawsuha@gmail.com \n\nAbbie Newman\nMission Kids Child Advocacy Center\nanewman@missionkidscac.org ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('138', 'vaishnavishelke648@gmail.com', '143', '5/31/2026 9:37:36 AM +00:00', '5/31/2026 9:43:12 AM +00:00', '\'\'Disease That Becomes a Disability\'\': Ethnographic Exploration of Long COVID Discourse in India', 'India, the country with the highest population, previously reported high infection and mortality during the global outbreak of the COVID-19 pandemic, and has left millions of people unattended who are living with post-infection complex chronic conditions, such as Long COVID and associated health conditions. Even though many are suffering from legitimate chronic conditions and debilities, people with evidence-based morbidity are generally prioritised, while those who fail to get a proper diagnosis are left to negotiate their way on their own, choosing between the currently overwhelmed public healthcare system, private care, or other pluralistic healing approaches. Due to inadequate research and ignorance by public healthcare agencies, the existing guidelines, research, and reports are proving inefficient in translating care into the practices of medical practitioners, increasing the burden on the healthcare system without assuring recovery. While a sizeable proportion of the population is trying to increase visibility through media reporting, digital activism, and individual advocacy, the deep-rooted epistemic inequality in knowledge production is creating a contested terrain of marginalisation and subversion. Using ethnographic approaches of following discourse around the people living with chronic illnesses after COVID-19 infection, interviews with healthcare professionals, and advocacy channels situated for the Indian population with Long COVID, this study highlights how contested power creates knowledge, why the logic of biomedicine leaves people unattended after a failed diagnosis, and how patients and professionals negotiate care amidst uncertainty.', 'vaishnavi  Shelke', 'vaishnavishelke648@gmail.com', 'vaishnavi  Shelke (indian institute of technology hyderabad )*', 'Shelke, vaishnavi *', 'vaishnavishelke648@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Professional Practice, Interventions, and Models of Care', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Post COVID Chronicity Exploring Patient’s Narrative and Logics of Care Among COVID-19 Survivors in Delhi_ (1).pdf (7,524,893 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Dr Haripriya Narsimhan \nAssociate Professor\nLiberal Arts Department, Indian Institute of Technology, Hyderabad\nharipriya@la.iith.ac.in ', 'PhD Best Paper Award'),
('139', 'hagarch@edu.aac.ac.il', '144', '5/31/2026 12:31:58 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 12:51:47 PM +00:00', '“What will I be?!”: Experiences of Israeli Journalists During Wartime', 'Journalists covering traumatic events face significant psychological risks that extend beyond secondary traumatic stress. The outbreak of the Iron Swords war placed Israeli journalists within an unfolding national catastrophe, requiring them to uphold professional standards while simultaneously belonging to the affected collective and, in some cases, coping with personal loss. This study provides a qualitative examination of journalists’ coping processes, ethical challenges, and sources of resilience under conditions of collective trauma.\n\nDrawing on Gilligan’s Listening Guide, 24 in-depth interviews were conducted with Israeli journalists. Analysis identified two central themes: moral dilemmas, reflecting tensions between the obligation to inform and the desire to protect the public, and identity struggles, expressed through the recurring question, “What will I be?!”, as participants reconsidered their professional futures. Contrapuntal voices revealed ongoing negotiations between helplessness and civic responsibility, the normalization of violence, and efforts to maintain professional functioning while confronting repeated exposure to human suffering. These processes point to the profound psychological and relational consequences of reporting during prolonged crisis.\n\nThis study contributes to the literature on trauma, mental health, and occupational resilience by conceptualizing resilience not as emotional distancing, but as a dynamic and relational process emerging through continuous negotiation between professional commitments and lived experience. Findings demonstrate how journalists navigate institutional expectations, personal exposure, and collective belonging while preserving their capacity to work in highly demanding environments.\n\nFindings further illustrate how journalists renegotiate professional responsibilities across individual, organizational, and societal levels. While newsroom cultures may discourage help-seeking and public polarization intensifies emotional strain, peer support emerges as a critical protective resource that promotes occupational well-being and strengthens resilience. The study highlights the need to recognize journalists working in conflict settings as a vulnerable professional group and to develop trauma-informed psychosocial support systems comparable to those available for first responders. The findings have important implications for social work practice, occupational mental health services, and organizational interventions designed to support professionals routinely exposed to trauma and human suffering.\n', 'Hagar Binoun-Chaki', 'hagarch@edu.aac.ac.il', 'Hagar Binoun-Chaki (Ashkelon Academic Collge)*', 'Binoun-Chaki, Hagar*', 'hagarch@edu.aac.ac.il*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Table .pdf (134,111 bytes); What will I be.docx (19,873 bytes)', '2', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Hagar Binoun-Chaki | Faculty of Social Work, Ashkelon Academic College', 'Paper Presentation'),
('142', 'rayray@asia.edu.tw', '147', '5/31/2026 1:43:11 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 1:43:11 PM +00:00', 'Withdrawal of Life-Sustaining Treatment in Chinese Cultural Context: Family Decision-Making Patterns in a Regional Hospital in Taiwan', 'Objectives\nThis study examines the decision-making processes surrounding withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment (WLST) within the Chinese cultural context. Specifically, it explores how filial piety, familism, and death-related taboos influence family decision-making patterns, surrogate decision-maker selection, and interactions between healthcare professionals and family members in Taiwan\'s healthcare system.\n\nDesign\nA retrospective document analysis was conducted on 34 patients who underwent WLST in a regional hospital in central Taiwan between 2024 and 2025. Data sources included medical records and social work documentation. Thematic analysis was employed to identify patterns in decision-making processes, while cross-cultural comparison with international literature was used to highlight culturally distinctive features of end-of-life decision-making in Chinese societies.\n\nModel\nThe study adopted a cultural framework encompassing three dimensions: (1) familial collectivism, which emphasizes family-centered rather than individual-centered decision-making; (2) filial piety ethics, which shape the moral obligations and psychological burdens experienced by adult children acting as surrogate decision-makers; and (3) death-related taboos, which hinder discussions regarding advance care planning and end-of-life preferences. Data were analyzed according to decision-making timelines, consensus-building processes, and patterns of decision-maker selection.\n\nResults\nParticipants were predominantly older adults, with 64% aged over 70 years. At the time of decision-making, 85% were unable to communicate their treatment preferences. The median decision-making period was only two days. Notably, 79% of families reached internal consensus before formal family meetings with healthcare teams, reflecting a culturally embedded preference for preserving family harmony. Sons served as the primary legal signatories in 47% of cases, surpassing spouses (15%) even when spouses were available, illustrating the continuing influence of filial responsibility within Chinese family systems. Furthermore, none of the patients had completed an advance directive, suggesting that death-related taboos remain a significant barrier to advance care planning. Despite these challenges, 91% of family members reported satisfaction with healthcare communication, a figure substantially higher than dissatisfaction rates commonly reported in Western literature.\n\nConclusions\nEnd-of-life decision-making in Taiwan demonstrates culturally distinctive characteristics shaped by familial collectivism, filial obligations, and evolving intergenerational values. Healthcare professionals should adopt culturally responsive communication approaches that respect collective family decision-making while facilitating discussions about patients’ preferences and autonomy. Social workers play a critical role as cultural mediators, supporting families in navigating tensions between traditional cultural values and contemporary patient-centered care principles. These findings contribute to a broader understanding of cultural diversity in end-of-life care and provide practical implications for culturally sensitive palliative care policy and interdisciplinary healthcare practice in Asian contexts.', 'Shy Jui Yi', 'rayray@asia.edu.tw', 'Shy Jui Yi (Department of Social Work, Asia University)*; Cai  JingYi (Department of Social Work, Taichung Tzu Chi Hospital); Lin  Yichia  (Department of Social Work, Taichung Tzu Chi Hospital); SYU SIOU YU (Department of Social Work, Taichung Tzu Chi Hospital); Wu Wan Yu (Department of Social Work, Taichung Tzu Chi Hospital)', 'Jui Yi, Shy*; JingYi, Cai ; Yichia , Lin ; SIOU YU, SYU; Wan Yu, Wu', 'rayray@asia.edu.tw*; jingyi3701@gmail.com; yichia@tzuchi.com.tw; ling3419@hotmail.com; tcmail@tzuchi.com.tw', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Professional Practice, Interventions, and Models of Care', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '投稿11th International Conference on Social Work in Health and Mental Health.docx (15,989 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Corresponding Author\nJui Yi Shih, Assistant Professor, Department of Social Work, Asia University, Taiwan.\nEmail: rayray@asia.edu.tw\nCo-authors\nJing Yi Cai, Medical Social Worker, Department of Social Work, Taichung Tzu Chi Hospital, Taiwan. Email: jingyi3701@gmail.com\nYi-Chia Lin, Director, Department of Social Work, Taichung Tzu Chi Hospital, Taiwan. Email: yichia@tzuchi.com.tw\nHsiu-Yu Hsu, Medical Social Worker, Department of Social Work, Taichung Tzu Chi Hospital, Taiwan. Email: ling3419@hotmail.com\nWan-Yu Wu, Medical Social Worker, Department of Social Work, Taichung Tzu Chi Hospital, Taiwan. Email: tcmail@tzuchi.com.tw', 'Paper Presentation'),
('143', 'cjh79327@gmail.com', '148', '5/31/2026 2:44:09 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 2:44:09 PM +00:00', 'Exploring Factors Associated with Mental Health Literacy and Intervention Confidence among Medical Social Workers in Taiwanese General Hospital', 'To strengthen human rights protections, enhance community-based prevention, and improve the social safety net, Taiwan comprehensively amended the Mental Health Act of Taiwan on December 13, 2023. The amended legislation requires local authorities in each county and city to establish a 24-hour emergency psychiatric intervention consultation hotline by December 2024. Our hospital was commissioned by the local government to operate this hotline, with psychiatrists and social workers jointly rotating as consultation responders. During the implementation process, many hospital social workers reported insufficient mental health literacy and low confidence in serving as consultation contacts.\n\nThis event prompted reflection on the preparedness of medical social workers to respond to psychiatric-related situations. As a general medical center in central Taiwan, our hospital’s Department of Social Work oversees social workers across all clinical specialties. During routine clinical practice, social workers may encounter patients or family members with diagnosed or suspected mental illness. Therefore, this study aimed to explore medical social workers’ experiences and confidence in managing such situations in clinical settings.\n\nThis study employed purposive sampling to recruit participants from 16 non-supervisory social workers at our hospital, categorized according to years of professional experience in medical social work. Participation was voluntary, and a total of seven medical social workers were enrolled. Data were collected using focus group interviews to capture participants’ subjective work experiences. Among the participants, five had no prior psychiatric practice experience. All participants had encountered patients or family members with diagnosed or suspected mental illness in clinical settings.\n\nData analysis yielded four major findings:\n\nInsufficient mental illness literacy leads to ineffective intervention;\nUnclear role positioning and difficulties in interdisciplinary collaboration;\nPerceived challenges in case management contribute to resistance;\nInadequate crisis identification ability raises safety concerns.\n\nBased on these findings, the study proposes the following recommendations. First, medical social workers in Taiwan’s general hospitals should proactively strengthen their knowledge of mental illness and psychological assessment competencies to improve intervention effectiveness and support the national social safety net. Second, institutions should incorporate psychiatric disorders and psychopharmacology, psychiatric team operation models, treatment strategies, levels of illness insight, and workplace safety awareness into orientation training. Regular participation in psychiatric education programs and interdisciplinary coordination meetings is also recommended.', 'Chen Chien Hua', 'cjh79327@gmail.com', 'Chen Chien Hua (Department of Social Work, China Medical University Hospital, Taiwan)*; CHEN CHIA YU  (Department of Social Work, China Medical University Hospital, Taiwan)', 'Chien Hua, Chen*; CHIA YU , CHEN', 'cjh79327@gmail.com*; conniechen0821@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Exploring Factors Associated with Mental Health Literacy and Intervention Confidence among Medical Social Workers in Taiwanese General Hospital.pdf (136,312 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Name: CHEN CHIEN HUA, \nPosition: Social Worker, \nInstitution: China Medical University Hospital, Taiwan, \nEmail: cjh79327@gmail.com\n\nName: CHEN CHIA YU, \nPosition: Leader / Social Work Supervisor, \nInstitution: China Medical University Hospital, Taiwan, \nEmail: conniechen0821@gmail.com', 'Poster Presentation'),
('144', 'pankajksk28@gmail.com', '149', '5/31/2026 4:39:04 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 4:39:04 PM +00:00', 'Tribal Health Policies, Programmes and Access: A Situational Analysis', 'Census 2011 reveals that the population of Scheduled Tribes consist 104 million with 8.6% of the overall population of the country. The constitution of India has given special status and protection for the tribal communities under the constitutional schedule V and VI. In the present scenario 90% of the tribal communities, reside in remote areas. 20% of the tribal population spread over the 169 districts and 50% population of tribes are from 809 blocks of the country. The tribal population has been scattered across the large areas of the country and despite having policies and Programmes, access to basic healthcare remained challenged for these tribal communities. As per UN Declaration under the articles 21, 23, 24, and 29 has been revealed that the Right to Health of Indigenous Peoples need to be protected with special focus on indigenous women, children, youth, persons with disabilities and elders. Despite governmental efforts and initiatives in terms of tribal development, policies and Programmes there are several concerns in accessing basic fundamental healthcare services to these indigenous communities in remote areas. The present paper reviews the ground realities of tribal health programmes and policies through existing health programmes, reports, policy documents, budgetary allocation and initiatives of local authorities. The paper concludes that strengthening the tribal healthcare system through proper execution of programs and policies can lead to affordable, accessible and achievable quality healthcare services to indigenous communities in a meaningful way. \n Keywords: Tribal health, Health Programmes, Policies, Access and Situational Analysis\n', 'Pankaj Kumbhar', 'pankajksk28@gmail.com', 'Pankaj Kumbhar (Department of Social Work, Delhi School of Social Work, University of Delhi)*', 'Kumbhar, Pankaj*', 'pankajksk28@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Tribal Health Abstract 2026.docx (14,180 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Nil', 'Paper Presentation'),
('145', 'hasnakavungal12@gmail.com', '150', '5/31/2026 6:30:13 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 6:30:13 PM +00:00', 'Between Advocacy and Obsolescence: Algorithmic Bias, Professional Role Erosion, and the Future of Medical Social Work in AI-Integrated Healthcare: A Critical Narrative ', 'Objectives  \nThis review closely examines how the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into healthcare is changing medical social work (MSW). It focuses on algorithmic bias, threats to professional identity, and the growing need for MSWs to act as advocates in AI-driven systems of care.\nDesign  \nA critical narrative review design was used. It synthesises peer-reviewed literature, policy documents, and professional discussions published between 2015 and 2025 across PubMed, Scopus, PsycINFO, and Social Work Abstracts. The review includes studies that address AI in healthcare, algorithmic decision-making, social work roles, and digital health equity.\nModel  \nThe review uses critical social work theory and the human-in-the-loop model. This positions MSWs as key interpreters, challengers, and ethical checks against automated systems. It examines three areas: AI-driven risk stratification and psychosocial assessment tools; algorithmic bias affecting marginalised groups; and the automation of administrative and case management tasks that MSWs typically handle.\nResults  \nEvidence shows that while AI increases efficiency in screening and documentation, algorithmic systems often include socioeconomic, racial, and gender biases that harm vulnerable populations. At the same time, the partial automation of key MSW functions can lead to skill loss and erosion of professional roles, a trend seen in various healthcare professions. Nonetheless, new frameworks suggest an expanded role for MSWs as bias auditors and patient advocates in AI-integrated healthcare.\nConclusion  \nAI does not simply threaten or empower medical social work; it redefines the profession. MSWs must actively engage with AI, ethical practices, and policy advocacy to ensure fair, human-centred care in a healthcare landscape that is becoming increasingly automated.\nKeywords: artificial intelligence, medical social work, algorithmic bias, professional role erosion, health equity, human-in-the-loop\n', 'Hasna I', 'hasnakavungal12@gmail.com', 'Hasna I (State Poverty Eradication Mission, Kerala Government)*', 'I, Hasna*', 'hasnakavungal12@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Hasna__abstract.docx (16,092 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Nil', 'Paper Presentation'),
('146', 'yaarakraus@gmail.com', '151', '5/31/2026 6:34:31 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 6:34:31 PM +00:00', 'Family vs. Healthcare Providers\' Reports on Psychosocial Risk in Pediatric Rehabilitation: Using the Psychosocial Assessment Tool as a Patient-Reported Outcome Measure', 'Patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs) are central to family-centered pediatric care, yet the degree of agreement between family-reported and clinician-rated psychosocial risk in pediatric rehabilitation remains poorly understood. This study examined concordance between family and healthcare provider (HCP) assessments using the Psychosocial Assessment Tool (PAT2.0) as a PROM and its staff version as a routine outcome measure (ROM) in a pediatric rehabilitation setting. Clinical archive data were analyzed from 183 children (mean age 9.27 years; 63.9% male) admitted to a pediatric rehabilitation department between 2008 and 2021 following a traumatic medical event. Families completed the PAT2.0, and their treating psychologist or social worker independently completed the Staff PAT2.0. Agreement was evaluated using chi-square tests, intraclass correlation coefficients (ICCs), and mountain plots across overall risk categories and seven PAT domains. Significant discrepancies were found between informants. HCPs classified 75.9% of families as Universal (low) risk, compared to only 42% by family self-report. ICC analyses revealed poor agreement across most domains (ICC < 0.5), with the exception of Family Problems (ICC = 0.50). Mountain plots revealed a bidirectional pattern of disagreement, with HCPs rating families as less functional in four domains (social support, child problems, sibling problems, and parental stress reactions) but more functional in three domains (structure and resources, family problems, and family beliefs). No sociodemographic or clinical variable consistently predicted agreement across domains, though injury etiology influenced family-reported risk prevalence, with families of children with acquired brain injury more frequently reporting Universal risk, potentially reflecting limited early awareness of the injury\'s subtle sequelae. HCPs systematically underestimate family psychosocial risk relative to family self-reports in pediatric rehabilitation, with poor concordance across most domains. These findings underscore critical challenges in PROM implementation and highlight the indispensable value of family self-report in capturing the full breadth of psychosocial need. Rather than treating informant discrepancies as measurement error, clinicians should recognize them as clinically meaningful signals reflecting the unique perspectives and access to information held by families and providers respectively. Enhanced training in multi-informant integration and a genuine commitment to family-centered care principles are essential for bridging this gap and ensuring that family voices are meaningfully embedded in clinical decision-making.', 'Yaara Sadeh', 'yaarakraus@gmail.com', 'Yaara Sadeh (University of Haifa)*; Tamar Silberg (Bar Ilan University)', 'Sadeh, Yaara*; Silberg, Tamar', 'yaarakraus@gmail.com*; tamarsilberg@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Professional Practice, Interventions, and Models of Care', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'PROMS_2026.docx (15,410 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Hadas Rosenne (Hadassah Medical Center)', 'hadasr@hadassah.org.il', '', '', '', '', 'Tamar Silberg, Phd, tamarsilberg@gmail.com, Bar Ilan University ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('147', 'mthembum4@ukzn.ac.za', '152', '5/31/2026 6:52:17 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 6:56:30 PM +00:00', 'The Experiences of Clinical Research Staff during an HIV Cure Trial with an Analytical Treatment Interruption in Durban, South Africa', 'HIV cure trials involving analytical treatment interruptions (ATIs) are expanding in resource-limited settings where HIV prevalence is highest, such as South Africa. Given the socio-economic challenges in these regions, ATI-inclusive trials may present unintended mental health risks to trial participants. To ensure participant safety, tailored mental health support should be part of the routine study implementation. While there is a trend toward more participant-centred study designs, less attention is focused on clinical research staff and their perceptions on managing participants with pre-existing or emerging trauma. \nWe explored the perspectives of clinical research staff at Females Rising through Education Support and Health (FRESH) involved in an ATI-inclusive study, by conducting in-depth interviews (IDIs). Interviews were audio-recorded, transcribed and analysed thematically. \nClinical staff reported that participants were optimistic and hopeful about their involvement. However, staff expressed concerns about the psychological impact of going through the ATI, which was associated with emotional distress and, for some, triggered pre-existing trauma. The intersection of prior trauma and heightened anxiety around viral rebound warranted specific mental health support. Staff who counselled participants on HIV disclosure identified common barriers that hindered HIV status disclosure and trial participation. Staff expressed the need for specialised training to support participants’ mental health Including participant-centred psychosocial support in the design of ATI-inclusive HIV cure trials may improve study adherence and retention while protecting participants’ mental health. Equipping clinical research staff with trauma-informed competencies should be considered a minimum standard in ATI-inclusive trials settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n', 'Maud Mthembu', 'mthembum4@ukzn.ac.za', 'Maud Mthembu (University of KwaZulu-Natal)*', 'Mthembu, Maud*', 'mthembum4@ukzn.ac.za*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Conference abstract Maud et al.docx (23,632 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Miriam Schiff (Hebrew University Of Jerusalem)', 'miriam.schiff@mail.huji.ac.il', '', '', '', '', ' 1. Andiswa Mlotshwa- University of KwaZulu-Natal (MlotshwaT@ukzn.ac.za)\n 2. Nosipho Funeka- University of KwaZulu-Natal ( 215023011@stu.ukzn.ac.za)\n3.  Thumbi Ndung’u- HIV Pathogenesis Programme (HPP), The Doris Duke Medical Research Institute, Nelson R. Mandela School of Medicine, University of KwaZulu-Natal, Durban, South Africa (thumbi.ndungu@ahri.org)\n4. Jamila K. Stockman- University of California San Diego, School of Medicine, Division of Infectious Diseases and Global Public Health, La Jolla, CA, USA (jstockman@health.ucsd.edu)\n5. Karine Dubé- University of Pennsylvania, USA (Karine.Dube@PennMedicine.upenn.edu)\n6. Krista L. Dong-2	Ragon Institute of Mass General Brigham (MGB), Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) and Harvard University, Cambridge, MA, USA & Havard Medical School, Boston, USA (woodil.iteach@gmail.com)', 'Paper Presentation'),
('148', 'Yarden.gliksman@gmail.com', '153', '5/31/2026 7:44:39 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 8:33:04 PM +00:00', 'Maternal Anxiety and Child Emotional Distress During War: The Buffering Roles of Maternal Self-Efficacy and Parent–Child Communication', 'Objectives\nWar-related stress can negatively affect both parents and children. The present study examined the association between maternal anxiety and child emotional distress during the first month of the Iron Swords war and explored whether maternal self-efficacy and parent–child communication serve as protective factors.\nDesign\nA cross-sectional study was conducted among 135 mothers and their children residing in central Israel during a period of daily missile attacks. Mothers completed standardized questionnaires assessing anxiety (GAD-7), parental self-efficacy (MAAPS), war-related parent–child communication, and child emotional distress (PEDS).\nModel\nThe study tested a moderation model in which maternal self-efficacy and parent–child communication were expected to buffer the relationship between maternal anxiety and child emotional distress.\nResult\nHigher maternal anxiety was associated with greater child emotional distress. However, this relationship was not significant among mothers reporting higher levels of self-efficacy or among those who engaged in conversations with their children about the war. Maternal anxiety increased with child age and decreased with maternal self-efficacy. The protective effects of self-efficacy and communication were consistent across child age groups.\nConclusion\nMaternal self-efficacy and open communication with children may reduce the negative impact of maternal anxiety on children’s emotional well-being during wartime. Interventions aimed at strengthening parental confidence and encouraging age-appropriate discussions about stressful events may enhance family resilience in contexts of armed conflict.\nKeywords: Maternal anxiety; Child emotional distress; Maternal self-efficacy; Parent–child communication; War-related stress.', 'Yarden Gliksman', 'Yarden.gliksman@gmail.com', 'Yarden Gliksman (Ruppin Academic Center)*', 'Gliksman, Yarden*', 'Yarden.gliksman@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract Conference.docx (16,632 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Soon Noi GOH (Changi General Hospital)', 'soon_noi_goh@cgh.com.sg', '', '', '', '', 'Ortal Buchnik Atzil, Tzlil Einziger, Lilac Lev-Ari\nDepartment of Behavioral Sciences, Ruppin Academic Center, Emek Hefer, 4025000, Israel. \n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('149', 'sharonnaparstek@gmail.com', '154', '5/31/2026 8:31:16 PM +00:00', '5/31/2026 8:31:16 PM +00:00', 'Perceived Physical Quality of Life, Depression, and Suicidal Ideation in Community-Dwelling Older Adults', 'Objectives: Suicide prevention in older adulthood is a major public health concern, particularly as physical decline, chronic illness, and reduced social participation may converge with mental health vulnerability. Drawing on the Interpersonal Theory of Suicide, this study examined whether perceived burdensomeness (PB), thwarted belongingness (TB), and depressive symptoms, explain the association between perceived physical quality of life (QoL) and suicidal ideation among community-dwelling older adults.\nDesign: The study included 191 community-dwelling adults aged 65 and above (M = 72.34, SD = 5.86; 71.7% female). Participants completed questionnaires assessing physical QoL (WHOQOL-BREF), depressive symptoms (PHQ-8), PB and TB (INQ-15), and suicidal ideation (SBQ-R). Data were collected online and face-to-face.\n\nModel: Group comparisons, hierarchical regression, and path models examined how physical QoL relates to suicidal ideation directly and indirectly through depression and interpersonal risk markers.\n\nResult: Older adults reporting suicidal ideation showed poorer physical QoL (p = .043), higher depressive symptoms (p = .028), and higher TB (p = .013) compared with those without suicidal ideation. Physical QoL (p = .004), depression (p = .004), and the PB × TB interaction (p = .002) significantly predicted suicidal ideation. Path models further suggested that poorer physical QoL was linked to suicidal ideation both directly and indirectly, particularly through TB and depressive symptoms.\n\nConclusion: Perceived physical QoL may signal broader psychosocial vulnerability in community-dwelling older adults. Findings highlight depression and belonging as prevention targets and support integrating subjective health assessment with screening for social connectedness in community-based work with older adults.\n', 'Sharon Naparstek', 'sharonnaparstek@gmail.com', 'Sharon Naparstek (Department of Psychology, Bar-Ilan University)*; Shani Kandler (Department of Psychology, Bar-Ilan University); Yari Gvion (Department of Psychology, Bar-Ilan University)', 'Naparstek, Sharon*; Kandler, Shani; Gvion, Yari', 'sharonnaparstek@gmail.com*; shaniabh99@gmail.com; yari@kadi.co.il', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', 'Geok Ling Lee (National University of Singapore)', 'swklgl@nus.edu.sg', '', '', '', '', 'Shani Kandler, position: MA student, email: shaniabh99@gmail.com\nYari Gvion, position: Professor, email: yari@kadi.co.il', 'Paper Presentation'),
('150', 'atalmon@mail.huji.ac.il', '155', '6/1/2026 7:28:47 AM +00:00', '6/1/2026 7:28:47 AM +00:00', '“Neglected Mothers”: How Childhood Emotional Neglect Shapes Adjustment to Pregnancy and Early Motherhood', 'Background: The transition to motherhood involves multiple developmental challenges, including adapting to bodily changes, developing a maternal identity, and forming an emotional bond with the fetus/baby. While these tasks can be demanding for many women, those who experienced childhood emotional neglect may face particular difficulties.\nObjective: This study examined a model clarifying the mechanisms linking childhood emotional neglect with women’s adjustment during pregnancy and the early postpartum period.\nParticipants and setting: A total of 394 Israeli women participated during pregnancy (Time 1) and again two months postpartum (Time 2).\nMethods: Participants completed questionnaires assessing childhood emotional neglect, body experience, maternal self-efficacy (anticipated at Time 1; maternal at Time 2), attachment to the fetus/baby, and depressive symptoms.\nResults: Structural equation modeling (SEM) indicated that childhood emotional neglect was associated with depressive symptoms at both Time 1 and Time 2. These associations were mediated by body experience during pregnancy (Time 1) and early motherhood (Time 2), as well as by anticipated maternal self-efficacy (Time 1) and maternal self-efficacy (Time 2). The model accounted for 56% of the variance in postpartum depressive symptoms (Time 2).\nConclusions: Findings highlight the long-term impact of childhood emotional neglect on adjustment to motherhood and suggest that body experience and maternal self-efficacy are key pathways through which early neglect relates to depressive symptoms in pregnancy and postpartum.', 'Anat Talmon', 'atalmon@mail.huji.ac.il', 'Anat Talmon (the Hebrew University of Jerusalem)*', 'Talmon, Anat*', 'atalmon@mail.huji.ac.il*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '1', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'NA', 'Paper Presentation'),
('151', 'sm.25hs1102@nitdgp.ac.in', '156', '6/1/2026 10:06:43 AM +00:00', '6/1/2026 10:06:43 AM +00:00', 'Caregivers’ Fatigue in Autism Families: A study in West Bengal', 'Title of Paper – Caregivers’ Fatigue in Autism Families: A Study in West Bengal.\n\nKeywords: Fatigue, Autism Spectrum Disorder, Social isolation, Societal stigma, Coping strategies.\n\nBackground:\n\nThe journey of the caregivers with an autistic child is deeply personal and complex, shaped by various experiences and challenges. Caregivers encounter different emotions across different stages in handling their ASD child. The prevalence of ASD in West Bengal is a complex phenomenon shaped by numerous factors, including cultural, socio-economic, and healthcare dynamics. Understanding factors is important to know how the caregivers of these children face different challenges of which they are an integral part. \n\nObjective:\n\nTo explore and understand the tiring experiences of the caregivers across different stages in handling their ASD child, social views towards them as caregivers of the ASD child, and to find out the ways of coping and resilience mechanisms with the situation. \n\nMethodology:\n\nA qualitative research approach has been used, and the study area is West Bengal. The research study is exploratory; therefore, no hypothesis was formulated. The data for the study were collected through home visits. Purposive sampling was used to select the sample. Caregivers of a total of ten children with ASD (five female and five male) were interviewed for the research study. The data were collected by administering a semi-structured interview schedule to the caregivers of children with ASD.\n\nResults:\n\nCaregivers of children with ASD navigate a challenging journey marked by fatigue, which affects their lives profoundly. The journey is paved with difficulties, including financial strain, lack of understanding and support, social isolation & societal stigma. Child behavioral difficulties cause caregiver fatigue, which, in turn, may lead to increased stress and the use of ineffective coping strategies. Thus, managing energy levels and reducing stressors is essential.\n\nConclusion:\n\nThe study contributes to the growing body of knowledge aimed at supporting and understanding the caregivers affected by ASD. Continued research and advocacy in the area of ASD caregivers’ fatigue with regard to culture and region specification are necessary to ensure that these families receive the necessary support to thrive.\n', 'SUDESHNA MONDAL', 'sm.25hs1102@nitdgp.ac.in', 'SUDESHNA MONDAL (NIT DURGAPUR)*', 'MONDAL, SUDESHNA*', 'sm.25hs1102@nitdgp.ac.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Final Abstract for Mental Health Seminar S Mondal A Jajodia. (1).docx (17,503 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Dr Angshu Jajodia\nAssistant Professor, Department of Humanities and Social Sciences, National Institute of Technology Durgapur\nPh.: 9434789090\nEmail ID: ajajodia.hu@nitdgp.ac.in\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('152', 'mkakot1@lsu.edu', '157', '6/1/2026 3:41:23 PM +00:00', '6/1/2026 3:47:18 PM +00:00', 'Effects of Mental Health conditions and substance use disorders on suicide risk among U.S. Adults: NSDUH 2024', 'Objectives:\nTo examine the effect of Mental health conditions and substance use disorder on suicide risk among U.S. adults.\nDesign:\nA cross-sectional design was used to analyze the National Survey on Drug Use & Health 2024 (n=47,299, adults ≥18), which employed a stratified, multistage complex survey design. Sampling weights were adjusted to reflect the U.S. civilian population rather than military officials or the non-institutionalized population. Race/ethnicity was analyzed as an intersectional covariate across seven racial groups.\nModel:\nBinary Logistic Regression was used to predict suicide risk with 12 model predictors: 6 MH indicators and 6 SUD indicators. Intersectional variation was evaluated by racial/ethnic stratification.\nResult:\nThe model was statistically significant with 34.8% variance in suicide risk (Nagelkerke R2 = .348). Individuals with severe mental illness showed results of higher odds of suicide risk (AOR = 26.27, 95% CI [26.21, 26.34]), followed by doubled odds of past year serious psychological distress (AOR = 2.18, 95% CI [2.18, 2.19]). Past year illicit drug use elevated significantly by 14.1%, yet accounting for psychological distress, the independent effect of specific SUD was negligible (AOR = 1.00). White & Hispanic populations were statistically significant while accounting for negligible variance as compared to psychological distress.\nConclusion:\nSerious mental illness and psychological distress are the primary predictors of suicide risk among U.S. adults, whereas SUDs do not have any independent effect if mental health indicators are controlled. Public health and clinical interventions must employ comprehensive MH assessments with substance use as critical precursors, and interventions should integrate racial constructs to examine and analyze racial diversification in mental health and substance use disorders and suicidality.\n', 'Monikornika Kakoty', 'mkakot1@lsu.edu', 'Monikornika Kakoty (Louisiana State University)*; Elaine M. Maccio (Louisiana State University)', 'Kakoty, Monikornika*; Maccio, Elaine M.', 'mkakot1@lsu.edu*; emaccio@lsu.edu', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract_ ICSWHMH1..docx (16,755 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Miriam Schiff (Hebrew University Of Jerusalem)', 'miriam.schiff@mail.huji.ac.il', '', '', '', '', 'Name: Elaine M. Maccio\nPosition: Associate Professor\nInstitution: Louisiana State University\nemail id: emaccio@lsu.edu', 'Poster Presentation'),
('153', 'dbw7840@nyu.edu', '158', '6/1/2026 7:23:30 PM +00:00', '6/1/2026 7:23:30 PM +00:00', 'From Trauma to Intimacy: Integrating Ferenczian and Functional Approaches to Sexual Health in Veterans with Military Sexual Trauma', 'Background:  \nMilitary Sexual Trauma (MST) is associated with significant yet under-recognized consequences for sexual health, intimacy, and relational trust among women veterans.  Although MST is strongly linked to PTSD and depression, sexual dysfunction remains a distinct and under-addressed clinical outcome that impacts overall well-being and relationship stability.   This study centers sexual health within trauma-informed care by examining how MST disrupts intimacy, embodiment, and trust.\n\nMethods:\nThis conceptual paper applies a theoretical framework to bridge research and practice. Drawing on Trauma Theory (Ferenczi; van der Kolk) and Sexual Functioning Theory (Barlow), it conceptualizes trauma as both psychologically and physiologically embodied and examines its effects on sexual functioning, including desire, arousal, and relational safety.  Ferenczi’s concept of betrayal trauma and “confusion of tongues” highlights the impact of violations of trust within hierarchical systems such as the military, while van der Kolk’s work explains how trauma is encoded in the body and shapes sensory and emotional responses. Barlow’s model further highlights how trauma-related anxiety and cognitive interference disrupt sexual functioning.  Together, these frameworks provide a clinically relevant lens for understanding survivors’ experiences and helps guide trauma-informed assessments and interventions.\n\nImplications for Practice:\nApplication of these frameworks highlights a gap in current practice: sexual health concerns among MST survivors are prevalent yet under-assessed due to stigma, underreporting, and provider discomfort. Survivors may experience diminished desire, avoidance of intimacy, and difficulty with trust and connection that are not fully explained by co-occurring mental health conditions.\n\nConclusions:\nIntegrating sexual health into trauma-informed care is important for improving responses to MST. This approach helps professionals- like social workers, health care providers, advocates and legal professionals to better understand trauma and provide more complete fair and supportive care.  \n\nKeywords: Military Sexual Trauma; Sexual Health; Women Veterans; Trauma Theory; Intimacy; Social Work Practice\n\n\n', 'DANIELLE WRIGHT', 'dbw7840@nyu.edu', 'DANIELLE WRIGHT (Independent )*', 'WRIGHT, DANIELLE*', 'dbw7840@nyu.edu*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', 'Soon Noi GOH (Changi General Hospital)', 'soon_noi_goh@cgh.com.sg', '', '', '', '', 'n/a', 'PhD Best Paper Award'),
('154', 'sassons@lemoyne.edu', '159', '6/2/2026 2:06:16 AM +00:00', '6/2/2026 2:06:16 AM +00:00', 'Interprofessional Community Education for Hypertension Prevention', 'Hypertension remains a leading modifiable risk factor for cardiovascular disease, disproportionately affecting communities with limited access to health education and reliable nutrition information. This presentation describes a community-based hypertension cooking and education event delivered on two occasions through an interprofessional collaboration among\noccupational therapy, nursing, physician assistant, and clinical mental health counseling faculty.\n\nThe program(s) focused on preventative care through food and movement, health literacy related to nutrition and lifestyle, and accessing reliable sources of health information.\n\nParticipants engaged in hands-on cooking demonstrations featuring low-sodium meals and culturally adaptable spice alternatives, alongside interactive education on blood pressure\nmanagement, interpreting nutrition labels, and identifying credible nutrition resources. Mindful eating practices were incorporated to address behavioral and psychosocial contributors to hypertension, highlighting the role of mental health in chronic disease management.\n\nInterprofessional faculty modeled collaborative practice by integrating discipline-specific perspectives into unified community messaging.\n\nThis presentation will highlight program design, interprofessional roles, participant engagement strategies, and lessons learned from hosting the event twice. Attendees will gain practical\ninsights into developing sustainable, interprofessional, community facing interventions that promote health literacy, preventative care, and equitable access to reliable nutrition information.', 'Samantha Sassone', 'sassons@lemoyne.edu', 'Samantha Sassone (Le Moyne College)*', 'Sassone, Samantha*', 'sassons@lemoyne.edu*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Interdisciplinary and Collaborative Practice', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', 'Miriam Schiff (Hebrew University Of Jerusalem)', 'miriam.schiff@mail.huji.ac.il', '', '', '', '', 'Dr. Samantha Sassone DNP, AGPCNP-C, RN, LMT\nAssistant Professor of Nursing \nLe Moyne College, Syracuse NY, USA\n\nDr. Marisa Davis, OTD, OTR, PTA\nProfessor of Practice Occupational Therapy Department \nLe Moyne College, Syracuse NY, USA \nhartmaa@lemoyne.edu ', 'Workshop'),
('155', 'keren.gat@biu.ac.il', '160', '6/2/2026 12:47:06 PM +00:00', '6/2/2026 12:59:48 PM +00:00', 'Trauma-Informed Care Implementation in Neurodevelopmental Settings: A Pilot Study Within a Shared Traumatic Reality', 'Objectives: Children with neurodevelopmental disorders (NDDs) face a 6.2 times higher risk for maltreatment compared to typically developing peers, yet trauma-informed care (TIC) remains underutilized in their care. This pilot study aimed to: (1) improve trauma-informed knowledge, attitudes, competence, and practices and reduce barriers to TIC implementation among healthcare providers (HCPs); and (2) examine whether therapists\' personal exposure within a shared traumatic reality impacts their ability to adopt TIC approaches.\nDesign: Twenty-one HCPs (85.7% females, mean age = 43.0) from a child development center participated in a two-day NDD-TIC training, six months after the October 7th terror attack and during the subsequent war in Israel. Pre- and post-training surveys assessed trauma-informed knowledge, attitudes, competence, and practices, alongside therapists’ self-reported trauma exposure and qualitative data.\nModel: An NDD-adapted TIC training addressing the unique characteristics of children with NDDs, grounded in SAMHSA\'s four principles: realizing the widespread impact of trauma, recognizing signs and symptoms, responding by integrating trauma knowledge into policies and practices, and resisting re-traumatization.\nResult: Paired-sample t-tests revealed significant improvements in HCPs’ self-competence and practical implementation of trauma-informed practices, with a marginally significant reduction in perceived barriers. No significant changes were found in knowledge or attitudes. Spearman’s correlation revealed that higher personal trauma exposure was associated with smaller attitude improvements (rs = −0.44, p = .045). Qualitative themes highlighted training gaps, difficulties with parents, and organizational barriers.\nConclusion: Brief NDD-TIC training enhances HCPs’ confidence and practice, yet personal exposure to shared trauma may hinder attitude changes and implementation. Findings suggest that therapist-focused support, including structured self-care protocols, peer debriefing, and trauma-sensitive supervision, may be critical to TIC adoption. Future research should investigate large-scale NDD-TIC implementation, incorporating long-term follow-up to examine sustained effects.\n', 'Keren Gat', 'keren.gat@biu.ac.il', 'Keren Gat (Bar Ilan University)*; Yaara Sadeh (University of Haifa and Sheba Medical Center); Ofer Golan (Bar-Ilan University); Omer Bar-Yosef (Sheba Medical Center and Tel-Aviv University); Tamar  Silberg (Bar-Ilan University and Sheba Medical Center)', 'Gat, Keren*; Sadeh, Yaara; Golan, Ofer; Bar-Yosef, Omer; Silberg, Tamar ', 'keren.gat@biu.ac.il*; yaarakraus@gmail.com; Ofer.Golan@biu.ac.il; Omer.BarYosef@sheba.health.gov.il; tamarsilberg@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '1', '1', '1', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '2027 ICSWHMH Abstract.docx (21,016 bytes)', '1', '', '0', 'Soon Noi GOH (Changi General Hospital)', 'soon_noi_goh@cgh.com.sg', '', '', '', '', 'Yaara Sadeh - PhD. Department of Pediatric Rehabilitation, the Edmond and Lily Safra Children’s Hospital, Sheba Medical Center, Tel-Hashomer, Ramat-Gan, Israel. Department of Psychology, University of Haifa, Haifa, Israel.\nyaarakraus@gmail.com\n\nOfer Golan - Professor. Department of Psychology, Bar-Ilan University, Ramat-Gan, Israel.\nOfer.Golan@biu.ac.il\n\nOmer Bar-Yosef - MD. The Weinberg Child Development Center, the Edmond and Lily Safra Children’s Hospital, Sheba Medical Center, Tel-Hashomer, Ramat-Gan, Israel. The Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, Tel Aviv University, Tel-Aviv Yaffo, Israel.\nOmer.BarYosef@sheba.health.gov.il\n\nTamar Silberg - Professor. Department of Psychology, Bar-Ilan University, Ramat-Gan, Israel. Department of Pediatric Rehabilitation, the Edmond and Lily Safra Children’s Hospital, Sheba Medical Center, Tel-Hashomer, Ramat-Gan, Israel.\ntamarsilberg@gmail.com', 'Best PhD Poster Award'),
('156', 'contact@transformersacademyllc.org', '161', '6/3/2026 5:24:04 PM +00:00', '6/3/2026 5:24:04 PM +00:00', 'TAMAR (Trauma. Addiction, Mental Health, and Recovery)', 'Title: Trauma, Addiction, Mental Health, and Recovery\nAuthor: Dr. Angelo Reynolds, Transformers Academy, LLC\n	     Website: www.drangeloreynolds.com\n             Email: contact@transformersacademyllc.org\nBackground: Dr. Reynolds is esteemed for his extensive experience in educational leadership, mental health facilitation, substance abuse counseling, and personal as well as professional development.\nLearning Objectives: Upon completion of this workshop, participants will:\n•	Acquire a comprehensive understanding of trauma, addiction, mental health challenges, and effective treatment approaches.\n•	Access evidence-based resources to support recovery and enhance well-being among individuals managing these concerns.\n•	Apply best practices in psychoeducation tailored to their organizational contexts.\nAbstract: Psychoeducation is a targeted therapeutic approach that teaches individuals about their mental health conditions, coping strategies, and support systems. Research shows it is highly effective at increasing treatment adherence, reducing relapse rates by 20% to 50% for severe illnesses, and empowering patients and their families.\nPsychoeducation has also been demonstrated as an effective intervention for trauma and addiction. This approach aids in normalizing responses, reducing shame, and equipping survivors with vital information regarding the effects of trauma on neurological and physiological functioning (National Institute of Health, 2026).\nResults: The facilitation of multiple workshops and seminars addressing substance abuse and mental health conditions has produced positive results. Participants have reported increased knowledge and a greater understanding of techniques to reduce relapse risk and manage mental health symptoms effectively.\nSources: National Association of State Mental Health Program Directors (NASMHPD), 1990; and National Institute of Health, 2026.\nPresentation Outline: TAMAR\nTAMAR represents Trauma, Addiction, Mental Health, and Recovery. This presentation aims to provide individuals with insight into how adverse experiences, referred to as \"trauma,\" may influence their lives and outlines pathways toward healing. Emphasis will be placed on identifying triggers and offering coping strategies to regulate stress and maladaptive behaviors.\n\nI. Introduction to Trauma, Addiction, Mental Health, and Recovery (TAMAR)\n1.	Addiction: Conceptual Foundations\n2.	Trauma: Definition and Impact\n3.	Treatment Modalities for Mental Health\nII. Physical and Emotional Abuse\n1.	Psychological Trauma\n2.	Techniques for Emotional Regulation\n3.	Physical and Biological Effects of Trauma\nIII. Boundaries and Safety\n1.	Establishing Intimacy and Trust\n2.	Effective Communication and Recognition\nIV. Closing\n1.	Discussion of Universal Best Practices\nV. Q&A Session\n\n', 'Dr. Angelo Reynolds', 'contact@transformersacademyllc.org', 'Dr. Angelo Reynolds (Transformers Academy, LLC)*', 'Reynolds, Dr. Angelo*', 'contact@transformersacademyllc.org*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Dr. Reynolds CV 5.29.26.pdf (211,221 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'None', 'Workshop'),
('157', 'zubin.savla24@sakec.ac.in', '162', '6/4/2026 7:36:32 AM +00:00', '6/4/2026 7:36:32 AM +00:00', 'Cervical Cancer Screening: Comparing Testing Methods', 'Cervical cancer is an avoidable condition; however, it poses one of the greatest threats to public health in underdeveloped countries due to a poor healthcare service delivery system rather than diagnostic accuracy. The purpose of this study is to assess the efficiency of the following five screening tests of cervical cancer: cytology, HPV infection molecular test, visual inspection, self-sampling, and biomarker triage based on their technical and operational efficiency. Basically, it should be noted that the efficiency of screening of the disease cannot be attributed to its diagnostic accuracy but to healthcare service delivery efficiency.', 'Zubin Savla', 'zubin.savla24@sakec.ac.in', 'Zubin Savla (Shah & Anchor Kutchhi Engineering College)*', 'Savla, Zubin*', 'zubin.savla24@sakec.ac.in*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Similarity_Report.pdf (1,386,903 bytes); Zubin_ Final_2026.pdf (1,264,691 bytes)', '2', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Dr.Asha Durafe\nasha.durafe@sakec.ac.in\n\nDr.Manisha Mane\nmanisha.mane@sakec.ac.', 'Paper Presentation'),
('158', 'Telzur@bgu.ac.il', '163', '6/4/2026 9:20:24 AM +00:00', '6/4/2026 9:20:24 AM +00:00', 'Prickly Pear Consumption, Women’s Health, and Work Productivity: An Interdisciplinary Dryland Approach to Fibromyalgia Management', 'Fibromyalgia syndrome (FMS) is a chronic condition associated with pain, fatigue, reduced quality of life, and substantial work productivity loss. Innovative, sustainable, and community-based interventions are needed to address both the health and social consequences of FMS. We build on a pilot study examining fresh prickly pear (Opuntia ficus-indica) as a complementary dietary intervention for women with FMS. \nPrickly pear is a climate-resilient crop adapted to arid environments and rich in bioactive compounds, including betalains, flavonoids, vitamins, fiber, and minerals with documented antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties. This interdisciplinary project brings together researchers from social work, health sciences, and agricultural sciences to explore the links between nutrition, health, and social functioning. Preliminary findings from our pilot study suggested improvements in quality of life among women consuming fresh prickly pear, consistent with previous evidence demonstrating reduced oxidative stress and enhanced antioxidant status.\nThe proposed study has three objectives: (1) to characterize seasonal differences in the phytochemical composition of prickly pear fruit (summer versus winter harvests); (2) to examine associations between seasonal metabolomic profiles and health outcomes among women with FMS; and (3) to evaluate whether improvements in health and quality of life are associated with enhanced work productivity and daily functioning, measured by the Work Productivity and Activity Impairment (WPAI) questionnaire.\nFrom a social work and public health perspective, the study addresses the broader social and economic burden of FMS, including absenteeism, presenteeism, and reduced participation in work and community life. By linking climate-resilient agriculture with health promotion, the project highlights the potential of locally produced functional foods to support women\'s well-being while contributing to sustainable rural development. The Negev region serves as a unique setting for examining how agricultural innovation can be leveraged to advance health equity, social participation, and economic resilience in dryland communities.\n', 'Noemi Tel-Zur', 'Telzur@bgu.ac.il', 'Noemi Tel-Zur (Ben Gurion University of the Negev)*; Orly Sarid (Ben Gurion University of the Negev); Orli Grinstein-Cohen (Ben Gurion University of the Negev)', 'Tel-Zur, Noemi*; Sarid, Orly; Grinstein-Cohen, Orli', 'Telzur@bgu.ac.il*; orlysa@bgu.ac.il; grinstie@bgu.ac.il', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Interdisciplinary and Collaborative Practice', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '', '0', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'no other authors', 'Poster Presentation'),
('159', 'victoria.rodriguez@mountsinai.org', '164', '6/4/2026 9:28:12 PM +00:00', '6/4/2026 9:28:12 PM +00:00', 'From Notes to Numbers: Transforming Social Work Documentation into Actionable Data', 'Objectives\nAs healthcare systems increasingly prioritize data-driven decision-making, social work documentation remains an untapped resource for informing system-level change. While electronic medical records (EMRs) have expanded data-collection capabilities, documentation practices often remain narrative, inconsistent, and disconnected from broader operational and performance goals. This presentation redefines documentation not only as a clinical task but also as a strategic tool for driving change and demonstrating impact.\nDesign\nThis presentation uses literature related to documentation standardization and healthcare data utilization and implementation.  It uses examples from Mount Sinai Hospital to highlight how effective change management, redesigning documentation templates, structuring data fields, and aligning workflows can improve data integrity, increase compliance, and generate meaningful insights to support both clinical practice and organizational strategy.\nModel\nFramed within the context of change management within one of the largest healthcare systems in the United States, this presentation addresses common barriers, including staff resistance to change, documentation burden, and competing priorities. The model offers practical strategies for engaging frontline staff, integrating user feedback, and sustaining adoption through empowering participants to lead meaningful improvements.\nResult\nThis presentation incorporates key lessons learned, including the importance of leadership alignment, staff engagement, workflow integration, and ongoing feedback. Implementation resulted in improved documentation standardization and enhanced opportunities for data capture. Standardized documentation structures created opportunities to strengthen reporting capabilities, identify workflow gaps, monitor trends, increase visibility into social work interventions, supporting quality improvement initiatives and operational decision-making. \nConclusion\nParticipants gain actionable approaches to redesign documentation systems to support data capture and usability, leverage EMR-derived data to evaluate performance and identify gaps and position social work as a measurable and visible contributor to health system outcomes. \n', 'Victoria Rodriguez', 'victoria.rodriguez@mountsinai.org', 'Victoria Rodriguez (Mount Sinai Hospital)*', 'Rodriguez, Victoria*', 'victoria.rodriguez@mountsinai.org*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Innovation, Technology, and Effectiveness', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'From Notes to Numbers Abstrat Dr. Victoria Rodriguez 6.4.26.docx (16,183 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'None', 'Paper Presentation'),
('160', 'heba.adileh@mail.huji.ac.il', '165', '6/5/2026 12:20:56 PM +00:00', '6/5/2026 12:20:56 PM +00:00', 'What Children Do Not Say, What Parents Do Not See: A Context-Informed Framework for Family-Centered Care in Pediatric Bone Marrow Transplantation', 'Background: Bone Marrow Transplantation (BMT) is a complex treatment requiring hospitalization for at least six weeks and prolonged protective isolation. During this period, children face significant restrictions on their daily activities, with only one primary caregiver permitted to remain with the child, whereas siblings are excluded from in-person visits. These circumstances may profoundly impact family functioning, relationships, and psychosocial well-being. Although both children and parents may experience considerable emotional distress, parental well-being and resilience are critical to supporting children\'s coping and recovery. Despite the significant psychosocial implications of BMT, children\'s perspectives remain underrepresented in the literature, and limited research has examined the experiences of children and parents as an intersected dyad.\nAims: The current study aims to explore how pediatric patients and their parents experience the BMT process, the resilience factors that facilitate their coping, and the various contexts that shape their experiences.\nMethod: The current study employed a qualitative methodology, utilizing the photovoice method. Children who participated in the study were asked to take pictures that describe the challenges they experience during the BMT process and their coping strategies. Data were collected through pictures captured by eight Palestinian-Muslim children and semi-structured depth interviews conducted with seven of them, and eight of their parents, for a total of 15 interviews. \nIn this study, the photovoice methodology served two purposes, based on two approaches in qualitative research. First, a phenomenological approach to examine children\'s experiences of BMT. According to it, the photovoice methodology is perceived as a method by which the researcher analyzes the meanings and interpretations of research participants\' pictures, treating them as narrative data to understand their life experiences. Second, based on the approach of community-based participatory research, which aims to promote social justice and empower research participants regarding the issue at the center of the research.\nResults: Analysis relied on the thematic approach and raised four main themes: 1) The first theme focused on the unique hospital characteristics that shape the child’s BMT experience. This theme included time, space, territory, and the humane dimensions. 2) The second theme focused on the resilience factors in the various aspects of life, which help children cope with the BMT experience. These aspects included: the child’s inner strength, social and familial support, the professional community, and the technological means. 3) The third theme shed light on the loneliness that children experience during and after BMT. In addition, this theme focused on the impact of the coronavirus pandemic on children\'s BMT experiences. 4) Finally, the fourth theme emphasized children’s and parents’ suffering in the child’s BMT process.\nDiscussion and Implications: Two core concepts informed the interpretation of the findings. First, the context-informed approach highlighted the various contexts shaping children\'s BMT experiences, including the hospital, home, Islamic religious beliefs, and the COVID-19 pandemic. Second, salutogenic and existential approaches highlighted the resilience factors and coping strategies used by children and parents to navigate the challenges they face during the BMT process.\nThe current study contributes to the fields of social work, health, and psychosocial care by amplifying children\'s voices and examining the intersected experiences of children and parents. Findings underscore the impact of cultural, religious, familial, and contextual factors on children\'s well-being and recovery. For healthcare social workers, the findings emphasize the need for context-informed, family-centered, and advocacy-based practices that recognize children\'s perspectives, acknowledge the intersected experiences of children and their families, and promote psychosocial well-being. The study also demonstrates the value of creative participatory methods, such as photovoice, for enhancing children\'s engagement in research and practice and informing more responsive healthcare services and policies.', 'Heba Adileh', 'heba.adileh@mail.huji.ac.il', 'Heba Adileh (The Hebrew University of Jerusalem)*', 'Adileh, Heba*', 'heba.adileh@mail.huji.ac.il*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Professional Practice, Interventions, and Models of Care', '', '2', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Abstract.docx (16,866 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Name: Dr. Hadass Moore\nPosition: Senior Lecturer\nInstitution: The Hebrew University of Jerusalem\nEmail: hadass.moore@mail.huji.ac.il', 'Paper Presentation'),
('161', 'menny@post.bgu.ac.il', '166', '6/6/2026 7:43:29 AM +00:00', '6/6/2026 7:43:29 AM +00:00', '\"I Look Through the Same Window and Now See Something Different\": A Trauma- and Resilience-Informed Photovoice Model Following a Human-Made Collective Disaster', 'Objectives: Human-made collective disasters disrupt systems of meaning, safety, and social relations while challenging communities’ ability to cope and recover. This study examines the implementation of the Trauma- and Resilience-Informed Photovoice Model (TRIPVM) as a Community-Based Participatory Research (CBPR) intervention following the October 7, 2023 attacks in Ofakim, Israel. Specifically, it explores how trauma, loss, and resilience are expressed through photovoice outputs and how individual and collective coping resources are identified and constructed through participatory processes.\nDesign: A CBPR design was employed within a large-scale community intervention involving 11 photovoice groups and 89 participants. Participants generated approximately 356 photovoice units. Data were analyzed using qualitative content analysis and a polytextual approach.\nModel: TRIPVM is a trauma- and resilience-informed community intervention model grounded in photovoice and CBPR principles. The model integrates four components: disaster management, resilience narratives, community ownership of representation and narrative, and task-level adaptations involving photography, facilitation, and public exhibition. The model aims to support meaning-making, strengthen agency, and facilitate the identification and construction of coping resources following collective disaster.\nResult: Four themes emerged: (1) negotiating traumatic space, (2) community as a coping resource, (3) choice as a coping resource, and (4) meaning-making of loss and bereavement. Findings demonstrate how participants reinterpreted traumatic spaces, mobilized community resources, exercised agency, and constructed meanings of loss, fostering resilience-oriented narratives alongside ongoing trauma and grief.\nConclusion: The findings highlight the potential of trauma-informed participatory approaches to support coping, meaning-making, and community resilience following collective disaster. TRIPVM positions photovoice as a structured community intervention that promotes narrative ownership, recognition, and recovery.\n\nKeywords: Photovoice; Community-Based Participatory Research; Collective Disaster; Trauma-Informed Practice; Community Resilience\n\n', 'Menny Malka', 'menny@post.bgu.ac.il', 'Menny Malka (Ben-Gurion University of the Negev)*', 'Malka, Menny*', 'menny@post.bgu.ac.il*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', '2027 ICSWHMH Abstract 1 Malka.docx (15,877 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Single author sobmissions ', 'Paper Presentation'),
('163', 'qureshi.nabila-sw@msubaroda.ac.in', '168', '6/6/2026 3:36:44 PM +00:00', '6/6/2026 3:36:44 PM +00:00', 'Lifestyle Disorders in Focus: A Study of Senior Perceptions and Knowledge in Rural Vadodara, Gujarat', 'Abstract\n\nIndia is undergoing a substantial demographic transition as the country has attained the label of an ageing nation with 7.7 % of its total population comprise of senior citizens of the nation of more than 60 years (Ingle (2008)).  According to the recent data by Lifestyle diseases surge in India, infectious ailments fall: (2025) highlights a noteworthy alteration in the country\'s health profile. Further, the data reveals that Lifestyle diseases in India are now more predominant than ever, affecting nearly half of the total population. \n\nAccording to an article published on September 2025, by The Hindu signifies that 60 % of mortality cases in India are a result of lifestyle-related diseases such as heart attacks, diabetes, stroke, cancer, and mental health syndromes connected to screen habit and seclusion. With this significant rise in the number of increases in life style disorders it is difficult for a nation to progress when its large number of populations are suffering from life style disorders. This demographic transition leads to the increasing demand of availability, accessibility and affordability of health, social, and commercial complications that require suitable initiatives. Additionally, it also requires focus, perception and awareness amongst the citizen for the precautionary as well as healthy lifestyle to prevent the life style disorders at an early stage of life. \n\nThe present study is carried out among rural and urban elderly population of the city to assess the level of knowledge regarding lifestyle disorders among rural and urban populations, to examine attitudes towards prevention, early detection, and management of lifestyle disorders, to analyze lifestyle practices related to diet, physical activity, addiction, and regular health check-ups, to provide recommendations for health education and community-based interventions to prevent lifestyle disorders. The study adopts descriptive research methodology on a sample size of 54 respondents residing in rural and urban area in and around the city of Vadodara. The data is collected during November 2025 to January 2026.\n\nThe findings of this study will pave the way in developing effective health education programs and community-based interventions aimed at preventing lifestyle disorders, to policymakers, healthcare professionals, and social workers who plays a pivotal role in spreading awareness among the masses on various health related issues.\n', 'Dr. Nabila Qureshi', 'qureshi.nabila-sw@msubaroda.ac.in', 'Dr. Nabila Qureshi (Faculty of Social Work, The Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda)*; Mauli Tailor (Faculty of Social Work, The Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda )', 'Qureshi, Dr. Nabila*; Tailor, Mauli', 'qureshi.nabila-sw@msubaroda.ac.in*; maulitailor6@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Lifestyle Disorders in Focus.docx (29,338 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Mauli Tailor- Research Scholar, Faculty of Social Work, The Maharaja Sayajirao University of Baroda', 'Paper Presentation'),
('164', 'einavs@mail.sapir.ac.il', '169', '6/6/2026 5:35:14 PM +00:00', '6/6/2026 5:35:14 PM +00:00', '“We cannot only be containing; we also need to be contained”: Wartime self-care among early-career social workers ', 'Objectives:\nSelf-care has become a central concept in social work due to increasing recognition of the emotional demands and trauma exposure inherent in practice. However, limited research has examined self-care among early-career social workers (ECSWs) operating within shared traumatic realities (STRs) during collective crises. This study explored how ECSWs practicing under wartime conditions perceive and enact self-care.\nDesign:\nA qualitative phenomenological design was employed to examine participants’ lived experiences of self-care in the context of ongoing war and continuous threat.\nModel:\nTwenty-two ECSWs participated in semi-structured interviews. Data were analyzed using thematic analysis to identify patterns in participants’ understandings, experiences, and practices of self-care across personal and professional domains.\nResults:\nFindings revealed considerable ambiguity regarding the meaning and boundaries of self-care. Participants rarely perceived self-care as embedded within organizational structures and reported limited institutional support. Nevertheless, organizational factors such as flexibility and a supportive workplace culture were experienced as beneficial when available. Participants described a range of personal self-care practices, including self-attunement, writing, mindfulness, use of trauma-informed professional knowledge, engagement in reflective supervision, and reliance on support from family and friends. The findings further suggest that within STR contexts, a hierarchy of self-care strategies emerges, whereby some practices become more difficult to sustain while others become essential for maintaining functioning and emotional stability.\nConclusion:\nThe findings highlight the importance of integrating structured organizational support, reflective supervision, and accessible mental health resources into social work settings operating under chronic crisis conditions. Strengthening these supports may enhance the resilience of ECSWs and promote the continuity and quality of trauma-informed care during collective and ongoing societal crises.\n', 'Einav Segev', 'einavs@mail.sapir.ac.il', 'Einav Segev (Sapir Academic college)*', 'Segev, Einav*', 'einavs@mail.sapir.ac.il*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Workforce Development, Wellbeing, and Leadership', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'We cannot only be containing.docx (23,996 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Einav Segev (no co-authors)', 'Paper Presentation'),
('165', 'radeshmukh_comp@jspmrscoe.edu.in', '170', '6/10/2026 11:35:22 AM +00:00', '6/10/2026 11:35:22 AM +00:00', 'An Intelligent Consumer Grievance Management System Using Machine Learning And Voice Recognition for Automated  Incident Prioritization', 'Customer complaints handling is an important \nfactor in managing business operations in service-based \nindustries because the delay in handling customer complaints \nand improper prioritization may affect the efficiency of the \nbusiness. The majority of the conventional techniques in \nhandling customer complaints follow manual operations. These \nmanual operations may cause inconsistencies and may not \nprovide the required analytical benefits. This paper proposes an \nadvanced system in handling consumer grievances using \nmachine learning and voice recognition technologies. The \nproposed system supports both text and voice-based grievance \nhandling operations. The proposed system uses AI-based \npreprocessing techniques and evaluates various supervised \nmachine learning models. The Support Vector Machine \nclassifier has the maximum accuracy of 92%, which is higher \nthan Naive Bayes and Logistic Regression. The proposed system \nindicates the efficiency of the proposed system in handling \ncustomer complaints in the real world. ', 'Rushali deshmukh', 'radeshmukh_comp@jspmrscoe.edu.in', 'Komal Katwe (JSPM Rajarshi Shahu College Of Engineering , Tathawade); Harshal Kale (JSPM Rajarshi Shahu College Of Engineering , Tathawade); Rushali deshmukh (JSPM Rajarshi Shahu College Of Engineering , Tathawade)*; prathamesh nagargoje (	JSPM Rajarshi Shahu College Of Engineering , Tathawade)', 'Katwe, Komal; Kale, Harshal; deshmukh, Rushali*; nagargoje, prathamesh', 'komalkatwe004@gmail.com; kharshal2102@gmail.com; radeshmukh_comp@jspmrscoe.edu.in*; prathameshnagargoje@gmail.com', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'CGMS (7).pdf (953,063 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'radeshmukh_comp@jspmrscoe.edu.in	Rushali	deshmukh	JSPM Rajarshi Shahu College Of Engineering , Tathawade', 'Paper Presentation'),
('167', 'rahulreddyh@gmail.com', '172', '6/13/2026 9:13:43 PM +00:00', '6/13/2026 9:13:43 PM +00:00', 'Knowledge Graph-Driven Self-Correction in LLM-Generated Clinical Laboratory Narratives', 'Large language models achieve strong precision-recall performance on clinical laboratory panel narrative generation, yet remain vulnerable to a class of errors that F1 cannot measure: confident LOINC binding errors, in which the model assigns a structurally valid but semantically wrong code and cannot self-correct when given only a verdict. We built a clinical knowledge graph from LOINC and reference range standards – derived entirely from clinical authority sources, not generated by a language model and used it as a non-generative critic in a two-stage validation pipeline. Stage 1 (rule-based Pydantic validators) correctly detects LOINC binding errors but achieves 0% correction after three retries because its feedback carries no clinical knowledge: it detects, it does not explain. Stage 2 (knowledge graph-grounded retrieval) detects the same errors, retrieves the clinical context, the wrong code’s definition, reference range, and the correct code for the intended analyte, and feeds a structured explanation to the LLM. Correction rate on all detected binding errors: 100%, achieved on the first attempt in both affected panels across 200 synthetic FHIR patient panels. The KG validator correctly handled 342 clinical synonyms without false positives, with similarity scores that cleanly separate synonyms (0.49 to 0.59) from true binding errors (0.23 to 0.29). Epistemic independence, building the validator from authoritative clinical sources rather than LLM-constructed graphs, is the property that makes explanation-driven self-correction possible.', 'Rahul Reddy Hanumanthgari', 'rahulreddyh@gmail.com', 'Rahul Reddy Hanumanthgari (Independent)*', 'Hanumanthgari, Rahul Reddy*', 'rahulreddyh@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Health Equity, Access, and Social Justice', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Knowledge Graph-Driven Self-Correction in LLM-Generated Clinical Laboratory Narratives.pdf (616,704 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Rahul Reddy Hanumanthgari\n\nrahulreddyh@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation'),
('168', 'dasparishma5@gmail.com', '173', '6/25/2026 5:06:47 AM +00:00', '6/25/2026 5:13:13 AM +00:00', 'Exploring psychosocial lived experiences of women with schizophrenia (WWS) and their families: An interpretative phenomenological study', 'Exploring psychosocial lived experiences of women with schizophrenia (WWS) and their families: An interpretative phenomenological study\n PhD Scholar: Ms Parishma Das, dasparishma5@gmail.com, 9101511891\nGuide: Dr Amaresha C, Associate Professor of DPSW, amarmsw@gmail.com, 9449603952\nDepartment of Psychiatric Social Work, Lokopriya Gopinath Bordoloi Regional Institute of Mental Health, Tezpur, Assam.\nBackground: Schizophrenia is a severe mental illness. The prevalence of schizophrenia in India is estimated to be 0.3% as per the Global Burden of Diseases (GBD). Irrespective of gender, it affects several domains of life. There is a lack of literature on exploring the lived experiences of women diagnosed with schizophrenia. Therefore, this study explored the psychosocial lived experiences of WWS and their families.\nObjectives: To understand the issues and needs experienced by WWS and their families through a phenomenological lens.\nMethodology/Design: An interpretative phenomenological (IPA) study was conducted among women diagnosed with schizophrenia, their spouses, and adolescent children seeking treatment from LGBRIMH, Tezpur, Assam. Participants were recruited using purposive sampling. In-depth semi-structured interviews were conducted separately among 5 family units, including 5 WWS, 5 spouses, and 5 adolescent children, to explore their lived experiences. Interviews were audio-recorded, transcribed, translated, and analyzed using IPA. Ethical approval was obtained from the Institutional Ethics Committee, and informed consent/assents were obtained from participants. \nResult: The analysis revealed various interconnected themes across participants. WWS described mainly stigma, low productivity & confidence, social withdrawal, obesity, etc. Spouses described care burden, domestic role reversal, economic burden, shame, guilt, stigma, etc. Adolescent children described care burden, role reversal, disruption in education and personal development, fear and confusion, emotional containment, etc.\nConclusion: The families of WWS have some well-established and distinct needs. This study highlights that there is a need to develop psychosocial interventions to address those issues and needs. \nKeywords: Women, Schizophrenia, Families, Lived experiences, Phenomenology\n', 'Parishma Das', 'dasparishma5@gmail.com', 'Parishma Das (LGB Regional Institute of Mental Health, Tezpur, Assam)*', 'Das, Parishma*', 'dasparishma5@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Conference Abstract (Parishma Das).docx (14,868 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Dr Amaresha C, Associate Professor of DPSW, \nDepartment of Psychiatric Social Work, Lokopriya Gopinath Bordoloi Regional Institute of Mental Health, Tezpur, Assam. amarmsw@gmail.com, 9449603952\n', 'PhD Best Paper Award')
INSERT INTO `abstract_details` (`id`, `email`, `paper_id`, `created`, `last_modified`, `paper_title`, `abstract`, `primary_contact_author_name`, `primary_contact_author_email`, `authors`, `author_names`, `author_emails`, `track_name`, `primary_subject_area`, `secondary_subject_areas`, `conflicts`, `assigned`, `completed`, `percent_completed`, `bids`, `discussion`, `status`, `requested_for_author_feedback`, `author_feedback_submitted`, `requested_for_camera_ready`, `camera_ready_submitted`, `requested_for_presentation`, `files`, `number_of_files`, `supplementary_files`, `number_of_supplementary_files`, `reviewers`, `reviewer_emails`, `metareviewers`, `metareviewer_emails`, `seniormetareviewers`, `seniormetarevieweremails`, `q1_co_authors`, `q2_type`) VALUES
('169', 'sarbaniborkotoky95@gmail.com', '174', '6/26/2026 4:13:19 AM +00:00', '6/26/2026 4:19:42 AM +00:00', 'Understanding the  Psychosocial Nuances  and the Lived Experiences of Couples in Empty Nest  : A Qualitative study', 'Introduction-  ‘Empty-Nest’, is a significant life event for many couples when  their children leave the parental homes. It can lead to range of  emotional, psychological, and social changes in the couples which is   referred to as ‘Empty -Nest Syndrome’.Despite of recognition of these challenges, there is a notable gap in literature  that addresses the psychosocial issues in Indian Context . Hence the aim of the study is to explore the psychosocial needs and lived experiences  among the couples who are in Empty-Nest Syndrome (ENS).\n\nObjective-The objective of this paper is to understand the psychosocial needs and lived experiences  among the couples who are in Empty-Nest (EN).\n\nMethodology- To explore the needs of the couples , Interpretative Phenomenological research approach has been used.  10  couples have been selected through snowball sampling  for in-depth interview from the urban areas of Tezpur, Assam. They were interviewed together and  Interpretative Phenomenological Analysis  has been used for the analysis of the in-depth interviews. \n\nResult-  The results reveals several emerging themes regarding their needs and lived experiences. The themes mostly captured the transition of a child-specific home to more of self - reflection, loss of a busy life, constant worriness, crisis of care , acceptance of current reality among the couples. \n\nConclusion - Many couples  in India are highly vulnerable to this transitional phase because  of the cultivist culture of Indian Society . Hence it is pertinent in addressing ES among elderly couples and in exploring the psychosocial needs .\n\nKeywords: Empty Nest, Empty-Nest Syndrome, Couples , Psychosocial needs, Indian Context.\n', 'Sarbani Borkotoky', 'sarbaniborkotoky95@gmail.com', 'Sarbani Borkotoky (LGBRIMH)*', 'Borkotoky, Sarbani*', 'sarbaniborkotoky95@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Sarbani B abstract  kochi docx.docx (15,370 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Dr Indrajeet Banerjee, Assistant Professor,  Department of Psychiatric Social Work, LGB Regional Institute of Mental Health, Tezpur, Assam, India, 784001\nGmail- indraj2015@gmail.com   \n', 'PhD Best Paper Award'),
('170', 'shaniy11@gmail.com', '175', '6/29/2026 3:25:24 PM +00:00', '6/29/2026 3:25:24 PM +00:00', 'Beyond the Acute Response: Trauma-Informed Social Work Research in Sexual Assault Care', 'Beyond the Acute Response: Trauma-Informed Social Work Research in Sexual Assault Care\nSexual assault is a major public health concern with long-term psychological, social, and health consequences. Social workers play a central role in Sexual Assault Referral Centers (SARCs), hospital-based services providing emergency medical, forensic, and psychosocial care during the first week following assault. In Israeli SARCs, social workers serve as case managers, accompanying survivors from admission through discharge. \nThis panel presents findings from a multi-center study conducted by social workers practicing in Israeli SARCs, highlighting the contribution of practitioner-led research to evidence-based social work. \nThrough three complementary presentations, the panel addresses the ethical challenges of conducting research with survivors of sexual assault, recovery trajectories following trauma, and the relationship between survivors\' experiences of care and PTSD symptoms. The empirical presentations draw on findings from a multi-center study of women treated in Israeli SARCs, using standardized measures of post-traumatic stress and post-traumatic growth to examine survivors’ recovery following sexual assault. These findings are discussed through the lens of Conservation of Resources (COR) theory, which emphasizes the role of resource loss and gain following trauma Together, the presentations demonstrate how research can both emerge from and strengthen trauma-informed social work practice. \nPresentation 1\nConducting Trauma-Informed Research with Survivors of Sexual Assault: Ethical and Practical Lessons from Multi-Center Studies\nDr. Hadas Rosenne\nThis presentation discusses the ethical and operational challenges of conducting research with survivors of sexual assault in healthcare settings. Drawing on experience from multi-center studies, it will address ethics approval processes, participant protection, practical lessons, and the unique dilemmas faced by social workers balancing the dual roles of practitioner and researcher.\n\nPresentation 2\nDoes time heal? Time Since Assault, Prior Trauma, and Recovery Trajectories Following Sexual Assault\nProf. Miriam Schiff\nUsing data collected from 78 women treated in Israeli SARCs, this presentation focuses on survivors with a history of prior sexual victimization, one of the most clinically complex populations seeking care in these settings. Findings suggest that time may function differently for these survivors, with distinct trajectories of post-traumatic stress symptoms and post-traumatic growth compared to women without a history of prior sexual victimization.\n\nPresentation 3\nPerceptions of Care and PTSD Outcomes Following Sexual Assault: Implications for Trauma-Informed Social Work Practice\nShani Yaakobi-Zelnik\nDrawing on data from 117 women treated in four Israeli SARCs, this presentation examines the relationship between survivors’ perceptions of care in Israeli SARCs and subsequent PTSD symptoms using a multivariable model. Findings indicate that perceived gains in resources during care, particularly empowerment, support, and a sense of control, are associated with lower PTSD symptoms, whereas loss of control is associated with greater distress. Drawing on Conservation of Resources theory, the presentation will discuss how trauma-informed care may function as a source of resource restoration and support recovery following sexual assault.\n', 'Shani Yaakobi-Zelnik', 'shaniy11@gmail.com', 'Shani Yaakobi-Zelnik (Paul Baerwald School of Social Work and Social Welfare, The Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Israel)*; Miriam  Schiff (Paul Baerwald School of Social Work and Social Welfare, The Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Israel); Hadas  Rosenne (Hadassah Medical Center, Jerusalem, Israel)', 'Yaakobi-Zelnik, Shani*; Schiff, Miriam ; Rosenne, Hadas ', 'shaniy11@gmail.com*; Miriam.Schiff@mail.huji.ac.il; HadasR@hadassah.org.il', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '2', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Beyond the Acute Response.docx (16,737 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Prof. Miriam Schiff, Dean, Paul Baerwald School of Social Work and Social Welfare, The Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Israel\n\nDr. Hadas Rosenne, Social Worker, Hadassah Medical Center, Jerusalem, Israel.\n\n', 'Panel Presentation'),
('171', 'navtiwari156@gmail.com', '176', '6/29/2026 3:30:16 PM +00:00', '6/29/2026 3:30:16 PM +00:00', 'Student mental health risk prediction', 'Mental health has become one of the most significant concerns affecting students worldwide, with increasing cases of stress, anxiety, depression, and emotional exhaustion resulting from academic pressure, social expectations, financial challenges, and personal issues. Poor mental health not only impacts students\' academic performance but also affects their physical well-being, relationships, and overall quality of life. Unfortunately, many students do not seek professional help due to fear of stigma, lack of awareness, or limited access to counseling services, leading to delayed diagnosis and intervention. Therefore, developing an intelligent system capable of identifying students at risk of mental health problems at an early stage has become increasingly important for educational institutions.\n\nThis paper presents a Student Mental Health Risk Prediction System that leverages machine learning techniques to predict the likelihood of mental health issues based on multiple behavioral, academic, and lifestyle factors. The proposed system analyzes key attributes such as age, gender, academic workload, sleep duration, stress levels, attendance, social interaction, physical activity, financial concerns, family support, and previous mental health history. Before model training, the collected dataset undergoes data preprocessing, including handling missing values, encoding categorical variables, feature scaling, and feature selection to improve model performance and prediction accuracy.\n\nSeveral supervised machine learning algorithms, including Random Forest, Decision Tree, Support Vector Machine (SVM), Logistic Regression, and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), are implemented and compared to determine the most effective prediction model. Model performance is evaluated using widely accepted metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, confusion matrix, and Receiver Operating Characteristic–Area Under the Curve (ROC-AUC). Comparative analysis enables the identification of the algorithm that provides the best balance between predictive accuracy and generalization. The proposed framework also incorporates explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) techniques, enabling counselors and educational administrators to understand the key factors influencing predictions and thereby increasing transparency and trust in the system.\n\nThe developed system is intended to serve as an early warning and decision-support tool for educational institutions, counselors, healthcare professionals, and parents by identifying students who may require timely psychological support. Early prediction enables institutions to implement personalized interventions, counseling programs, and preventive mental health initiatives before symptoms become severe. Experimental evaluation demonstrates that machine learning-based predictive models can effectively identify students at risk with high reliability while reducing false predictions. The proposed approach contributes to the advancement of intelligent healthcare and educational analytics by providing a scalable, cost-effective, and data-driven solution for mental health monitoring. Ultimately, the system aims to foster healthier learning environments, improve student well-being and academic success, and support proactive mental healthcare through the responsible application of artificial intelligence.', 'Navika  Tiwari', 'navtiwari156@gmail.com', 'Navika  Tiwari (Alliance University)*', 'Tiwari, Navika *', 'navtiwari156@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Public Health, Mental Health, and Population Well-being', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'mental_health_risk_prediction.pdf (321,103 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'lalli.k\nprofessor\nAlliance University\nlalli.k@alliance.edu.in', 'Paper Presentation'),
('172', 'vaanitiwari11@gmail.com', '177', '6/29/2026 4:05:06 PM +00:00', '6/29/2026 4:34:47 PM +00:00', 'Negotiating Trauma-Informed Responses to Gender-Based Violence in Kashmir: Perspectives of Social Work Practitioners', 'Keywords: Gender-based violence, trauma-informed practice, social work, ecological model, Kashmir, domestic violence\nObjectives: Social work practitioners occupy a critical position between survivors of gender-based violence (GBV) and state support systems. In conflict-affected settings such as Kashmir, this role is shaped by complex social, institutional, and political dynamics. This paper examines the obstacles encountered by social work practitioners in implementing trauma-informed approaches to supporting survivors of GBV and explores how these challenges are situated within a broader ecosystem of response.\nDesign: The study employs a mixed method research design. Qualitative data were collected through in-depth interviews with five social work professionals engaged in domestic violence support in Kashmir. Quantitative data were obtained from organizational reports, practice experience, case records and institutional records from Tata Institute of Social Sciences Special Cell For Women (2021-2024). Data were analyzed using the ecological model to examine interactions across individual, community, institutional, and structural levels.\nModel: The ecological model is used as an analytical framework to understand how trauma-informed responses are shaped by relationships among survivors, communities, service providers, and state institutions.\nResults: Findings indicate significant variation in how practitioners understand the relationship between trauma, conflict, and GBV. Social norms and pressures to preserve family relationships frequently influence survivors\' help-seeking pathways. Practitioners reported limited trust from communities, where social work interventions are sometimes perceived as threatening established social structures. Institutional constraints, including pressure to prioritize reconciliation, the location of services within police stations, and inconsistent legal responses, further hinder trauma-informed practice. The region’s history of instability, collective violence and trauma emerge as critical contextual forces that shape every aspect of caregiving and care seeking; experiences that alter, fundamentally, individuals’ sense of safety, trust and connection to community and institutions.  \nConclusion: The findings suggest that effective trauma-informed responses require more than procedural compliance. Strengthening support for survivors necessitates greater institutional accountability, improved practitioner training, and response systems that are responsive to survivors\' lived realities and the complexities of conflict-affected contexts.\n', 'Vaani Tiwari', 'vaanitiwari11@gmail.com', 'Surraya Khan (Independent Researcher); Vaani Tiwari (ANANDI)*', 'Khan, Surraya; Tiwari, Vaani*', 'khansurraya97@gmail.com; vaanitiwari11@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'Negotiating Trauma-Informed Responses to Gender-Based Violence in Kashmir_ Perspectives of Social Work Practitioners.docx (139,321 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Surraya P. Khan                                                            \nIndependent Researcher                                                     \nkhansurraya97@gmail.com                                               \n9541695018                                                                        \nLiver, Pahalgam, Jammu and Kashmir-192401                 \n\nVaani Tiwari        \nProgramme Lead, ANANDI \nvaanitiwari11@gmail.com \n6394338181\nDevgadh Baria, Dahod, Gujarat 389380 \n\n', 'Paper Presentation'),
('173', 'tanishka.israni03@gmail.com', '178', '6/30/2026 6:44:24 AM +00:00', '6/30/2026 6:44:24 AM +00:00', 'From Compliance to Care: Embedding Trauma-Informed Mental Healthcare Systems in Higher Education', 'Objectives\nThe needs of the current-day student have traversed far beyond providing a comprehensive course curriculum. Educational institutes are increasingly being recognised as a site for mental health crises yet the care given to students remains fragmented, reactive and clinician-dependent. The objective of this paper is to present a model for incorporating trauma-informed care within a higher education setting in India. The paper aims to: (1) describe the development of a culturally informed mental health infrastructure that assimilates into the existing system; and (2) initiate the integration of principles of trauma-informed care into institutional policy\nStudy Design\nThis paper uses a practice innovation framework to describe the design and implementation of a tiered mental health model at a National Law University in India. The model was developed in response to identified gaps in an institution’s crisis response capacity. Key components include: the development of the university\'s first Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) for both general mental health concerns and mental health emergencies; delivery of Psychological First Aid training for teaching and non-teaching staff using the Look, Listen, Link framework by Red Cross; trauma-informed care training for nursing staff; and coordination of crisis response for high-risk cases such suicidal ideation and active psychosis. The model aligns with recently issued guidelines by the National Task Force (NTF). However, the vision behind creation of the model is to extend beyond state compliance. The model envisions cultivating a trauma-informed culture by psychoeducating multiple stakeholders. \n\nResult\nImplementation of the model is underway. However, several components of the framework yielded notable outcomes. Some significant outcomes involved increased help-seeking behavior among the student community on campus. Notably, the teaching and non-teaching staff sparking conversation around mental health and therapy bolstered student confidence in recognising their mental health needs. Streamlining crisis response protocol seemed to reduce feelings of helplessness and panic in times of a crisis.  Over 2,700 hours of individual psychotherapy were delivered, spanning undergraduate, postgraduate, and PhD students. Staff across teaching and non-teaching roles received training in trauma-informed first response, resulting in improved capacity to identify early warning signs and initiate timely referrals. Formalised SOPs enabled a structured, consistent institutional response to mental health emergencies reducing ambiguity for first responders in crisis. By clearly defining the escalation pathways for Red Flag cases, the coordination became more streamlined within the institutional network and external psychiatric referral network. In addition to on-campus support, integration of digital tools such as Cogni Art, journaling, and basic psychometric assessments helped in keeping the help-seeking narrative alive in informal conversation among students.\nConclusion\nThis paper argues that trauma-informed care cannot be delivered sustainably through individual therapy with the campus counselor alone. Real institutional change requires embedding trauma awareness into policy. The model demonstrates that a single practitioner, working within a policy mandate, can catalyse some change; however a systemic change can only be successful with everyone’s contribution at an institutional and state level. This model offers a transferable framework for universities, especially in the stigmatized landscape of Indian universities, seeking to move beyond checkbox compliance toward trauma-informed institutions. The distinction between compliance and care is what determines whether a student in crisis encounters a system built to receive them, or one merely built to report on them.\n', 'Tanishka Israni', 'tanishka.israni03@gmail.com', 'Tanishka Israni (India)*', 'Israni, Tanishka*', 'tanishka.israni03@gmail.com*', 'ICSWHMH2027', 'Trauma, Resilience, and Healing', '', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0', 'Disabled (0)', 'Awaiting Decision', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'No', 'ICSWHMH2027_Abstract_TanishkaIsrani.docx.pdf (106,025 bytes)', '1', '', '0', '', '', '', '', '', '', 'Tanishka Israni (Author), Counselling Psychologist at National Law Institute University, Bhopal\nEmail: tanishka.israni03@gmail.com', 'Paper Presentation')
